// Generated by the keyword plan build. Server-only (blog and tool pages). Do not edit by hand.
export const BLOG_BODIES = {"off-road-vs-street-legal-australia":{"hook":"In Australia, a pedal-assist e-bike with a 250W motor that meets the EPAC standard can be used on public roads. Electric dirt bikes and e-bikes above 250W are for off-road and private property use only.","intro":["In Australia, a pedal-assist e-bike that meets the 250W standard can be used on public roads and paths, subject to state and territory rules. Electric dirt bikes and e-bikes with more powerful motors are for off-road and private property use only.","That one line settles most buying decisions, but the details matter: what counts as pedal assist, who checks it, and what changes from state to state. This guide explains the difference in plain language and points you to the regulators who write the rules."],"sections":[{"h":"Road-legal e-bikes: the 250W pedal-assist rule","p":["The e-bikes allowed on public roads are treated as bicycles. In general they have a motor rated at no more than 250W continuous power, the motor assists only while you pedal, and the assistance cuts out once you reach about 25 km/h. Bikes built to the European EN 15194 standard (often called EPAC) are designed to meet these limits.","A small throttle is sometimes allowed for walk-assist at very low speed, but a bike whose throttle can drive the motor at higher speeds is treated differently. Check the rule in your state before assuming a throttle bike is road legal.","If you want a bike for commuting, start with the [commuter and urban e-bikes](/shop/commuter-and-urban-e-bikes/) or [folding e-bikes](/shop/folding-e-bikes/) categories, where pedal-assist models are the norm."]},{"h":"Electric dirt bikes and e-motos: off-road and private property only","p":["Electric dirt bikes, mini bikes and most e-motos have motors well above 250W and are not built to road standards. They are sold for off-road and private property use only. They are not road legal, and they cannot simply be registered for the road.","Torque E-Bikes lists these bikes with that wording on purpose, and we will not describe a high-power bike as street legal. If you are comparing [electric dirt bikes](/shop/electric-dirt-bikes/), plan where you will ride them first: a private property, a licensed track or an approved off-road area."]},{"h":"State and territory differences","p":["Road rules are set by each state and territory, so details such as helmet rules, where you can ride on footpaths and how fast a power-assisted bike may be assisted can differ. Always check the current rule with the regulator for the state where you will ride."],"ul":["New South Wales: Transport for NSW (transport.nsw.gov.au)","Victoria: VicRoads (vicroads.vic.gov.au)","Queensland: Department of Transport and Main Roads (tmr.qld.gov.au)","Western Australia: Department of Transport (transport.wa.gov.au)","South Australia: Department for Infrastructure and Transport (dit.sa.gov.au)","Tasmania, the ACT and the Northern Territory: the state or territory road authority"]},{"h":"Age, licence and helmet requirements","p":["On public roads an e-bike that meets the pedal-assist rules is treated like a bicycle, and riders must wear an approved helmet. For dirt bikes used on private land, the land owner and your state may set their own rules on age, supervision and licensing. Do not rely on a general guide for numbers that vary by state: read the regulator page and the land owner’s conditions.","For riding gear that suits each type of riding, see our guide to [helmets and protective gear for e-bikes](/blog/helmets-and-protective-gear-for-e-bikes/)."]},{"h":"What the rules mean for conversion kits","p":["A conversion kit can turn an ordinary bicycle into an e-bike. A pedal-assist kit that stays within the 250W limits can be a road-legal e-bike, but a higher-powered kit makes the bike an off-road machine. Fitting a powerful kit to a bicycle does not make it a registered motorcycle.","Our [conversion kit guide](/blog/e-bike-conversion-kits-explained/) and [conversion kit finder](/tools/conversion-kit-finder/) explain how to match a kit to where you plan to ride."]},{"h":"A short history of why the rules look the way they do","p":["Australia’s e-bike rules grew out of an older idea: a power-assisted bicycle is still a bicycle if the motor only helps you pedal and the help is modest. Early rules set a low motor limit, and later changes aligned Australia with the European standard for electrically power assisted cycles, which is why you see the 250W and 25 km/h figures so often. The aim was to let a new kind of bike share paths and roads with ordinary bikes without needing registration or a licence, while keeping fast, powerful machines in the motor vehicle system.","That history explains a common source of confusion. Rules about “e-bikes” are really rules about a category of bicycle with a set of limits, not about anything that has a battery and two wheels. As soon as a bike goes outside those limits, a different set of rules applies, and those rules were never written with off-road electric motorcycles in mind. That gap is why electric dirt bikes are treated as off-road equipment rather than road vehicles."]},{"h":"How a police officer or inspector would assess your bike","p":["If you are stopped, the questions are practical. Does the bike have pedals that work? Does the motor assist only while pedalling, up to the cut-off speed? Is there a plate or label stating the motor power and the standard the bike meets? Is there a throttle, and does it work above walking pace? Are you wearing a helmet and using lights if it is dark?","It helps to keep the specification sheet or compliance statement, the receipt and any photos of the motor plate where you can find them. If you have modified the bike, keep records of what you changed. Officers are not mechanics, but a clear paper trail and a bike that matches its description make the conversation short."]},{"h":"Choosing a bike when you want to stay on the right side of the rules","ul":["Ask the seller to confirm in writing that the bike meets the pedal-assist limits for road use in your state.","Check the motor plate, the display and the manual for the rated power and the assist cut-off speed.","Be cautious about bikes sold with a “de-restriction” mode, an app that removes limits or a speed key.","Prefer bikes from sellers who list a clear off-road warning on powerful models.","Keep the original specification: if you later change the motor, controller or battery, the bike may no longer meet the definition."],"p":["If you are shopping for a road-legal bike, start in the [commuter and urban e-bikes](/shop/commuter-and-urban-e-bikes/) and [folding e-bikes](/shop/folding-e-bikes/) categories. If you want an off-road machine, go to [electric dirt bikes](/shop/electric-dirt-bikes/) and plan where you will ride before you buy."]},{"h":"Off-road riding in practice: venues, permission and etiquette","p":["Off-road does not mean anywhere you like. Public land, parks, beaches and bushland are mostly closed to motorised bikes, and some also restrict e-bikes. The places you can ride legally are private property with the owner’s permission, licensed or club motocross tracks and venues that accept electric bikes. Always check the venue’s rules on age, supervision, protective gear and noise.","Good etiquette protects access for everyone. Stay on marked tracks, keep to agreed hours, avoid riding near people and animals and take your rubbish home. Electric bikes are quieter, which makes them easier on neighbours, but a quiet bike can surprise other trail users, so slow down and announce yourself when you approach."]},{"h":"What happens when the rules change","p":["E-bike regulation is moving. Governments are reviewing power limits, throttle rules, battery safety standards and how to treat higher-powered bikes, and some states have published or consulted on changes. Because of this, a guide like this one is a snapshot. Check the regulator’s current page when you buy, and again if you move states, modify the bike or hear about a rule change in the news.","Torque E-Bikes will keep describing high-powered bikes as off-road and private property equipment, and will not describe them as street legal. If a rule changes in a way that affects a product we list, we will update the product page and this guide."]},{"h":"Examples: would this bike be allowed on the road?","ul":["**A 250W pedal-assist commuter bike with assistance that stops at 25 km/h:** this fits the pedal-assist definition and is treated as a bicycle, subject to state rules.","**A fat-tyre bike with a 750W motor and a thumb throttle:** the motor power is above the limit and the throttle can drive the bike without pedalling, so it is not a road-legal power-assisted bicycle.","**An electric dirt bike with no pedals:** this is an off-road machine. It is not road legal and cannot be registered for the road.","**A bicycle converted with a 1,000W kit:** the conversion takes it outside the limits, so it is an off-road machine.","**A folding e-bike with a 250W motor, pedal assist and a walk-assist button:** walk-assist at very low speed is generally accepted, but check your state’s wording."],"p":["These examples are general. Always check the bike’s rated power, the assist cut-off speed and any throttle behaviour against your state regulator’s current page."]},{"h":"Helmets, lights and other rules that apply to every rider","p":["Even on a road-legal e-bike, the ordinary bicycle rules still apply. In every state a rider must wear an approved bicycle helmet, give way as required, obey signals and signs and ride with lights and reflectors at night. Riding while using a handheld phone, riding under the influence of alcohol and riding on a footpath where it is not allowed can bring fines. Some states set different rules for children, for shared paths and for how fast you may ride on a footpath, so check your state’s page.","Because an e-bike is heavier and faster than it feels, courtesy matters. Slow down near pedestrians, announce yourself with a bell, keep left or right as your state requires and leave space when passing. These habits keep you safe and keep e-bikes welcome on shared paths."]},{"h":"If you are unsure: a checklist to bring to the regulator","ul":["The bike’s make, model and year.","The motor’s rated continuous power and the maker’s compliance statement.","Whether the bike has a throttle and how it behaves.","The speed at which assistance stops.","Any modifications you have made.","Where you plan to ride."],"p":["Contact the regulator by phone or email, and keep a copy of their reply. If a seller says a bike is road legal but you cannot find supporting information, treat the claim with caution until you have confirmed it."]},{"h":"Where to find official information","p":["Official information is published by each state and territory road authority, and by national bodies that set vehicle standards. Search the regulator’s website for pages on power-assisted bicycles, e-bikes and personal mobility. Look for the date the page was last updated, and prefer official pages over news articles or social media posts, which can be out of date or wrong."]},{"h":"Key takeaways","ul":["A pedal-assist e-bike within the power and speed limits is treated as a bicycle.","Electric dirt bikes and powerful e-bikes are for off-road and private property use only.","Rules are set by each state and territory, so check your regulator.","Keep your bike’s specification and receipt, and do not assume a seller’s “street legal” label is enough.","Wear a helmet, use lights at night and ride considerately wherever you are."]},{"h":"Common misunderstandings about e-bike rules","p":["A few myths cause most of the confusion. The first is that any bike with pedals is a bicycle. A bike with pedals but a powerful throttle motor is still treated by its power and how it is driven, not by whether it has pedals. The second is that a “street legal” label from a seller settles the question. The label does not decide it: the rules do, and they are set by your state or territory.","The third is that a conversion kit is exempt. Fitting a kit changes the bike, and if the result exceeds the limits for a power-assisted bicycle, the bike is no longer one. If you are unsure, ask the regulator for your state before you ride on a public road."]},{"h":"Riding on bike paths, footpaths and trails","p":["Where a road-legal e-bike may ride on paths and footpaths varies by state and by council, and shared paths often have their own speed limits and signs. Many national parks, mountain bike parks and trail networks also set their own rules for e-bikes, and some restrict them to certain trails.","Before riding somewhere new, check with the land manager. For off-road bikes, riding on public land is usually not permitted at all, so plan to ride on private property or at a venue that allows electric bikes."]},{"h":"Insurance, liability and landowners","p":["Insurance cover for e-bikes and off-road bikes differs between insurers. Some home and contents policies cover e-bikes, others exclude motorised vehicles, and compulsory third-party insurance does not apply to bikes that are not registered for the road. Ask your insurer in writing what is covered, particularly for a high-powered bike or a conversion.","If you ride on someone else’s land, get their permission and understand their conditions. A landowner may require supervision, protective gear or a signed waiver, and you remain responsible for your own safety and for other people on the property."]},{"h":"How to check a rule yourself","ul":["Search the regulator’s website for your state with the words “power-assisted bicycle” or “e-bike”.","Check the date on the page, because rules change.","Look for the motor power limit, the speed at which assistance cuts out and any throttle conditions.","If the page does not answer your question, phone or email the regulator and keep a copy of the reply."],"p":["This guide is general information, not legal advice. Torque E-Bikes does not set the rules, and the regulator for your state has the final say."]},{"h":"Power, speed and what “pedal assist” really means","p":["The headline number in most discussions is 250W, but the rule is about more than watts. A road-legal power-assisted bicycle is one where the motor helps only while you pedal, the motor’s continuous rated power stays within the limit, and the assistance stops at a set speed (commonly 25 km/h). Peak power on a spec sheet can be higher than continuous power, so look for the continuous rating and any compliance statement from the maker.","A bike can have pedals and a motor and still fall outside the definition. If the motor can drive the bike without pedalling at speeds above walking pace, or if its rated power is above the limit, regulators treat it differently. That is why a fat-tyre bike with a 750W motor and a thumb throttle is sold as off-road or private-property equipment, however many pedals it has."]},{"h":"How regulators describe e-bikes and e-motos","p":["Each state road authority publishes its own wording, but the shape is the same. A power-assisted pedal cycle that meets the definition is a bicycle, so bicycle road rules apply. Anything more powerful is a motor vehicle, and a motor vehicle used on a public road needs to meet vehicle standards, be registered and be ridden by a licensed rider. Off-road electric motorbikes are generally not built to those standards, which is why they are limited to private property.","The practical rule is simple: if a seller cannot show you that a bike meets the pedal-assist definition for your state, do not assume it can be used on public roads. Ask for the compliance statement in writing, and check it against the regulator’s page."]},{"h":"Buying from overseas and “grey” imports","p":["Bikes ordered from overseas sellers are often built to the rules of another country. A bike sold as an “EPAC” in Europe is designed around the 250W and 25 km/h limits, but a bike from a market with higher limits may not comply in Australia even if it looks identical. Imported bikes can also arrive without the compliance information, warranty or parts support that you would expect locally.","Before you import or buy a parallel-import model, check the motor rating, the assist cut-off speed, whether there is a throttle and how it behaves, and who will honour the warranty. Australian Consumer Law guarantees apply to goods sold to Australian consumers, but chasing an overseas seller is harder than dealing with a local one."]},{"h":"Common scenarios","ul":["**Commuting on a 250W pedal-assist bike:** generally treated as a bicycle. Wear an approved helmet, use lights at night and follow the rules for shared paths in your state.","**Riding a 1,000W electric dirt bike to the shops:** not permitted. It is an off-road machine, and riding it on a public road risks fines and loss of licence.","**Letting a teenager ride a high-powered e-bike on private land:** the road rules do not apply on private land, but safety, supervision and the landowner’s conditions do. Read our [kids’ electric dirt bike guide](/blog/kids-electric-dirt-bikes/) before you decide.","**Fitting a bigger motor or removing a speed limiter:** it can change the legal status of the bike. Check with your state road authority before you modify a road-legal bike.","**Hiring or lending an e-bike:** the person riding is responsible for riding it lawfully, so check the bike meets the rules where they will ride."]},{"h":"Penalties, safety and sensible habits","p":["Penalties for riding an unregistered high-powered bike on a public road can include fines, demerit points and the bike being impounded, and insurance may not respond if you have a crash. Even where a bike is allowed on the road, e-bikes are heavier and faster than they feel, so ride predictably, give way on shared paths and keep your speed down around pedestrians.","A few habits make the biggest difference: wear a helmet every time, keep your lights charged, service your brakes before you need them, and keep the compliance information for your bike with your receipt in case anyone asks. If you are ever unsure about a rule, ask the regulator rather than relying on a forum post."]},{"h":"A state-by-state checking routine","p":["Because rules sit with each state and territory, the most reliable way to stay legal is a short routine you repeat when you buy a bike, move state or modify the bike. First, find the road authority’s page on power-assisted bicycles or e-bikes and note the date. Second, write down the motor power limit, the speed at which assistance must stop, any throttle conditions and the helmet and lighting rules. Third, compare that list with your bike’s specification and compliance statement. Fourth, keep a copy of the page and the specification with your purchase records.","If anything does not match, ask the seller to explain why, or ask the regulator by email. A written answer from the regulator is worth far more than a forum post when a police officer or insurer asks questions."]},{"h":"Hire bikes, workplaces and shared schemes","p":["Hire and share schemes carry their own conditions, such as age limits, speed caps and restricted areas. If you ride a hired e-bike you are still responsible for riding it lawfully. At work, check whether your employer allows e-bikes inside buildings or in bike storage, because lithium battery safety rules are becoming common, and some buildings restrict charging."]},{"h":"Teenagers, young riders and supervision","p":["Age rules for e-bikes and for off-road bikes differ, and they depend on where the bike is ridden. On a public road, a young rider on a road-legal e-bike follows bicycle rules and helmet requirements. On private land, there may be no legal age limit for an off-road bike, but you carry the responsibility. Choose a bike sized for the child, supervise every ride, insist on full protective gear and set clear boundaries. Our [kids’ electric dirt bike guide](/blog/kids-electric-dirt-bikes/) and [electric balance bike guide](/blog/electric-balance-bikes/) cover this in more detail."]},{"h":"A three-question buying checklist","ul":["Where will I ride? Roads and bike paths point to a 250W pedal-assist e-bike. Private land and trails point to an off-road bike.","How much power do I need? More power is not better if it puts the bike outside the rules you need to follow.","Who is the rider? Age, height and experience matter for dirt bikes, so check the maker’s guidance and read our [kids’ electric dirt bike guide](/blog/kids-electric-dirt-bikes/) for young riders."]},{"h":"Quick recap","p":["In this guide we covered road-legal e-bikes: the 250W pedal-assist rule; electric dirt bikes and e-motos: off-road and private property only; state and territory differences; age, licence and helmet requirements; what the rules mean for conversion kits; and a short history of why the rules look the way they do. Come back to the section you need when you are deciding, and use the related categories and guides linked above to compare real products in Australian dollars. If you would like help applying any of this to your own bike, your budget and where you ride, message the team on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 or use the contact page, and we will reply with clear, honest advice."]}],"faq":[{"q":"Are e-bikes legal in Australia?","a":"Pedal-assist e-bikes that meet the 250W EPAC standard can be used on public roads and paths, subject to state rules. Electric dirt bikes and higher-powered e-bikes are for off-road and private property use only. Always check your state or territory road authority before you ride."},{"q":"Do I need a licence or registration for an e-bike?","a":"A compliant 250W pedal-assist e-bike is generally treated like a bicycle, but rules differ by state, so confirm with your road authority. Electric dirt bikes are not registrable for road use in Australia and are limited to private property and off-road riding."},{"q":"Does Torque E-Bikes sell road-legal e-bikes?","a":"Yes. The commuter, folding and cargo categories include pedal-assist e-bikes. Each product page states its motor power, and anything above 250W is marked off-road only. Message the team on WhatsApp if you are unsure which model suits where you ride."},{"q":"What is the difference between a road-legal e-bike and an electric dirt bike?","a":"A road-legal e-bike adds a limited 250W motor to a pedalled bicycle. An electric dirt bike is a motorbike-style machine with far more power and no pedal-assist limit, so it is restricted to private land and off-road use. They suit different riders and different places."},{"q":"Where does Torque E-Bikes list e-bikes that are road legal and those that are off-road only?","a":"Torque E-Bikes lists e-bikes that are road legal and those that are off-road only in the commuter & urban e-bikes category, with the specification and the price in Australian dollars on each product page. The team can confirm fitment, stock or freight on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 before you order."}],"keywords":{"primary":"electric bikes and the law","supporting":["e bike legislation","australian states are proposing stricter e-bike laws and age limits","legal e bikes","ebike rules","are surrons legal in australia"]},"words":3214},"best-electric-bikes-in-australia":{"hook":"The best electric bike depends on where you ride. Commuter e-bikes suit daily trips, e-mountain bikes suit trails, folding e-bikes suit small spaces and cargo e-bikes suit loads.","intro":["The best electric bike in Australia is the one that fits how and where you ride. A bike that is perfect for a flat daily commute is wrong for steep trails, and a trail bike is wasted on a short ride to the station.","Instead of a single ranking, this guide shows how to narrow the choice in five steps, then points you to the right category so you can compare real models side by side in Australian dollars."],"sections":[{"h":"Start with the type of riding","p":["Match the bike to the journey. Commuting and errands suit pedal-assist city bikes with mudguards, lights and a rack. Trail riding suits an e-mountain bike. Sand, gravel and rough tracks suit a fat-tyre bike. Small flats and mixed transport suit a folding bike, and loads or children suit a cargo bike."],"ul":["City and commuting: [commuter and urban e-bikes](/shop/commuter-and-urban-e-bikes/)","Trails and hills: [electric mountain bikes](/shop/electric-mountain-bikes/)","Sand, gravel and rough tracks: [fat-tyre and scrambler e-bikes](/shop/fat-tyre-and-scrambler-e-bikes/)","Small spaces and mixed transport: [folding e-bikes](/shop/folding-e-bikes/)","Loads and family: [cargo bikes and e-trikes](/shop/cargo-bikes-and-e-trikes/)"]},{"h":"Check the motor and the rules","p":["In Australia a pedal-assist e-bike with a 250W motor can use public roads, subject to state rules. Bikes with more powerful motors are for off-road and private property use only. Read the product page, and see our guide to [e-bike laws in Australia](/blog/off-road-vs-street-legal-australia/) if you are unsure."]},{"h":"Battery size and real-world range","p":["Battery capacity is usually quoted in watt-hours (Wh). A larger battery gives more range but adds weight and cost. The range in a brochure is a best case: hills, headwinds, heavy riders, cold weather and high assist levels all shorten it.","Choose a battery with some spare capacity for your longest regular trip. Our [e-bike battery guide](/blog/electric-moto-maintenance-lithium-safety/) explains how to look after it so it lasts."]},{"h":"Brakes, frame and components","p":["E-bikes are heavier and faster than normal bikes, so brakes matter. Look for hydraulic disc brakes on hilly or fast bikes, and check that the frame size suits your height. Reliable components and spare parts matter more than a long feature list."]},{"h":"How the main e-bike types compare on cost, comfort and use","p":["Different types of e-bike trade price, comfort, speed and practicality in different ways. Commuter and city bikes are usually the most affordable per useful feature, and they come ready for daily use. Folding bikes cost more per feature but solve storage problems. Cargo bikes and trikes cost more again, because they are built stronger and carry loads. E-mountain bikes sit at the top of the range for components and suspension. Fat-tyre bikes fall in between, with a focus on rough and soft surfaces.","Rather than ask which is best overall, ask which type does the job you actually have. A rider who spends 90 per cent of their time on sealed roads gets little from a full-suspension e-MTB, and a rider on rough trails gets little from a slim-tyred commuter."]},{"h":"What “good” looks like at each price level","ul":["**Entry level:** a simple hub-motor bike with a cadence sensor, mechanical or basic hydraulic brakes and a modest battery. Fine for short, flat trips.","**Mid-range:** a better motor, a larger battery, hydraulic brakes, a good frame, mudguards and lights, and a longer warranty.","**Premium:** a torque-sensing mid-drive motor, integrated battery, high-quality suspension and brakes, and strong dealer support."],"p":["Look for the features that matter to your riding at your price. A mid-range bike with the right fit and parts often beats a premium bike that is the wrong shape for your use."]},{"h":"Testing a bike before you buy","p":["If you can test ride, ride the bike on a route like your own. Try the assist levels, use the brakes hard once, ride up a hill and over a bump and notice how the bike feels at walking pace and at speed. Check how easy it is to remove the battery, fold the bike if it folds and lift it if you will need to carry it. Pay attention to noise, because a motor that whines or rattles can be irritating on every ride.","If you buy online and cannot test ride, ask for a size chart, a video or photos of the bike next to a person, and check the return policy. Read owner reviews for the specific model, not only the brand."]},{"h":"Real-world range, charging and cost to run","p":["A typical e-bike costs very little to run. Charging a few hundred watt-hours costs a small amount of electricity, so the cost per kilometre is a fraction of a car’s. The bigger costs are wear items and depreciation. Plan for tyres, chains, brake pads and the occasional service, and set aside money for a replacement battery in the longer term.","Range depends on assist level, rider weight, terrain, wind and temperature. If you want to ride further, use lower assist on flat ground, keep tyres properly inflated, change gear early on climbs and avoid riding with the battery very low in cold weather."]},{"h":"Buying for specific people and situations","ul":["**For an older rider:** a step-through frame, a comfortable saddle, reliable brakes and a motor that starts gently.","**For a teenager:** a bike that fits now, with good brakes, lights and a helmet, and clear rules about where to ride.","**For a rider with a bad back or knees:** an upright position, a suspension seat post and a torque-sensing motor.","**For shift workers:** bright lights, reflective gear and a battery with margin for night rides.","**For a family:** a longtail cargo bike with child seats, strong brakes and a stand."]},{"h":"What to do before you spend your budget","p":["Decide the type, set a budget range, shortlist three bikes and check each against our buying questions: motor, battery, brakes, warranty, support and rules. Read the product page carefully, ask questions if anything is missing and check the delivery details for a lithium battery. When you are ready, browse by category: [commuter and urban](/shop/commuter-and-urban-e-bikes/), [mountain](/shop/electric-mountain-bikes/), [folding](/shop/folding-e-bikes/), [cargo and trikes](/shop/cargo-bikes-and-e-trikes/) and [fat-tyre](/shop/fat-tyre-and-scrambler-e-bikes/). For lower-priced options see [budget e-bikes](/electric-bikes/budget/)."]},{"h":"A closer look at commuter e-bikes","p":["Commuter e-bikes are designed around the daily routine: a comfortable upright position, tyres that roll well and resist punctures, mudguards, a rear rack and often built-in lights. The motor is usually a hub unit or a modest mid-drive, with a battery sized for a typical working week of short trips. They are the e-bike most people should consider first, because they do the most common jobs well.","When comparing commuters, look past the motor. Check the brakes, because a good hydraulic disc brake gives confidence in wet traffic. Check the gearing, because a bike with a wide range copes with hills better. Check the weight, because you will lift the bike onto racks or up steps. And check the battery location and lock, because a battery you can remove is easier to charge and harder to steal.","A step-through frame is not just for people with limited mobility. It makes mounting with a bag or a child seat easier, and it makes stopping at lights less awkward. Many riders choose a step-through because they like the convenience."]},{"h":"A closer look at electric mountain bikes","p":["E-mountain bikes are the most specialised type, and the most varied. At one end are hardtail bikes with front suspension, which are simple, light and affordable. At the other end are full-suspension bikes with long travel, strong brakes and powerful motors. The right one depends on your trails: smooth fire trails need little suspension, while rocky, steep singletrack rewards more.","The motor and battery are the heart of an e-MTB. Mid-drive motors are the norm, because they put weight low and central and let you use the gears to climb. Torque sensors give smooth, predictable help. Look at battery capacity, how easy it is to remove and whether you can fit a second battery for long rides. Check the warranty too, because motors and batteries are costly to replace.","Also check where you can ride. Some trail networks and parks restrict e-bikes, so find out before you buy. Pedal-assist e-bikes that meet the pedal-assist limits are treated as bicycles for road rules, but land managers can set their own conditions."]},{"h":"A closer look at folding and compact e-bikes","p":["Folding e-bikes shrink to fit under a desk, in a boot or on a train. The compromises are smaller wheels, which feel twitchy on rough roads, and often a heavier frame because the fold mechanism and battery add weight. For many riders the trade is worth it: a bike you can bring inside is a bike that does not get stolen, and a bike you can take on the train extends your range.","Test the fold. A mechanism that takes a minute and feels secure will be used. One that is fiddly will not. Check the folded size against your space, the weight against what you can lift and the battery against what you need for the ride."]},{"h":"A closer look at cargo bikes, trikes and fat-tyre bikes","p":["Cargo bikes and trikes trade speed and agility for carrying capacity and stability. A longtail handles like a long bike, and a front-loader puts the load where you can see it. Trikes stay upright when you stop, which suits riders who prefer not to balance or who carry awkward loads. All of them benefit from strong brakes, a good stand and a battery sized for the extra weight.","Fat-tyre bikes suit sand, gravel, snow and relaxed riding on rough ground. The wide tyres add comfort and grip. They also add weight and rolling resistance, so choose a battery with enough capacity, and expect range to drop on hard surfaces. If you want one bike for beach paths, fire trails and the occasional trip to the shops, a fat-tyre bike can be a good compromise."]},{"h":"Questions the best e-bike should be able to answer","ul":["Who built the motor and the battery, and can I buy replacements?","What is the battery’s capacity in watt-hours, and what real range do owners report?","Which brake brand and size are fitted?","What is the frame material and the rider weight limit?","What are the warranty terms and who services the bike near me?","Does the bike meet the pedal-assist definition for road use in my state?"],"p":["If the seller cannot answer these, treat that as information. The best bike for you comes with clear answers and someone who will stand behind it."]},{"h":"Common ways to save money without buying a poor bike","ul":["Choose a bike with common parts and proven components rather than a long feature list.","Skip features you will not use, such as a full-suspension frame if you ride on roads.","Buy a good lock and lights before upgrading the saddle or grips.","Check whether last year’s model is available at a lower price.","Compare the cost of delivery, because heavy bikes with batteries can cost more to ship."]},{"h":"Questions to ask yourself before you buy","p":["How far will I ride most days, and what is the longest trip I expect? Where will I ride, and are there hills, sand or rough tracks? Where will I keep and charge the bike? What do I need to carry? What rules apply where I live? What is my total budget, including a helmet, lock and lights? Write down your answers, because they turn a long list of bikes into a short one.","If you answer these questions and still cannot decide between two bikes, message the team with your answers and the two models. We will tell you which we think suits you better and why, and if neither is right we will say so."]},{"h":"After you buy: the first month","p":["In the first month, ride in a variety of conditions, note how much battery you use, check bolts and brakes after the first few rides and book a service if anything feels wrong. Learn your assist levels and gears, and set the saddle and bars to suit you. A bike that fits and is set up well is more enjoyable and safer, and it keeps you riding."]},{"h":"Reading reviews sensibly","p":["Reviews are useful, but they vary in quality. Look for reviews from owners who have used the bike for months, not days. Pay attention to comments about service, battery life, brake performance and parts availability, because these show up with time. Be cautious of reviews that read like advertisements, and remember that a single review reflects one rider’s situation."]},{"h":"Key takeaways","ul":["The best e-bike is the one that fits your riding, your body and your budget.","Commuter bikes suit most riders, and specialist bikes suit specific jobs.","Compare bikes in the same type and price band.","Check battery size in watt-hours, brakes, warranty and support.","Make sure the bike meets the rules for where you will ride."]},{"h":"A last word on choosing well","p":["The best e-bike is the one you will ride often. Choose a type that suits your life, a bike that fits and a seller who will help you if something goes wrong. Check the rules for where you will ride, and add a helmet, lights and a lock to your budget. When you are ready, browse the [electric bikes](/electric-bikes/) page or message the team with your questions."]},{"h":"Questions to ask before you buy any e-bike","ul":["What is the motor’s rated power, and does the bike meet the pedal-assist limits if I want to ride it on roads?","How big is the battery, and what real-world range do riders report for my weight and terrain?","Who services the bike, and are spare parts such as batteries, chargers and brake pads easy to get?","What does the warranty cover, for how long, and who handles a claim?","How heavy is it, and can I lift it, store it and carry it if I need to?"]},{"h":"Price bands and what to expect","p":["E-bike prices cover a wide range, and a higher price usually buys a better motor and battery, better brakes, a stronger frame and a longer warranty. Lower-priced bikes can be good value for short, flat trips, but check the brakes, battery and warranty with extra care.","Rather than compare across types, compare bikes of the same type in the same price band. A commuter e-bike and an e-mountain bike at the same price will have very different strengths. The [budget e-bikes](/electric-bikes/budget/) page lists the lowest-priced models, and each category page shows its price range in Australian dollars."]},{"h":"Weight, storage and charging at home","p":["E-bikes are far heavier than normal bikes. If you live in an apartment, need to carry the bike upstairs or put it in a car, weight and a removable battery matter. Plan where the bike will live and where you will charge it, because a battery should be charged in a ventilated spot away from anything flammable.","If space is tight, a folding bike may suit better: see the [folding e-bike guide](/blog/folding-electric-bikes/)."]},{"h":"Test ride and fit","p":["A bike that is the wrong size will never feel right. Check the maker’s size chart, and if you can, sit on the bike or compare it with one you already ride. If you are buying online, ask the seller about returns and fitment advice before ordering, and message the team if you want a second opinion on size."]},{"h":"Commuter e-bikes: the everyday choice","p":["If most of your riding is to work, the shops or the station, a commuter e-bike gives the best return. Look for a comfortable upright position, mudguards, a rear rack, built-in lights and tyres that handle wet roads. A step-through frame makes starting and stopping easier, and a removable battery makes charging at work or in an apartment simple.","Commuters should put reliability ahead of headline features. Check that brake pads, tyres and chains are standard sizes you can buy locally, and that the seller can supply spare parts. See the [commuter e-bike guide](/blog/commuter-e-bikes/) for a checklist, and browse [commuter and urban e-bikes](/shop/commuter-and-urban-e-bikes/)."]},{"h":"Electric mountain bikes: trails and hills","p":["An e-MTB suits riders who want to climb more and ride further on trails. Suspension, strong brakes and a durable frame matter more than a long feature list, and a mid-drive motor with a torque sensor usually feels the most natural on technical climbs. Check where you plan to ride, because some trail networks restrict e-bikes.","Our [e-MTB buying guide](/blog/electric-mountain-bike-buying-guide/) covers hardtail versus full suspension, motors and battery size, and you can compare models in the [electric mountain bikes](/shop/electric-mountain-bikes/) category."]},{"h":"Folding, cargo and fat-tyre bikes: special jobs","ul":["**Folding e-bikes** suit small flats and mixed transport. Check folded size and weight: [folding e-bike guide](/blog/folding-electric-bikes/).","**Cargo e-bikes and trikes** carry shopping, tools or children. Check load ratings and brakes: [cargo guide](/blog/electric-cargo-bikes/) and [trike guide](/blog/electric-trikes-for-adults/).","**Fat-tyre e-bikes** handle sand, gravel and rough tracks. They are heavier and can be less efficient on hard roads: [fat-tyre guide](/blog/fat-tyre-e-bikes/)."]},{"h":"Understanding battery size with a simple example","p":["Battery capacity in watt-hours is the voltage multiplied by the amp-hours. A 36V battery rated at 10Ah holds 360Wh, and a 48V battery at 14Ah holds 672Wh. Many riders see consumption somewhere in the region of 10 to 25 Wh per kilometre depending on assist level, hills, rider weight and wind, so a 500Wh battery might cover anywhere from about 20 to 50 km. Treat that as a rough guide, not a promise.","If your daily round trip is 30 km with hills, a 500Wh battery used at medium assist will be working hard, and a larger pack or a second battery gives you a safety margin. If your trip is 8 km on flat ground, a smaller battery is fine and saves weight and money."]},{"h":"Warranty, servicing and what “good value” means","p":["A cheaper bike that cannot be serviced is poor value. Ask who services the bike, how long parts take to arrive and what the warranty covers. Batteries and motors are the costly parts, so find out whether they are covered and for how long, and whether the seller stocks replacements. Australian Consumer Law guarantees apply on top of any warranty.","Good value usually means a bike that suits your riding, uses common wearable parts, has clear specifications and comes with someone who will answer your questions. If you want help comparing two bikes, message the team with your budget and typical trip."]},{"h":"Three example shopping lists","ul":["**The first-time commuter:** a step-through commuter e-bike, mid-size battery, mudguards, rack, lights, a D-lock, an approved helmet and a repair kit.","**The weekend trail rider:** a hardtail e-MTB with hydraulic brakes, a larger battery, a trail helmet, gloves, a pump and spare tube, and a rack for the car.","**The family:** a longtail cargo e-bike with child seats, a strong stand, good lights, two locks and a rain cover."],"p":["Use these as a starting point and adjust for your budget and where you ride. A good bike with the right accessories will serve you better than an expensive bike you cannot use comfortably."]},{"h":"Mistakes first-time buyers make","ul":["Buying on price alone and ignoring brakes and warranty.","Choosing the wrong frame size.","Forgetting about storage, charging and weight.","Assuming any e-bike can be ridden on any road or trail.","Skipping a helmet, lights and a lock."]},{"h":"Set a budget and compare like for like","p":["Prices span a wide range depending on motor, battery and frame. If you are watching costs, the [budget electric bikes](/electric-bikes/budget/) page lists the lowest-priced models, and the [all e-bikes](/electric-bikes/) page lets you browse by type.","Compare two or three bikes of the same type, in the same price band, on motor, battery, brakes, weight and warranty. Ask the team on WhatsApp if you want help narrowing the list."]},{"h":"Quick recap","p":["In this guide we covered start with the type of riding; check the motor and the rules; battery size and real-world range; brakes, frame and components; how the main e-bike types compare on cost, comfort and use; and what “good” looks like at each price level. Come back to the section you need when you are deciding, and use the related categories and guides linked above to compare real products in Australian dollars. If you would like help applying any of this to your own bike, your budget and where you ride, message the team on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 or use the contact page, and we will reply with clear, honest advice."]}],"faq":[{"q":"What should I look for in an electric bike?","a":"Start with where you will ride, then check motor power, battery capacity, frame type and brakes. Compare the stated range with your usual trip length, and confirm whether the model is road legal or off-road only. Weight and warranty support also matter for daily use."},{"q":"How far can an electric bike go on one charge?","a":"Range depends on battery size, rider weight, terrain and assist level, so treat any claim as a guide. Check the manufacturer figure on the product page and allow a margin for hills and headwinds. A larger battery or lower assist setting stretches the distance."},{"q":"Which brands does Torque E-Bikes stock?","a":"The range covers more than 100 brands across e-bikes, electric dirt bikes, parts and gear. Browse the brand pages to see each maker’s current models and prices, or filter the shop by category if you already know the type of bike you want."},{"q":"Is it better to buy a cheap e-bike or a more expensive one?","a":"A lower price can suit short, flat trips, but check the battery, brakes and warranty before deciding. Higher-priced bikes usually add stronger components and longer range. Compare two or three models in the same category rather than judging by price alone."},{"q":"Where does Torque E-Bikes list the electric bikes this guide compares?","a":"Torque E-Bikes lists the electric bikes this guide compares in the commuter & urban e-bikes category, with the specification and the price in Australian dollars on each product page. The team can confirm fitment, stock or freight on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 before you order."}],"keywords":{"primary":"electric bikes guide","supporting":["electric bikes in guide","how to choose electric bikes in","electric bikes in explained","electric bikes in for beginners","electric bikes in tips"]},"words":3162},"e-bike-brands-in-australia":{"hook":"E-bike brands differ mainly by bike type, price band and parts quality. This guide groups the brands listed at Torque E-Bikes by what each one makes.","intro":["People searching for e-bike brands in Australia often start with household names such as Norco, Avanti, Orbea and Focus, then discover that many electric bikes are sold under smaller brands. A brand name tells you who stands behind the bike, but it does not tell you how good the bike is.","This guide explains what to compare between brands, how local support affects ownership, and where to see the brands Torque E-Bikes lists. We do not stock every brand mentioned here, and we only describe the brands we list."],"sections":[{"h":"Why a brand name is only part of the answer","p":["Established bicycle makers often add electric models to a range they already sell through bike shops. Newer e-bike brands may sell online and ship direct. Neither is automatically better. The questions that matter are the same: who sells it, who services it, and where do the parts come from?"]},{"h":"What to compare between brands","ul":["Motor and battery: who makes them, how common are they, and can you buy a replacement?","Warranty: how long, what it covers and who handles a claim in Australia.","Parts and servicing: are spare parts listed, and can a local bike shop work on the bike?","Frame and components: brakes, gears, tyres and suspension suited to how you ride.","Price: compare bikes of the same type and spec, not different categories."]},{"h":"Imported brands versus locally supported brands","p":["An imported brand is not worse by default, but local support changes the ownership experience. Check who handles warranty, how long parts take to arrive and whether the seller can answer technical questions. Australian Consumer Law guarantees apply whichever brand you buy."]},{"h":"Brands you can browse at Torque E-Bikes","p":["Torque E-Bikes lists electric dirt bikes, e-bikes, parts and gear from many brands. You can browse them all on the [brands page](/brands/), or open a brand to see its full range with prices in Australian dollars.","If you are comparing dirt bikes, start with the [Sur-Ron models guide](/blog/sur-ron-models-explained/) and the [Talaria guide](/blog/talaria-electric-dirt-bikes/). For fat-tyre bikes, see the [fat-tyre e-bike guide](/blog/fat-tyre-e-bikes/)."]},{"h":"How to research a brand properly","p":["Start with the brand’s website and look for clear specifications, contact details and a physical or registered business address. Search for reviews on independent sites, forums and social media, and look at how the brand responds to complaints. Check the warranty terms and the length of time the brand has been trading. A brand that is transparent and responsive is more likely to look after you.","Be wary of brands that appear and vanish, change names often or offer prices that seem too good to be true. Check whether you can reach someone by phone or message before you buy, and whether spare parts are available."]},{"h":"Brand types: bicycle makers, e-bike specialists and importers","ul":["**Bicycle makers** add e-bikes to established ranges, with dealer networks.","**E-bike specialists** focus on electric bikes and often innovate on design and battery integration.","**Importers and online brands** sell direct, often with wide ranges and competitive prices.","**Off-road specialists** make electric dirt bikes and e-motos for private land."],"p":["Each type has strengths and weaknesses. Match the type to your needs, and check the support you will receive."]},{"h":"Brands and the rules","p":["Whatever the brand, the rules depend on the bike’s specification and how it is used. A road-legal pedal-assist e-bike from any brand must meet the same limits, and a high-powered bike from any brand is off-road only. Read our [e-bike laws guide](/blog/off-road-vs-street-legal-australia/) before you buy."]},{"h":"Browsing brands at Torque E-Bikes","p":["The [brands page](/brands/) lists every brand we stock, with the number of products and a link to each brand’s range. Open a brand to compare models and prices. If you are unsure which brand suits you, message the team with your riding and budget."]},{"h":"Brand loyalty versus value","p":["Brand loyalty can be useful when it reflects good service, but it can also blind you to better options. Compare what each brand offers against your needs, and be prepared to choose a less famous brand if it gives better value and support. Read the warranty, check parts and ask existing owners. A good brand earns your trust with transparency, consistent quality and helpful service, not with advertising.","When you find a brand you like, check that the model you want is well supported, and that the seller is reliable. Keep your receipt and records, and keep in touch with the seller if you have questions."]},{"h":"A last word on brands","p":["Brands matter less than the bike, the support and the seller. Use brand names as a starting point, then check the details. Ask about parts, warranty and service, and choose the bike that fits your riding. If you want a brand we do not stock, we will say so, and help you find an alternative from our range."]},{"h":"A short checklist for judging any brand","ul":["How long has the brand been selling, and does it publish clear specifications?","Does it list warranty terms and a way to contact support?","Are spare parts, such as batteries and chargers, available separately?","Do independent owners describe the same strengths and problems over time?","Does the seller explain the rules for riding in Australia honestly?"]},{"h":"Why the same bike can appear under different names","p":["Many e-bikes share frames, motors and batteries from the same factories, then are sold under different brand names. That is not a problem in itself, but it means a brand name does not guarantee unique engineering. Compare the specification and the support, not the logo."]},{"h":"Where to start for each type of rider","ul":["Commuting: see the [commuter e-bike guide](/blog/commuter-e-bikes/) and the [commuter and urban e-bikes](/shop/commuter-and-urban-e-bikes/) category.","Trails: see the [e-MTB buying guide](/blog/electric-mountain-bike-buying-guide/).","Off-road bikes: see the [electric dirt bike guide](/blog/best-electric-dirt-bikes-in-australia/).","Sand and gravel: see the [fat-tyre guide](/blog/fat-tyre-e-bikes/)."]},{"h":"How to read a spec sheet without being fooled","p":["Spec sheets are written to sell, so read them with a few filters in mind. Motor power is often quoted as peak power rather than continuous power. Battery capacity should be given in watt-hours: if only volts or amp-hours are shown, multiply them to compare like with like. Range figures are almost always best case, and weights may exclude the battery.","Look for the details that tell you how the bike is built: brake brand and type, motor and sensor type, frame material, tyre width, rider weight limit and the warranty terms. A brand that is open about these is usually easier to deal with after the sale."]},{"h":"Service, warranty and what happens when something breaks","p":["Every brand has a good day. What separates brands is what happens on a bad one. Before you buy, find out whether you would send the bike to a local workshop or ship it, how long parts take, and whether the seller will help you diagnose a fault. Batteries and controllers are the parts most likely to need attention, so check how they are covered.","Australian Consumer Law gives you rights if a product is faulty, and those rights apply whichever brand you choose. In practice a seller that responds quickly and stocks spare parts is worth more than a famous logo."]},{"h":"Ten questions to ask before buying from any brand","ul":["Who sells and services the bike in Australia?","What is the warranty and who handles claims?","What are the motor and battery brands?","How big is the battery in watt-hours?","Are spare parts available separately?","What is the bike’s weight and rider limit?","Does the bike meet the pedal-assist definition for road use?","What is included in the box?","What do independent owners say after a year of use?","How is a lithium battery shipped to my address?"]},{"h":"Brands we do not stock","p":["Searches for Norco, Avanti, Orbea, Specialized, Focus and similar names are common, but Torque E-Bikes does not list those brands. If one of them is your first choice, we would rather you buy it from an authorised dealer than guess. If you want an alternative, tell us your budget and riding and we can suggest something from the range."]},{"h":"Quick recap","p":["In this guide we covered why a brand name is only part of the answer; what to compare between brands; imported brands versus locally supported brands; brands you can browse at Torque E-Bikes; how to research a brand properly; and brand types: bicycle makers, e-bike specialists and importers. Come back to the section you need when you are deciding, and use the related categories and guides linked above to compare real products in Australian dollars. If you would like help applying any of this to your own bike, your budget and where you ride, message the team on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 or use the contact page, and we will reply with clear, honest advice."]}],"faq":[{"q":"How do I compare e-bike brands?","a":"Compare the same type of bike across brands, then check motor, battery capacity, brake type, frame material and warranty support in Australia. Spare-part availability matters as much as the headline specification. Price should be judged against those points, not on its own."},{"q":"Does the brand matter more than the specs?","a":"Specs describe what the bike can do, while the brand tells you how well it is supported. A well-supported brand makes warranty claims, batteries and spare parts easier to get in Australia. Look at both, and favour clear specifications over marketing claims."},{"q":"Can I see all brands in one place?","a":"Yes. The brand pages list every product from that maker with current prices, and the shop can be filtered by category. Start with the brand hub for the make you are interested in, then compare it with another brand in the same category."},{"q":"Is an imported brand worse than an Australian-supported one?","a":"Not necessarily, but local support changes the ownership experience. Check who handles warranty, how parts are supplied and whether a battery replacement is available. Brands with a local distributor are usually easier to service than direct imports. See the sur-ron range for current models and prices."},{"q":"Where does Torque E-Bikes list each brand mentioned in this guide?","a":"Torque E-Bikes lists each brand mentioned in this guide in the sur-ron category, with the specification and the price in Australian dollars on each product page. The team can confirm fitment, stock or freight on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 before you order."}],"keywords":{"primary":"e-bike brands guide","supporting":["norco","ebox","focus bikes","orbea","malvern star bike"]},"words":1335},"e-bike-conversion-kits-explained":{"hook":"An e-bike conversion kit adds a motor, battery and controller to an existing bicycle. Kits above 250W are for off-road and private property use only in Australia.","intro":["A conversion kit adds a motor, battery and controller to a bicycle so it becomes an e-bike. It is often cheaper than buying a new e-bike if you already own a bike you like, but only if the bike suits the kit and you are happy to handle installation.","This guide covers what is in a kit, the main kit types, what to check before you buy and how Australian rules apply. When you are ready, our [conversion kit finder](/tools/conversion-kit-finder/) can point you to a suitable kit."],"sections":[{"h":"What a conversion kit includes","ul":["A motor: a hub motor built into a wheel, or a mid-drive motor that drives the chain.","A battery and charger, and sometimes a battery mount.","A controller, display and wiring harness.","Throttle or pedal-assist sensor, brake cut-off levers and fixings."],"p":["Check exactly what is in the box. Some kits omit the battery, and extras such as torque arms or a rack may be sold separately."]},{"h":"Hub motor or mid-drive?","p":["A hub kit replaces the front or rear wheel and is the simplest to fit. A mid-drive kit replaces the crankset and uses the bike’s gears, which suits hills and gives a natural feel, but it is more work to install and needs a compatible bottom bracket."]},{"h":"Will it fit your bike?","p":["Check the frame material and strength, the dropout width, the wheel size, the brake type and the room for a battery. High-powered motors put extra force on the dropouts, so heavy-duty kits need torque arms and a strong frame."],"ul":["Dropout width and axle size match the motor.","Wheel size and brake type (rim or disc) match the wheel.","There is space for the battery and wiring.","The frame can take the extra weight."]},{"h":"The 250W rule and off-road kits","p":["A pedal-assist kit that keeps the bike within the 250W limits can result in a road-legal e-bike. More powerful kits turn the bike into an off-road machine for private property use. Read our guide to [e-bike laws in Australia](/blog/off-road-vs-street-legal-australia/) before buying."]},{"h":"Batteries, brakes and safety","p":["The battery is the most important safety item in a kit. Buy a quality pack, use the supplied charger and follow our [battery care guide](/blog/electric-moto-maintenance-lithium-safety/). Upgrade the brakes if you add power: see the [brakes guide](/blog/e-bike-and-dirt-bike-brakes/).","Browse current kits and drivetrain parts in the [motors, drivetrain and sprockets](/shop/motors-drivetrain-and-sprockets/) category, and the [batteries, chargers and electronics](/shop/batteries-chargers-and-electronics/) category."]},{"h":"Hub motor kits in more detail","p":["Hub motor kits come as a complete wheel, a controller, a battery and a throttle or sensor. Geared hub motors are small and light, and they freewheel with little resistance, which suits road conversions. Direct-drive hub motors are heavier and can be very strong, but they produce drag when unpowered and are more common in higher-powered kits. The choice affects weight, noise, efficiency and how the bike rides when you switch the motor off.","When choosing a hub kit, check the wheel size, the dropout width, the brake type, the freewheel or cassette type and the cable exit position. A front hub kit changes steering feel and is gentler on the frame, and a rear hub kit gives better traction and uses the bike’s gears. For most riders, a rear geared hub is the best balance."]},{"h":"Mid-drive kits in more detail","p":["A mid-drive kit replaces the bottom bracket and crank with a motor unit that drives the chain. It uses the bike’s gears, so the motor can stay in an efficient speed range, and the weight is low and central, which improves handling. Installation requires the correct bottom bracket shell width and standard, and the chain, cassette and derailleur take extra load, so expect faster wear and shift carefully under power.","Mid-drive kits suit hilly terrain, touring and riders who want a natural feel. They cost more and take more time to fit than hub kits. Check frame compatibility carefully, because not all frames have room for the motor or allow the chain line it needs."]},{"h":"Controllers, displays and wiring","p":["The controller manages power between the battery and the motor, reads sensors and applies limits. A good controller has protection for over-current, low voltage and over-temperature. The display shows speed, battery and assist level, and some displays allow you to set limits. Use the connectors supplied, route cables away from moving parts, add cable ties and heat-shrink where needed and make sure no wire is pinched or stretched when the handlebars turn.","Waterproofing matters for connectors in the wet. Use sealed connectors or apply grease and heat-shrink to keep water out. Check connections regularly during the first few weeks, because vibration can loosen them."]},{"h":"Batteries for conversions","p":["Conversion batteries come in frame-mounted, rack-mounted and bottle-style forms. Choose a battery that matches the controller’s voltage and current rating, with a protection system built in. The capacity decides range and the weight affects handling, so mount the battery low and secure it firmly. A loose battery can damage the frame and wiring, and may become a hazard.","Never mix voltages, and never use a charger that is not made for your battery. If you are shipping or storing batteries, remember lithium cells are Class 9 dangerous goods, so follow the carrier’s rules and the maker’s storage advice. See the [battery care guide](/blog/electric-moto-maintenance-lithium-safety/)."]},{"h":"Brakes, tyres and wheels on a converted bike","p":["A motor adds weight and speed, so the brakes, tyres and wheels you already have may need upgrading. Rim brakes can struggle with the extra mass, particularly in the wet, so many riders switch to disc brakes. Tyres should be rated for the speed and load, and wheels need strong rims and spokes, especially with a hub motor. Fit brake cut-off sensors so the motor stops when you brake.","If you are unsure whether your bike can handle a conversion, ask a bike mechanic to inspect the frame, fork, brakes and wheels before you begin. It is cheaper to find a problem before you start than after you have fitted the kit."]},{"h":"Legal and insurance questions for converted bikes","p":["A converted bike is judged by what it is after conversion. If the motor, controller and settings keep it within the pedal-assist limits, it can be treated as a road-legal e-bike, subject to state rules. If it exceeds them, it is an off-road machine, and it cannot be ridden on public roads. Keep records of the kit’s specifications and settings. Check with your insurer whether a converted bike is covered, because some policies exclude modified bikes.","If you plan to sell the bike, tell the buyer what it is and what it is rated for. Selling a high-powered conversion as a standard bicycle can cause serious problems for both of you."]},{"h":"When a conversion is the wrong choice","ul":["The frame is carbon, very light or damaged.","The bike is a cheap, heavy model with poor brakes.","You need a warranty on the whole bike.","You do not want to spend time on installation and testing.","You want a bike that is guaranteed to meet the road rules without checking."],"p":["In these cases, a ready-made e-bike is usually the better choice. Read the [e-bike buying guide](/blog/best-electric-bikes-in-australia/) and browse [all electric bikes](/electric-bikes/)."]},{"h":"A step-by-step overview of a typical hub kit installation","p":["Every kit comes with its own instructions, and you should follow them, but the broad sequence is similar. First, check the parts against the packing list. Second, remove the wheel you are replacing and, if needed, transfer the tyre and tube or fit new ones. Third, fit the motor wheel to the dropouts with the torque arm and tighten the axle nuts to the specified torque. Fourth, mount the battery, run the wiring neatly and connect the controller, display, throttle or sensor and brake cut-offs. Fifth, check everything with the wheel off the ground, then ride slowly in a safe area.","Take photos before you begin, because they help you remember where cables went. Use cable ties and spiral wrap to keep wires tidy, and leave enough slack at the handlebars for steering. If a connector does not fit, do not force it."]},{"h":"Troubleshooting a new conversion","ul":["**The motor does not run:** check the battery switch, the connectors, the brake cut-off sensors and the display.","**The motor surges or cuts out:** check the connectors and the battery’s current rating.","**The display shows an error:** check the manual for the code, and check wiring.","**There is a clicking noise:** check the freewheel, spokes and axle nuts.","**The range is poor:** check tyre pressure, assist level and battery condition."],"p":["If a problem persists, stop riding and ask the kit seller or a bike mechanic for help. A faulty battery or controller can be unsafe."]},{"h":"Choosing between a kit and a ready-made e-bike","p":["A kit makes sense when you already own a bike you love, when you enjoy building and when you want to customise. A ready-made e-bike makes sense when you want a warranty, a tested design and a quicker start. If you are unsure, compare the whole cost: kit, battery, brake and tyre upgrades, tools and time. Also compare risk. A kit that does not suit your frame is a lot of money and effort wasted, while a ready-made bike can be returned if it does not suit."]},{"h":"Keeping a conversion reliable","p":["Check the torque arms, axle nuts, wiring and battery mount at regular intervals, and after any big ride on rough ground. Keep the chain clean and the tyres at the right pressure. Replace brake pads early, because the bike is heavier and faster than it was. Store the battery as the maker recommends and keep a record of the kit’s model numbers, in case you need a replacement part."]},{"h":"A parts checklist for a first conversion","ul":["The kit: motor, controller, display, throttle or sensor, brake levers with cut-offs and cabling.","A battery that matches the controller, with a mount.","A charger made for that battery.","Torque arms, spare nuts and washers.","Tyre levers, a spare tube and a pump.","Cable ties, heat-shrink, spiral wrap and electrical tape.","A torque wrench, metric hex keys, a multimeter and a crimping tool.","Thread locker and grease."]},{"h":"Questions to put to the kit seller","ul":["What are the motor’s continuous and peak power ratings?","What voltage and current does the controller support?","Is the battery included, and what is its capacity and protection?","What wheel sizes, dropout widths and brake types does the kit suit?","Is there a warranty, and who supplies spare parts?","Can the controller be programmed or limited?"]},{"h":"A realistic timeline and budget","p":["For a first-time installer, allow a full weekend. Day one is for checking the bike, fitting the wheel or motor and mounting the battery. Day two is for wiring, testing and adjustments. Budget for extras you did not expect: a few cables, a better lock, a brake upgrade or a rack. If something does not fit, you may need to wait for a part, so do not plan to use the bike the next day.","Do not be discouraged by the length of this guide. Many first conversions go smoothly when the kit suits the bike and the installer takes their time. The key is to check compatibility first, follow the instructions and test carefully."]},{"h":"Where to get help with a conversion","p":["A local bike shop can inspect your frame, check your brakes and wheels and sometimes fit the kit for you. Online forums can help with troubleshooting, but treat advice carefully and check it against the kit’s manual. If you are stuck, the kit seller should be able to help. Send them clear photos and the model numbers, and describe exactly what happens."]},{"h":"Key takeaways","ul":["A kit can convert your existing bike, but the frame, wheels and brakes must suit it.","A pedal-assist kit within the limits can result in a road-legal e-bike, and higher-powered kits are off-road only.","Check dropout width, wheel size and brake type before you order.","Budget for a battery, brake upgrades, tools and time.","Test carefully, and recheck bolts and wiring after the first rides."]},{"h":"Where a conversion kit fits among the options","p":["People usually consider three routes to an e-bike: buy a ready-made bike, convert a bike they own, or build a custom machine from parts. A ready-made bike is the simplest, with a warranty and a design that has been tested as a system. A conversion lets you keep a bike you like and choose your own motor and battery, but you take on responsibility for fit and safety. A custom build suits experienced builders who want specific parts and are comfortable with electrical work.","If you are new to e-bikes, test ride a few ready-made bikes first so you know what you like. If you then decide a conversion is the right way, you will know which motor feel and assist levels to look for."]},{"h":"A last word on choosing a kit","p":["Take your time choosing a kit, and check compatibility carefully. Measure your bike, read the instructions and ask questions. A conversion done well gives you a bike that you built and understand, and one that serves you for years. If you are unsure at any point, ask for help. The [conversion kit finder](/tools/conversion-kit-finder/) can narrow your options, and the team can check your measurements before you order."]},{"h":"Installation: what to expect","p":["Fitting a hub-motor kit usually means removing your wheel, fitting the motor wheel and torque arms, mounting the battery, running the wiring and setting up the controller, display and brake cut-off sensors. A confident home mechanic can often do this in a day. A mid-drive kit takes longer, because the crankset and bottom bracket come off.","Take your time with the wiring: connectors must be fully seated, wires secured away from moving parts and the battery mounted so it cannot move. If you are not confident with the electrical side, ask a bike shop or a qualified person to check the work before you ride."]},{"h":"How much does a conversion really cost?","p":["The kit price is only part of the total. Add a battery if it is not included, any brake or wheel upgrades, torque arms, a rack for the battery, tools and your time or a workshop’s labour. Compare that total with a ready-made e-bike of the same type before you decide, and remember that a ready-made bike is designed as a system."]},{"h":"Common mistakes","ul":["Choosing a kit that is too powerful for the frame.","Ignoring torque arms on high-powered motors.","Using the wrong battery connector or an unprotected battery.","Leaving the old brakes in place on a faster, heavier bike.","Assuming a converted bike is road legal without checking the rules."]},{"h":"Looking after a converted bike","p":["Check bolts, torque arms and wiring regularly in the first weeks of use, because vibration loosens things. Keep the chain clean and aligned (see the [chain care guide](/blog/chain-alignment-high-torque-motors/)), look after the battery, and replace brake pads and tyres early rather than late."]},{"h":"Choosing power, voltage and battery for your build","p":["Voltage and power are related but not the same. A 36V or 48V kit is common for road-style conversions that stay near the 250W limit, while higher-voltage systems are used in off-road builds. Higher voltage lets a motor deliver more power at lower current, but it also means more serious wiring, a stronger controller and more care with safety.","Match the battery to the kit: the voltage must match the controller, the connector must suit the wiring and the pack must be able to supply the current the motor will draw. A battery that cannot supply enough current will cut out under load, and one that is oversized for the controller can damage it."]},{"h":"A worked example: converting a commuter bike","p":["Say you own a hybrid bike with rim brakes and a 700c rear wheel, and you want an e-bike for a 12 km commute with a few hills. A rear hub-motor kit built for a 700c wheel with a 250W motor, a 36V battery of around 400 to 500Wh and a basic display would fit the job. The steps are to check the rear dropout width and axle size, order a kit with the right wheel size, fit the wheel and torque arm, mount the battery and wiring, set up the brake cut-offs and test the system before you ride.","The same bike could take a mid-drive kit if you want to use the gears on hills, but you would need a compatible bottom bracket and more installation time. For most commuters the hub kit is the simpler choice."]},{"h":"Checklist before you buy a kit","ul":["Frame material and condition: look for cracks and check the dropouts are strong.","Rear dropout width (for example 135 mm on many mountain and hybrid bikes) and axle type.","Wheel size and rim width for the motor wheel.","Brake type: rim or disc, and the room for a disc rotor on a hub motor.","Gear type: cassette or freewheel, and the number of speeds.","Battery location: frame mount, rack mount or bottle-cage style.","Display, throttle or pedal-assist sensor needs.","Warranty, return policy and spare parts for the motor and controller."]},{"h":"Safety, testing and servicing after the build","p":["Test the system with the wheel off the ground first, then ride slowly in an empty area. Check that the brake cut-offs work, that the motor does not surge and that all connectors and bolts stay tight. After the first few rides, re-check the torque arms, axle nuts and wiring, because vibration loosens things.","Keep records of what you fitted, the battery model and the date. If you sell or lend the bike later, the next owner should know it has been converted and what it is rated for. See also our [DIY conversion planning guide](/blog/diy-2000w-conversion-guide/) for off-road builds."]},{"h":"Where to buy parts and who can help","p":["Browse kits, motors and drivetrain parts in the [motors, drivetrain and sprockets](/shop/motors-drivetrain-and-sprockets/) category and batteries in the [batteries, chargers and electronics](/shop/batteries-chargers-and-electronics/) category. If you are unsure whether a kit suits your bike, send the team your bike’s make, model, wheel size and brake type and we will tell you what we think will fit before you order."]},{"h":"Quick answers from people thinking about a conversion","ul":["**Can I convert a carbon frame?** Many makers advise against hub motor conversions on carbon frames because of clamping and torque loads. Check with the frame maker.","**Will a conversion void my bike’s warranty?** It can. Check the frame warranty before you modify the bike.","**How long does it take?** A confident home mechanic can fit a hub kit in a few hours to a day, and a mid-drive kit takes longer.","**Can I take the kit off later?** Usually yes, if you keep the original wheel and parts, but check for drilled holes or modified dropouts.","**Do I need special tools?** Common bike tools, a torque wrench and basic electrical tools such as crimpers and a multimeter."]},{"h":"Total cost and time planning","p":["Add up the kit, battery, brake upgrades, tools, accessories and your time. Compare that total with a ready-made e-bike that includes a warranty and support. If the numbers are close, the ready-made bike is usually the lower-risk choice. If you love your existing bike or want a custom build, a conversion can be very satisfying."]},{"h":"Kit or ready-made e-bike?","p":["A kit can cost less if you already own a suitable bike, but you also pay for a battery, tools and time. A ready-made e-bike is simpler and its parts are designed to work together. If you are unsure, compare the full cost of each before you decide."]},{"h":"Quick recap","p":["In this guide we covered what a conversion kit includes; hub motor or mid-drive?; will it fit your bike?; the 250W rule and off-road kits; batteries, brakes and safety; and hub motor kits in more detail. Come back to the section you need when you are deciding, and use the related categories and guides linked above to compare real products in Australian dollars. If you would like help applying any of this to your own bike, your budget and where you ride, message the team on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 or use the contact page, and we will reply with clear, honest advice."]}],"faq":[{"q":"What does an e-bike conversion kit include?","a":"A kit normally includes a motor, battery, controller, throttle or pedal sensor, display and wiring. Some kits add torque arms and a charger. Check the contents list carefully, because brakes, cranks and mounting hardware are often sold separately. See the motors, drivetrain & sprockets range for current models and prices."},{"q":"Can any bicycle be converted to electric?","a":"Many bicycles can be converted, but the frame, wheel size, dropout spacing and brake type must suit the motor you choose. High-power kits need a strong frame and good brakes. Ask the team on WhatsApp to check compatibility before you order."},{"q":"Does Torque E-Bikes sell conversion kits?","a":"Yes. The motors, drivetrain and sprockets category lists conversion kits, motors, torque arms and drivetrain parts. Each listing shows the motor power, and kits above 250W are marked as off-road and private property use only. Open the motors, drivetrain & sprockets category to see everything that is currently in stock."},{"q":"Is a conversion kit cheaper than buying a new e-bike?","a":"A kit can cost less if you already own a suitable bike, but the total depends on the battery, installation time and any parts that need upgrading. Compare the full cost of the kit and upgrades against a ready-made e-bike in the same category."},{"q":"Where does Torque E-Bikes list conversion kits?","a":"Torque E-Bikes lists conversion kits in the motors, drivetrain & sprockets category, with the specification and the price in Australian dollars on each product page. The team can confirm fitment, stock or freight on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 before you order."}],"keywords":{"primary":"conversion kit","supporting":["cycle conversion kit","conversion kit with bike","conversion kit guide","how to choose conversion kit","conversion kit explained"]},"words":3154},"best-electric-dirt-bikes-in-australia":{"hook":"The best electric dirt bike depends on rider size, terrain and budget. Lightweight models suit beginners and trails, while higher-power bikes suit experienced riders on tracks.","intro":["An electric dirt bike is a battery-powered off-road motorbike with no fuel, no clutch and a quiet motor. In Australia, high-powered models are for off-road and private property use only, so the first question is where you will ride.","Rather than name a single winner, this guide shows how to choose between models by rider, power and battery, then points you to the range so you can compare real bikes."],"sections":[{"h":"Decide where you will ride","p":["Private property, a licensed track or an approved off-road area are the places to ride a dirt bike. Check the land owner’s rules and your state’s requirements first. Dirt bikes are not road legal, and we do not sell them as street-legal machines."]},{"h":"Match the bike to the rider","p":["Seat height, weight and power delivery matter more than headline numbers. A lighter, lower-powered bike is easier to learn on and is usually the better first purchase for an adult beginner. Young riders need a model sized to their height with a power limiter: see the [kids’ electric dirt bike guide](/blog/kids-electric-dirt-bikes/)."]},{"h":"Motor power and battery capacity","p":["More power gives stronger acceleration and more speed, but it is harder to control and drains the battery faster. Battery capacity decides how long you can ride. Compare the motor rating, voltage and battery size in the product specification, and keep in mind that real riding time depends on terrain, rider weight and how hard you ride."]},{"h":"Popular brands and models","p":["Sur-Ron and Talaria are the names most riders ask about. Read the [Sur-Ron models guide](/blog/sur-ron-models-explained/) and the [Talaria guide](/blog/talaria-electric-dirt-bikes/) for how the ranges are laid out, then browse every [electric dirt bike](/shop/electric-dirt-bikes/) we list."]},{"h":"Key takeaways","ul":["Electric dirt bikes are for off-road and private property use only.","Choose a bike that suits the rider’s size and experience, with a low-power mode to learn on.","Check battery capacity, brakes, suspension and parts support.","Plan where you will ride and wear full protective gear.","Look after the battery and drivetrain, and check the bike before every ride."]},{"h":"Where electric dirt bikes fit among other off-road bikes","p":["Electric dirt bikes sit between mountain bikes and petrol motorbikes. They are faster and heavier than a mountain bike, and quieter and simpler than a petrol bike. This makes them a good fit for riders who want trail fun without the noise and maintenance of an engine, and for families who want to share a hobby. They also suit riders who are happy to charge and plan rides around battery life."]},{"h":"Finding a bike that grows with the rider","p":["Some riders want a bike they can keep for years. Look for models with adjustable suspension, riding modes and common parts. A bike with a spare battery option and a good aftermarket can adapt as your skills grow. Be realistic, though: if you progress quickly, you may want a bigger bike later, and a good bike keeps its resale value if you look after it. Keep records and the original parts to help when you sell."]},{"h":"Choosing a bike for a beginner adult","p":["If you have never ridden an off-road motorbike, choose a lighter bike with a low-power mode and learn the throttle gently. Electric power is instant, so a bike that feels tame in a low mode can surprise you in a high one. Take a first ride in an open area away from obstacles.","Taller or heavier riders should check the seat height, suspension and rider weight limit. A bike that is too small or too weak is uncomfortable and wears out faster."]},{"h":"Weight, handling and suspension","p":["Weight affects how easy the bike is to pick up, load and manoeuvre. Suspension travel and quality affect comfort and control on rough ground. A heavier bike with better suspension can be easier to ride fast, while a lighter bike is easier to learn on. Choose for how you ride, not for the highest number on the specification."]},{"h":"Charging, ride time and spare batteries","p":["Ride time depends on battery size and how hard you ride. For long days, consider a second battery or a larger pack, and plan where you will charge. Follow the maker’s charging instructions and the safety advice in the [battery care guide](/blog/electric-moto-maintenance-lithium-safety/)."]},{"h":"Running costs and maintenance","p":["Electric dirt bikes have no engine oil, filters or spark plugs, so routine servicing is simpler. You still need to look after brakes, tyres, chain, sprockets and the battery. Budget for wear items such as tyres and brake pads, and for protective gear."]},{"h":"Understanding riding modes and power delivery","p":["Most electric dirt bikes let you choose between modes that limit power and sometimes top speed. A low mode softens the throttle and is ideal for learning, a middle mode suits general trail riding, and a high mode gives full power for experienced riders. Take the time to learn each one in an open space before you ride on tighter trails.","Because electric torque arrives instantly, the way the throttle is mapped matters. A bike with smooth, progressive delivery is easier to control than one that jumps on the first twist. If you can, ask riders who own the model how it feels in the lowest mode."]},{"h":"Where to ride legally and responsibly","p":["In Australia, high-powered electric dirt bikes are for private property and approved off-road areas only. Public parks, footpaths, roads and most bushland are off limits. Look for licensed motocross tracks, private land where you have permission and venues that accept electric bikes, and always check the operator’s rules and any age or licence requirements first.","Quiet bikes are easier on neighbours, but they still need courtesy: keep to agreed hours, stay inside boundaries and respect other users. Our [e-bike laws guide](/blog/off-road-vs-street-legal-australia/) explains why these bikes cannot be used on public roads."]},{"h":"A short pre-ride checklist","ul":["Check tyre pressure and look for damage.","Test both brakes and make sure the levers feel firm.","Check the chain tension and sprocket bolts.","Check the battery is secure and charged.","Wear a motocross helmet, goggles, gloves, boots and body armour."]},{"h":"Buying checklist for an electric dirt bike","ul":["Rider height, weight and experience match the bike.","Motor power and riding modes suit the riding area.","Battery capacity and charger are appropriate, with the option of a spare.","Brakes, suspension and tyres suit the terrain.","Parts, warranty and support are available in Australia.","Delivery arrangements for a lithium battery are clear.","Protective gear is included in the budget."]},{"h":"Adult, youth and mini bikes","p":["Adult bikes are larger, with more power and suspension. Youth bikes are sized for teenagers and smaller adults, with lower power. Mini bikes and balance bikes suit children. Choose the size that suits the rider now, not the one they might grow into. See the [kids’ electric dirt bike guide](/blog/kids-electric-dirt-bikes/) and the [Yozma IN10 guide](/blog/yozma-in10-electric-mini-dirt-bike/)."]},{"h":"Frequently asked questions from new riders","ul":["**Do I need a licence?** Rules depend on where you ride. On private land there is usually no road licence, but check the venue’s and landowner’s requirements.","**Can I ride it on the road?** No. Electric dirt bikes are off-road only.","**How long does charging take?** It depends on the battery and charger, so check the product page.","**Can I ride in the rain?** Check the maker’s guidance, and avoid submerging the bike or pressure-washing electrical parts.","**What is the best first bike?** A lighter bike with riding modes that suits your size is usually the best start."]},{"h":"Maintenance schedule","ul":["After every ride: wipe the bike, check tyres and brakes.","Every month: lubricate and check chain tension, check bolts.","Every few months: check brake pads, rotors, bearings and suspension.","Every year: service suspension and replace worn drivetrain parts."]},{"h":"Gear, charging and care","p":["Wear a certified helmet, goggles, gloves, boots and body armour every time you ride: see our [protective gear guide](/blog/helmets-and-protective-gear-for-e-bikes/). Follow the maker’s charging instructions and our [battery care guide](/blog/electric-moto-maintenance-lithium-safety/), and keep the drivetrain clean and tight with the [chain care guide](/blog/chain-alignment-high-torque-motors/)."]},{"h":"Quick recap","p":["In this guide we covered decide where you will ride; match the bike to the rider; motor power and battery capacity; popular brands and models; where electric dirt bikes fit among other off-road bikes; and finding a bike that grows with the rider. Come back to the section you need when you are deciding, and use the related categories and guides linked above to compare real products in Australian dollars. If you would like help applying any of this to your own bike, your budget and where you ride, message the team on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 or use the contact page, and we will reply with clear, honest advice."]}],"faq":[{"q":"Are electric dirt bikes good for beginners?","a":"Electric dirt bikes have no clutch or gearbox and deliver smooth power, which many beginners find easier to learn on. Choose a lightweight model, start in a low power mode if available and wear full protective gear. Ride only on private property or approved off-road areas."},{"q":"How long does an electric dirt bike battery last?","a":"Ride time depends on battery capacity, terrain, rider weight and how hard the bike is ridden. Treat the manufacturer figure as a guide and expect less on steep or sandy ground. A second battery or a fast charger extends your riding day."},{"q":"Which electric dirt bike brands does Torque E-Bikes list?","a":"The range includes Sur-Ron, Talaria, Segway, KTM, Husqvarna and other makers, plus kids’ electric dirt bikes. Open the electric dirt bikes category and filter by brand to see current models and prices. Prices are in Australian dollars and each product page lists its specifications."},{"q":"Is an electric dirt bike better than a petrol one?","a":"Electric bikes are quieter, need less maintenance and have no fuel or clutch, but ride time is limited by the battery. Petrol bikes refuel quickly and suit very long days. The better choice depends on where you ride and how long you ride for."},{"q":"Where does Torque E-Bikes list electric dirt bikes for adults?","a":"Torque E-Bikes lists electric dirt bikes for adults in the electric dirt bikes category, with the specification and the price in Australian dollars on each product page. The team can confirm fitment, stock or freight on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 before you order."}],"keywords":{"primary":"electric dirt bikes guide","supporting":["electric dirt bikes in guide","how to choose electric dirt bikes in","electric dirt bikes in explained","electric dirt bikes in for beginners","electric dirt bikes in tips"]},"words":1327},"sur-ron-models-explained":{"hook":"Sur-Ron makes several electric off-road bike models, including the Light Bee, Ultra Bee, Storm Bee and Hyper Bee. They differ in power, battery and intended use.","intro":["Sur-Ron makes some of the best-known electric off-road bikes, and its model names can be confusing. The Light Bee is the lightweight bike most people picture, the Storm Bee is a larger, heavier-duty platform, and the Ultra Bee sits at the high-power end.","This guide explains how the range is organised and what to compare. Specifications change between model years, so use it to build a shortlist, then confirm the details on the product page and with the manufacturer."],"sections":[{"h":"The Sur-Ron family at a glance","ul":["**Light Bee (including Light Bee X and Light Bee S):** the lightweight bikes, popular for trails and mixed riding.","**Storm Bee:** a larger platform built for harder off-road use.","**Ultra Bee:** the high-power model, for experienced riders.","**Hyper Bee and others:** newer variants that appear from time to time. Check the current listing."]},{"h":"Light Bee X and Light Bee S","p":["The Light Bee X and Light Bee S are the usual starting points. They are light for an electric off-road bike, which makes them approachable, but they are still powerful bikes that need protective gear and a safe place to ride. See current listings in the [electric dirt bikes](/shop/electric-dirt-bikes/) category."]},{"h":"Storm Bee and Ultra Bee","p":["Larger, more powerful models give stronger acceleration and more capability on rough terrain, but they ask more of the rider. If you are new to dirt bikes, start with something lighter and move up when you are confident."]},{"h":"What to compare between models","ul":["Motor power and how it is delivered (riding modes).","Battery voltage and capacity, which decide how long you can ride.","Weight, seat height and suspension travel.","Rider size and experience level.","Spare parts and upgrade availability: see [Sur-Ron spares](/shop/sur-ron-spares/)."]},{"h":"Choosing your first electric dirt bike from the Sur-Ron family","p":["A first bike should be easy to handle and forgiving. In the Sur-Ron family, that usually points to the Light Bee, because it is lighter and easier to manoeuvre than heavier models. Start in a low riding mode, in an open area, with full gear. As you gain experience, you can use higher modes and tackle more demanding terrain.","If you are tall or heavy, check the rider limits and suspension settings. If you are shorter, check the seat height and that you can reach the ground. If you are unsure, send the team your height and weight and ask for advice."]},{"h":"Typical uses for each Sur-Ron type","ul":["**Light Bee:** trail riding, learning, family use on private land.","**Storm Bee:** tougher terrain and heavier riders.","**Ultra Bee:** experienced riders who want more performance.","**Others:** check the product page for the intended use."]},{"h":"Customisation and parts","p":["Sur-Ron bikes are popular for customisation, with a wide range of tyres, wheels, suspension, brakes and body parts. Choose upgrades that match your model and year, and fit them correctly. Check that changes do not breach warranty or venue rules, and keep the original parts. See the [Sur-Ron spares](/shop/sur-ron-spares/) category."]},{"h":"Transport and storage","p":["A Sur-Ron is light enough for two people to lift, but it needs a proper tie-down in a ute, trailer or van. Remove the battery if you can, and secure the bike at several points. Store it indoors, away from moisture and heat, with the battery at a partial charge."]},{"h":"Common mistakes first-time Sur-Ron buyers make","ul":["Choosing a bike that is too powerful for their experience.","Skipping protective gear or buying cheap gear.","Not planning where they will ride.","Ignoring brake and tyre upgrades.","Forgetting about transport and charging."]},{"h":"Buying new or used, and what to check","p":["If you consider a used bike, ask for the model year, service history and battery condition. Check the frame, swingarm and suspension for damage, inspect the tyres, brakes and chain, and ask about modifications. Battery health is the key: ask how old the battery is and how many cycles it has had, and ask to see it charge. Buying new gives you a warranty and known condition, which suits beginners.","Whichever you choose, check the paperwork, serial numbers and that the bike is not stolen. Ask questions, take your time and be wary of prices that seem too good."]},{"h":"A last word on choosing a Sur-Ron","p":["A Sur-Ron can be a brilliant machine in the right hands. Choose the model that suits your size and skill, plan where you will ride and buy gear before the bike arrives. Keep up with maintenance, respect the rules and ride within your limits. If you are unsure which model to choose, talk to us. We will help you narrow the options and be honest about what we think will suit you."]},{"h":"Choosing between the Light Bee and the Storm Bee","p":["The Light Bee suits riders who want a light, easy-to-handle bike for trails and mixed riding. The Storm Bee is a heavier-duty platform for riders who want more capability on rough terrain and are comfortable with a larger bike. If you are not sure, the lighter bike is usually the safer starting point, and you can always move up later."]},{"h":"Upgrades and spares","p":["Sur-Ron bikes have a strong aftermarket for wheels, suspension, brakes, swingarms and charging parts. Always match each part to your exact model and year, because components are not always interchangeable between versions. See the [Sur-Ron spares](/shop/sur-ron-spares/) category and ask the team to confirm fitment."]},{"h":"Model years and what changes","p":["Specifications and parts change between model years, so a part for an earlier Light Bee may not suit a newer one. When you compare bikes, compare the same model year, and check what is included, such as the charger and any tools."]},{"h":"A simple way to shortlist a Sur-Ron","ul":["List the riders: height, weight and experience.","List the terrain: tight trails, open tracks, hills or sand.","Pick the lightest bike that handles that terrain, because weight makes the bike easier to learn and recover.","Check which riding modes you get and whether they can be limited.","Check parts, charger and warranty before comparing price."]},{"h":"Charging, batteries and range planning","p":["Charging time and range vary by battery size and charger. Plan rides around the battery you have, carry a spare if you want a long day, and charge in a ventilated spot away from anything flammable. Do not leave the battery charging unattended, and store it partly charged if the bike will sit for weeks. Our [battery care guide](/blog/electric-moto-maintenance-lithium-safety/) has the details."]},{"h":"Real-world questions Sur-Ron buyers ask","ul":["**Is it too much bike for a beginner?** Lighter models in a low riding mode are manageable, but power is instant, so learn in an open area with full gear.","**Can a taller or heavier rider use a Light Bee?** Check the rider limits and think about suspension setup, because very tall riders can find the bike small.","**How long does a ride last?** It depends on the battery, terrain and mode, so plan around a margin and consider a spare battery.","**What do I need to buy with the bike?** Protective gear, a lock and tools, and a safe place to charge."]},{"h":"Keeping a Sur-Ron reliable","p":["Wear items such as tyres, brake pads, chains and sprockets need regular checks, and the aftermarket offers many parts. Keep a log of what you replace, tighten bolts often because off-road riding shakes them loose, and keep the battery connectors clean and dry. See [Sur-Ron spares](/shop/sur-ron-spares/) and the [chain care guide](/blog/chain-alignment-high-torque-motors/)."]},{"h":"Safety, supervision and riding area","p":["A Sur-Ron is a capable machine. Learn the controls in an open area, wear full protective gear and ride only where you have permission. Do not ride alone in remote areas, tell someone where you are going, and carry a phone and first aid kit. Check brakes, tyres and bolts before every ride."]},{"h":"Rules and gear","p":["Sur-Ron bikes are for off-road and private property use only in Australia. They are not road legal, and we do not describe them as street legal. Read our guide to [e-bike laws in Australia](/blog/off-road-vs-street-legal-australia/) and wear full protective gear: see the [gear guide](/blog/helmets-and-protective-gear-for-e-bikes/).","Curious about speed and range? Read [how fast a Sur-Ron goes](/blog/how-fast-does-a-sur-ron-go/)."]},{"h":"Quick recap","p":["In this guide we covered the Sur-Ron family at a glance; light Bee X and Light Bee S; storm Bee and Ultra Bee; what to compare between models; choosing your first electric dirt bike from the Sur-Ron family; and typical uses for each Sur-Ron type. Come back to the section you need when you are deciding, and use the related categories and guides linked above to compare real products in Australian dollars. If you would like help applying any of this to your own bike, your budget and where you ride, message the team on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 or use the contact page, and we will reply with clear, honest advice."]}],"faq":[{"q":"What is the difference between the Sur-Ron Light Bee and Ultra Bee?","a":"The Ultra Bee is the larger, more powerful of the two, while the Light Bee is lighter and aimed at trail and everyday riding. Compare the manufacturer’s published motor, battery and weight figures for the exact model year before choosing."},{"q":"Are Sur-Ron bikes road legal in Australia?","a":"No. Sur-Ron bikes are off-road electric motorbikes and are not road legal or registrable in Australia. Use them only on private property or approved off-road areas, and check local rules for where riding is permitted. See the sur-ron range for current models and prices."},{"q":"Does Torque E-Bikes stock Sur-Ron bikes and parts?","a":"Yes. The Sur-Ron brand page lists the bikes currently available plus spares and accessories. Browse the Sur-Ron spares category for swingarms, wheels, charging parts and more, or ask the team to confirm fitment for your model. Open the sur-ron category to see everything that is currently in stock."},{"q":"Which Sur-Ron model is best for a first-time buyer?","a":"A lighter, lower-powered model is usually easier to learn on, but the right choice depends on rider size and terrain. Compare the models in the same price band and keep budget for protective gear and a spare battery. Message the team on WhatsApp if you want advice on the sur-ron range."},{"q":"Where does Torque E-Bikes list Sur-Ron models?","a":"Torque E-Bikes lists Sur-Ron models in the sur-ron category, with the specification and the price in Australian dollars on each product page. The team can confirm fitment, stock or freight on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 before you order. Prices are in Australian dollars and each product page lists its specifications."}],"keywords":{"primary":"surron lightbee","supporting":["surron hyperbee","surron ultra bee hp","e bikes ultra bee","surron lightbee x","surron lightbee guide"]},"words":1336},"how-fast-does-a-sur-ron-go":{"hook":"A Sur-Ron’s top speed depends on the model, the controller setting, the rider’s weight and the terrain. Published figures differ between models, so check the manufacturer’s numbers for the exact bike.","intro":["How fast a Sur-Ron goes depends on the model, the riding mode, the gearing, the rider’s weight and the surface. The number in a specification is a best-case figure, and real-world speed and range are usually lower.","This guide explains what actually decides speed and range so you can read a specification sensibly. These bikes are for off-road and private property use only in Australia."],"sections":[{"h":"What decides top speed","ul":["The motor’s power and the controller’s limits.","The riding mode selected on the bike.","Gearing: the front and rear sprocket sizes change how the power is used.","Rider weight, terrain and wind.","Tyres and tyre pressure."]},{"h":"Why range is never one number","p":["Battery capacity sets the energy you start with, but how you ride sets how quickly it is used. Fast riding, steep hills, soft sand and heavy riders all shorten range. Riding gently in a low power mode stretches it.","A useful habit is to plan rides around half of the maker’s quoted range until you learn how your bike behaves."]},{"h":"Reading a specification","p":["Look at the battery voltage and capacity, the motor’s continuous and peak power, the weight and the gearing. Compare the same measures across models rather than relying on top speed alone. If a number is missing or unclear, ask the seller or the manufacturer before you order."]},{"h":"Speed, power and battery: how the pieces fit together","p":["An electric dirt bike’s performance comes from a chain of parts. The battery supplies energy and sets how much current it can deliver. The controller decides how much of that current goes to the motor, and applies the riding mode’s limits. The motor turns current into torque. The drivetrain, with its sprockets and chain, multiplies that torque and sets the relationship between motor speed and wheel speed. Change any link in the chain and the others feel it.","That is why two bikes with similar motors can feel very different. A bike with a limiting controller or a smaller battery may never reach the motor’s peak, and a bike with a different gear ratio may accelerate harder but run out of top speed sooner. When you compare bikes, compare the whole system, not only the motor figure."]},{"h":"Estimating your own range","p":["You can estimate range from battery capacity and consumption. If the battery is rated at around 2,000Wh and your riding uses 40 Wh per kilometre on hilly trails, you can expect roughly 50 km. If you ride harder or on sand and use 80 Wh per kilometre, that falls to around 25 km. These numbers are only examples, but they show why the same bike can have very different ranges depending on how and where it is ridden. Track your own consumption over a few rides to build a realistic picture."]},{"h":"Heat, cold and battery performance","p":["Batteries perform best at moderate temperatures. In very hot weather, the controller may reduce power to protect the system, and charging should wait until the battery has cooled. In cold weather, range can drop noticeably, and the battery should be warmed before charging. Keep the bike out of direct sun when parked, and avoid riding hard in extreme heat."]},{"h":"Safe speeds for different terrain","ul":["On tight single-track, keep speeds low enough to stop within your sight line.","On open fire roads, watch for ruts, loose gravel and other users.","On sand, keep power smooth and avoid sudden braking.","On wet ground, reduce speed and brake early.","Never ride faster than your skill and your brakes can handle."]},{"h":"Planning upgrades responsibly","p":["If you plan to increase performance, upgrade brakes, tyres and suspension first, and then consider power. Keep records of changes, and check that modifications do not breach warranty or venue rules. A faster bike is not always a better bike, and the best upgrade is often better riding skill and gear."]},{"h":"Questions riders ask about range","p":["Riders often ask how far they can ride on one charge. The honest answer is that it depends on the battery, terrain, rider weight, riding mode and style. A gentle ride on firm trails can go much further than a hard ride on sand and hills. Rather than rely on a single number, build your own estimate by noting battery use over a few rides.","If you need more range, consider a second battery, a lower riding mode or a smoother riding style. Avoid leaving the battery fully discharged, because it can shorten its life, and plan rides so that you return with some charge left."]},{"h":"A last word on speed and range","p":["Speed and range are headline numbers, but control and safety matter more. Learn the bike, use the right mode and plan rides with a margin. Wear full gear and ride where it is allowed. If you want to know more about a specific model, ask us."]},{"h":"Gearing and sprocket changes","p":["Changing the front or rear sprocket changes how the motor’s power is used. A larger rear sprocket (or smaller front) gives stronger acceleration and a lower top speed, and the reverse gives a higher top speed with less acceleration. Match the change to how you ride, and keep the chain aligned and in good condition: see the [chain care guide](/blog/chain-alignment-high-torque-motors/)."]},{"h":"Riding modes and power delivery","p":["Most electric dirt bikes offer riding modes that limit power. Use a lower mode while you learn the bike and on tight terrain, and a higher mode only when you are confident and in a suitable open area. Modes also stretch the range."]},{"h":"Speed and the rules","p":["Speed on private property is your responsibility, and these bikes are not road legal in Australia. See the [e-bike laws guide](/blog/off-road-vs-street-legal-australia/). Wear full protective gear every time you ride."]},{"h":"Planning a ride around real range","p":["Range depends on how you ride more than on the figure in the brochure. Gentle trail riding in a low mode can stretch a battery, while repeated hard acceleration, hill climbs and sand drain it quickly. A useful approach is to ride your first few sessions with the battery display visible, note how much charge a typical ride uses, then plan future rides with a margin.","If you regularly run out of charge before you want to stop, a second battery or a larger pack is more useful than chasing a higher top speed."]},{"h":"Keeping the bike safe at higher speeds","ul":["Check brake pads and rotor condition before every ride.","Keep tyres at a pressure suited to the surface.","Check the chain, sprockets and axle bolts regularly.","Wear full protective gear every time, including body armour.","Ride within your ability and in areas where you can stop safely."]},{"h":"Common myths about electric dirt bike speed","ul":["“Top speed is what matters.” For most trail riding, torque delivery and control matter more.","“More power always means more fun.” More power needs more skill, better brakes and better gear.","“The advertised range is what you will get.” Real range depends heavily on terrain and rider.","“All modes are the same.” Modes can change power, throttle response and sometimes speed limits."]},{"h":"Choosing settings for different rides","p":["A low mode suits learning, tight trails and long rides. A medium mode suits general trail riding. A high mode suits open tracks and experienced riders. Switch modes only when the bike is stopped or you are in a safe, open area, and learn how the bike behaves in each mode."]},{"h":"Questions to ask about any figure you read online","ul":["Which model year and version is it about?","Was it measured on a flat road or on a trail?","What was the rider’s weight and the mode used?","Was the speed measured by GPS, or read from the display?","Who measured it, and could someone else repeat it?"]},{"h":"Going faster is a safety decision","p":["Higher speed means longer stopping distances and more severe crashes. Upgrading power without upgrading brakes and protective gear is a poor trade. See our [brakes guide](/blog/e-bike-and-dirt-bike-brakes/) and [protective gear guide](/blog/helmets-and-protective-gear-for-e-bikes/).","Compare the full range in the [Sur-Ron models guide](/blog/sur-ron-models-explained/) and browse [electric dirt bikes](/shop/electric-dirt-bikes/)."]},{"h":"Quick recap","p":["In this guide we covered what decides top speed; why range is never one number; reading a specification; speed, power and battery: how the pieces fit together; estimating your own range; and heat, cold and battery performance. Come back to the section you need when you are deciding, and use the related categories and guides linked above to compare real products in Australian dollars. If you would like help applying any of this to your own bike, your budget and where you ride, message the team on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 or use the contact page, and we will reply with clear, honest advice."]}],"faq":[{"q":"How fast does a Sur-Ron go?","a":"Top speed varies by model, controller mode, rider weight and surface, so there is no single figure. Use the manufacturer’s published speed for your exact model as the reference, and remember the bikes are for off-road and private property use only."},{"q":"What affects a Sur-Ron’s range?","a":"Range changes with battery capacity, rider weight, terrain, temperature and how hard the throttle is used. Riding in a lower power mode and on flatter ground stretches range. Carry a spare battery if you plan a long day. See the sur-ron range for current models and prices."},{"q":"Where can I see current Sur-Ron models and prices?","a":"The Sur-Ron brand page lists every model currently available with Australian-dollar prices, and each product page links to the relevant parts. Message the team on WhatsApp if you want help choosing between models. Open the sur-ron category to see everything that is currently in stock."},{"q":"Is a faster bike always better?","a":"Not always. A very fast bike is harder to control for newer riders and uses battery quickly. Choose a model and power mode that matches your skill, your terrain and the protective gear you own. Message the team on WhatsApp if you want advice on the sur-ron range."},{"q":"Where does Torque E-Bikes list Sur-Ron bikes and spares?","a":"Torque E-Bikes lists Sur-Ron bikes and spares in the sur-ron category, with the specification and the price in Australian dollars on each product page. The team can confirm fitment, stock or freight on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 before you order."}],"keywords":{"primary":"how fast do a surron go","supporting":["how fast does a surron go","surron ultra bee range","how fast do a surron go guide","how fast do a surron go explained","how fast do a surron go for beginners"]},"words":1334},"talaria-electric-dirt-bikes":{"hook":"Talaria makes electric off-road bikes including the Sting, Komodo and XXX. This guide compares the models listed at Torque E-Bikes by rider type and price.","intro":["Talaria builds a range of electric off-road bikes, and the model names (Sting, XXX, Komodo and others) cover frames with different power levels and rider sizes. Choosing between them comes down to how you ride and how experienced you are.","This guide explains the families, what to compare and how Talaria bikes sit alongside Sur-Ron. Bikes are for off-road and private property use only in Australia."],"sections":[{"h":"The Talaria families","ul":["**Sting:** performance off-road models in several versions.","**XXX:** a full-size model with its own range, plus the smaller XXX Mini.","**Komodo:** another model line in the range.","**Junior models:** smaller bikes for younger riders."],"p":["Names and versions change by model year. See the current Talaria range on our [Talaria brand page](/brands/talaria/)."]},{"h":"What to compare","ul":["Motor power and the riding modes available.","Battery voltage and capacity.","Weight, seat height and suspension.","Rider height and experience.","Parts and upgrade support."]},{"h":"Talaria or Sur-Ron?","p":["They are different brands with different models, so compare like for like: motor, battery, weight and price for a similar rider and use. Our [Sur-Ron models guide](/blog/sur-ron-models-explained/) covers the other side of that comparison."]},{"h":"Understanding the rider fit for a Talaria","p":["Talaria bikes come in several sizes. Seat height, weight and ergonomics differ between models, and a bike that suits a 175 cm rider may feel small for a 190 cm rider. Check the seat height and the maker’s recommended rider height, and sit on the bike if possible. Check that you can reach the ground with the balls of your feet, reach the controls comfortably and keep your elbows slightly bent.","Younger riders and smaller adults often prefer the junior and smaller models, which are lighter and easier to handle. Heavier riders should check the weight limits and the suspension settings."]},{"h":"Setting up suspension for your weight","p":["Suspension setup affects handling and comfort. Check the maker’s guidance for sag, preload and damping, and adjust in small steps. If the bike feels harsh, bottoms out or wallows, revisit the settings. Re-check after the first few rides, because components bed in. If you are unsure, a suspension specialist or the dealer can help."]},{"h":"Preparing a Talaria for a day out","ul":["Charge the battery fully the night before, and carry a spare if you want a long ride.","Check tyre pressure, chain tension and brake pads.","Pack water, a tool kit, a phone and first aid.","Tell someone your route and expected return.","Check weather, venue rules and any fire bans."]},{"h":"Aftermarket upgrades: what to consider","p":["Many riders upgrade tyres, brakes, grips, footpegs and bash plates. Upgrades that improve safety, such as brakes and tyres, are usually worth the money. Power upgrades demand more care, because they stress components and change how the bike behaves. Match each upgrade to your model, and keep the original parts in case you need them for warranty. See the [electric dirt bikes](/shop/electric-dirt-bikes/) category and the [Talaria brand page](/brands/talaria/)."]},{"h":"How to read reviews and forum posts about Talaria bikes","p":["Online discussions about Talaria and similar bikes are lively, which can be helpful and confusing. Pay attention to who is writing: an owner of the exact model and year is more useful than a general opinion. Be careful with speed and range claims, because conditions vary. Treat modification advice with caution, because changes can affect safety, warranty and legality.","Look for patterns. If many owners mention the same problem, such as a loose bolt or a worn part, it is worth checking on your bike. If advice contradicts the manual, follow the manual or ask the dealer."]},{"h":"Storing and transporting a Talaria","p":["Store the bike indoors, in a dry place, with the battery at a partial charge if it will not be used for a while. For transport, use a ute, trailer or van with proper tie-downs, and remove the battery if possible. Check the weight, and use ramps rather than lifting. Secure the bike at several points so it cannot shift."]},{"h":"Choosing accessories for a Talaria","p":["Useful accessories include a bash plate to protect the underside, hand guards, a good set of tyres for your terrain and a spare charger or battery for long days. A tool kit, tie-downs for transport and a stand for maintenance are practical. Check that accessories match your model and year, and fit them correctly.","Do not forget protective gear, which matters more than any accessory. A helmet, goggles, gloves, boots and armour should be in your kit before you ride."]},{"h":"A last word on Talaria bikes","p":["Talaria bikes are popular for good reasons, but the best one is the one that suits you. Compare models, check specifications and plan where you will ride. Look after the bike, wear proper gear and respect the rules. If you want help choosing between the Talaria models we list, we are happy to advise."]},{"h":"Choosing by rider and terrain","p":["A lighter Talaria suits smaller riders and tighter trails, while a larger model suits taller riders and harder terrain. Check seat height and rider size, and think about the surface you ride most. Ask the team if you want help comparing two models."]},{"h":"Parts, upgrades and servicing","p":["Before you buy any electric bike, check that spare parts such as batteries, chargers and brake pads can be bought in Australia. For Talaria bikes, match each part to the exact model and year, and have drivetrain, brakes and tyres checked regularly. See our [chain care](/blog/chain-alignment-high-torque-motors/) and [brakes](/blog/e-bike-and-dirt-bike-brakes/) guides."]},{"h":"How to compare two Talaria models fairly","p":["Put the two models side by side and compare the same measures: continuous and peak motor power, battery voltage and capacity, weight, seat height, suspension travel, brakes and what is included in the box. Then compare the price in Australian dollars, including freight and any extras you need such as a charger or protective gear.","Do not compare a model from one year with another from a different year without checking what changed. Ask the seller to confirm the exact model year and specification before you order."]},{"h":"Servicing and parts","p":["Keep a small kit of wear items: brake pads, a chain and sprockets, tyres and spare bolts. Check them regularly and replace them before they fail. Match every part to the exact model and year, and keep a record of what you have fitted so ordering later is easier. See our [brakes guide](/blog/e-bike-and-dirt-bike-brakes/) and [tyre guide](/blog/e-bike-tyres-and-tubes/)."]},{"h":"Riding, charging and battery care for a Talaria","p":["Electric dirt bikes put heavy demands on batteries, so look after yours. Use the supplied charger, let the battery cool after a hard ride before charging, and avoid leaving it fully charged for long periods. Keep the contacts clean, and store the battery somewhere cool and dry. If the bike will sit for several weeks, leave the battery partly charged and check it from time to time.","Learn how the bike behaves in each riding mode, and use the lowest mode when you are on unfamiliar ground. Replace worn tyres, brake pads and chains before they fail."]},{"h":"Gear, rules and where to ride","p":["Wear a motocross helmet, goggles, gloves, boots and body armour, and ride only on private property or approved off-road areas. Read our [e-bike laws guide](/blog/off-road-vs-street-legal-australia/) and [protective gear guide](/blog/helmets-and-protective-gear-for-e-bikes/)."]},{"h":"Quick answers","ul":["**Which Talaria is best for a beginner?** A lighter model in a low mode is usually easier to learn on.","**Can I use a Talaria on the road?** No. They are for off-road and private property use only.","**Where can I get parts?** Check the parts available locally before buying, and match each part to the model and year.","**How should I care for the battery?** Follow the manual, charge in a ventilated place and store it partly charged."]},{"h":"Looking for a model that is not listed?","p":["Some Talaria models people search for, such as the X3, MX3 and MX4, are not currently listed at Torque E-Bikes. Our [Talaria X3, MX3 and MX4 guide](/blog/talaria-x3-mx3-and-mx4/) explains how they fit in the range, and you can message the team to ask what can be sourced.","For everything else, browse [electric dirt bikes](/shop/electric-dirt-bikes/)."]},{"h":"Quick recap","p":["In this guide we covered the Talaria families; what to compare; talaria or Sur-Ron?; understanding the rider fit for a Talaria; setting up suspension for your weight; and preparing a Talaria for a day out. Come back to the section you need when you are deciding, and use the related categories and guides linked above to compare real products in Australian dollars. If you would like help applying any of this to your own bike, your budget and where you ride, message the team on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 or use the contact page, and we will reply with clear, honest advice."]}],"faq":[{"q":"What is the difference between the Talaria Sting and Komodo?","a":"The Sting is Talaria’s performance-oriented off-road line, while the Komodo is a different model with its own frame and specification. Compare the manufacturer’s published motor, battery and weight figures for the model year you are considering. See the talaria range for current models and prices."},{"q":"Are Talaria bikes road legal in Australia?","a":"No. Talaria electric dirt bikes are off-road machines and are not road legal or registrable in Australia. Ride them only on private property or in approved off-road areas, wearing full protective gear. Browse the talaria category to compare models side by side."},{"q":"Which Talaria models does Torque E-Bikes list?","a":"The Talaria brand page shows the current models, including Sting and Komodo variants and the XXX. Prices are in Australian dollars and shipping is arranged Australia-wide before payment. The team can confirm fitment or availability on WhatsApp. Prices are in Australian dollars and each product page lists its specifications."},{"q":"Is a Talaria better than a Sur-Ron?","a":"They are different brands with different models, so compare like for like: motor, battery, weight, suspension and price. Both are off-road bikes. Read the model pages side by side and decide by the terrain and riding style you have in mind."},{"q":"Where does Torque E-Bikes list Talaria bikes?","a":"Torque E-Bikes lists Talaria bikes in the talaria category, with the specification and the price in Australian dollars on each product page. The team can confirm fitment, stock or freight on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 before you order. Open the talaria category to see everything that is currently in stock."}],"keywords":{"primary":"talaria triple x","supporting":["talaria xx","talaria triple x guide","how to choose talaria triple x","talaria triple x explained","talaria triple x for beginners"]},"words":1335},"electric-mountain-bike-buying-guide":{"hook":"An electric mountain bike is a mountain bike with a pedal-assist motor and battery. Hardtails suit smoother trails and budgets, while full-suspension e-MTBs suit rough terrain.","intro":["An electric mountain bike (e-MTB) is a mountain bike with a pedal-assist motor and battery. The motor adds power as you pedal, which makes climbs easier and lets you ride further on trails.","This guide covers the choices that matter most: hardtail or full suspension, motor and battery, brakes and frame size."],"sections":[{"h":"Hardtail or full suspension","p":["A hardtail has suspension only at the front. It is simpler, lighter and cheaper to maintain, and suits smoother trails and tighter budgets. A full-suspension bike has a rear shock as well, giving more grip and comfort on rough ground at a higher price and with more maintenance."]},{"h":"Motor and battery","p":["Mid-drive motors sit at the cranks and use the bike’s gears, and they usually feel natural on climbs. Hub motors sit in a wheel and are common on lower-priced bikes. Battery capacity decides range: larger batteries add range and weight. Choose a capacity that covers your longest regular ride."]},{"h":"Brakes, wheels and frame size","ul":["Hydraulic disc brakes give strong, controllable stopping on descents.","Wheel size (27.5 or 29 inch) affects handling and the bike’s fit for your height.","Frame size should match your height and reach, so check the maker’s size chart."]},{"h":"Where you can ride","p":["Trail access rules vary by land manager and state, and some trails restrict e-bikes. Check with the land manager before you ride. For road and path rules, see our guide to [e-bike laws in Australia](/blog/off-road-vs-street-legal-australia/)."]},{"h":"Choosing wheel size and frame geometry","p":["E-MTBs use 27.5 inch, 29 inch or mixed wheels. Larger wheels roll over obstacles more easily and carry speed, while smaller wheels are more manoeuvrable and suit shorter riders. Mixed setups, with a larger front wheel and a smaller rear wheel, aim to combine the advantages. Frame geometry affects handling: a longer, slacker bike is more stable at speed, and a shorter, steeper bike is more agile.","Choose based on your height, the trails you ride and your style. If you can, test ride bikes with different geometries to feel the difference."]},{"h":"Understanding suspension travel and setup","p":["Suspension travel is how far the wheel moves. Cross-country bikes use around 100 to 120 mm, trail bikes around 130 to 150 mm and enduro bikes 160 mm or more. More travel absorbs larger hits but adds weight and complexity. Choose travel to suit your trails, not the biggest number you can afford.","Set up the suspension for your weight and style. Use the maker’s guide for sag, then adjust rebound and compression to taste. Check the settings after a few rides."]},{"h":"Tyres, brakes and drivetrain on an e-MTB","p":["E-MTBs are heavy, so tyres with strong casings and good grip matter. Brakes need to be powerful and consistent, so look for four-piston hydraulic calipers and large rotors. Drivetrains work harder, so they wear faster. Clean and lubricate the chain regularly and replace it early. Consider e-bike-specific drivetrain parts."]},{"h":"Charging, storage and transporting","p":["Charge the battery in a safe place, and avoid leaving it in a hot car. Store the bike indoors if you can, and remove the battery for long periods of storage. Use a rack rated for the weight, and remove the battery before lifting."]},{"h":"Common e-MTB questions answered","p":["Many riders ask how long e-MTB batteries last, how far you can ride and whether the motor makes you lazy. A typical battery gives several hours of trail riding at moderate assist, and range depends on terrain, assist and rider weight. The motor helps with climbs, so you ride more, but you still work. Riders often find they ride further and enjoy it more, and many report improved fitness.","Another common question is about maintenance. E-MTBs need regular care: clean and lubricate the drivetrain, check brakes, inspect suspension and keep the battery and connectors clean. Some riders service their own bikes, and others take them to a workshop. Plan for servicing, and budget for wear items."]},{"h":"A last word on e-mountain bikes","p":["An e-MTB opens up trails and hills for many riders. Choose a bike that fits you, with brakes and suspension that suit your riding, and a battery that gives you the range you want. Look after it, ride respectfully and check local rules. If you want advice on models and budgets, send us your questions, and we will help you choose."]},{"h":"Climbing, descending and the right assist level","p":["Most e-MTBs offer several assist levels. Use a lower level to save battery on flatter sections and a higher one on climbs. On descents the motor does nothing, so the bike’s weight and brakes matter more than on a normal bike. Practise braking early and smoothly, and keep your weight back on steep sections."]},{"h":"Maintenance for trail bikes","ul":["Clean the drivetrain after muddy or dusty rides.","Check brake pads and rotors often, because e-bikes wear them faster.","Keep tyre pressure suited to the trail and your weight.","Store and charge the battery as the maker recommends."]},{"h":"Transporting an e-MTB","p":["E-MTBs are heavy, so check the weight rating of any car rack before you buy, and remove the battery before lifting if you can. Secure the bike properly and never leave a lithium battery in a hot car."]},{"h":"Motors, sensors and battery integration","p":["E-MTB motors are usually mid-drive units that sit at the cranks. Torque sensors respond to how hard you pedal, which makes power delivery smooth and proportional on technical climbs, while cadence sensors respond to pedalling speed and can feel less natural. Batteries are either integrated into the frame, which looks clean and lowers weight, or mounted externally, which is often easier to swap.","Check how easy it is to remove the battery for charging, and whether the maker sells spare batteries and chargers. Look at the display too: you want to see assist level, speed and battery charge clearly in sunlight."]},{"h":"Sizing, geometry and suspension setup","p":["Pick the frame size that matches your height and reach, then set the suspension sag using the maker’s guide. Too little sag makes the bike harsh and too much makes it wallow. Adjust tyre pressure to suit your weight and the trail, and take time to learn how the bike handles with the extra weight of the motor and battery."]},{"h":"Budget, value and what to prioritise","p":["Spend first on the frame, brakes and motor, because those are hard to change later. Tyres, grips, saddles and pedals can be upgraded cheaply. A hardtail with good brakes and a reliable motor often beats a full-suspension bike with poor components at the same price. Check the warranty on the motor and battery, because these are the most expensive parts.","Look at weight too. E-MTBs are heavy, and a few kilograms can change how easy the bike is to handle and lift. If you will carry the bike up steps or onto a car, check the weight and the battery’s removability."]},{"h":"Trail etiquette and safety on an e-MTB","ul":["Give way to walkers and slower riders, and slow down on shared trails.","Do not use high assist levels on narrow, crowded trails.","Follow local trail signage and e-bike access rules.","Wear a full-face or trail helmet with gloves and knee protection.","Carry a repair kit, water and a phone."]},{"h":"Quick answers","ul":["**Do I need a special helmet?** A trail or enduro helmet gives more coverage than a road helmet, and many riders choose a full-face helmet for steep descents.","**Will an e-MTB ruin my fitness?** Many riders find they ride more often and for longer, and still get a workout.","**How long do batteries last?** They lose capacity gradually over years, depending on use and care.","**Can I ride an e-MTB on any trail?** No. Check local access rules, because some trails restrict e-bikes."]},{"h":"Compare models","p":["Browse [electric mountain bikes](/shop/electric-mountain-bikes/) and compare two or three in the same price band on motor, battery, suspension and brakes. For all types in one place, see the [e-bike buying guide](/blog/best-electric-bikes-in-australia/)."]},{"h":"Quick recap","p":["In this guide we covered hardtail or full suspension; motor and battery; brakes, wheels and frame size; where you can ride; choosing wheel size and frame geometry; and understanding suspension travel and setup. Come back to the section you need when you are deciding, and use the related categories and guides linked above to compare real products in Australian dollars. If you would like help applying any of this to your own bike, your budget and where you ride, message the team on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 or use the contact page, and we will reply with clear, honest advice."]}],"faq":[{"q":"What is the difference between an e-MTB and a normal mountain bike?","a":"An e-MTB adds a pedal-assist motor and battery to a mountain bike frame, so power is added as you pedal. It weighs more but makes climbs and long rides easier. Brakes and suspension are usually stronger to handle the extra weight and speed."},{"q":"Can I ride an e-MTB on any trail?","a":"Not always. Some trails restrict or ban e-bikes, and rules vary by land manager and state. Check the trail owner’s rules before you ride, and keep to the speed and etiquette rules posted for shared tracks. See the electric mountain bikes range for current models and prices."},{"q":"Where can I see e-mountain bikes at Torque E-Bikes?","a":"The electric mountain bikes category lists every e-MTB currently available with Australian-dollar prices. Filter by brand to compare models, and message the team on WhatsApp if you want help with frame sizing. Open the electric mountain bikes category to see everything that is currently in stock."},{"q":"Is a hardtail e-MTB good enough for most riders?","a":"A hardtail suits smoother trails and a tighter budget, and it is simpler to maintain. A full-suspension bike is more comfortable on rough, technical terrain. Choose by the trails you ride most often. Message the team on WhatsApp if you want advice on the electric mountain bikes range."},{"q":"Where does Torque E-Bikes list electric mountain bikes?","a":"Torque E-Bikes lists electric mountain bikes in the electric mountain bikes category, with the specification and the price in Australian dollars on each product page. The team can confirm fitment, stock or freight on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 before you order."}],"keywords":{"primary":"mountain e cycle","supporting":["electric mountain","mountain e cycle guide","how to choose mountain e cycle","mountain e cycle explained","mountain e cycle for beginners"]},"words":1323},"folding-electric-bikes":{"hook":"A folding e-bike is an electric bike with a hinged frame that folds for storage and transport. It suits small homes, car boots and mixed-mode commuting.","intro":["A folding electric bike has a hinged frame that folds down for storage and transport. It suits small flats, car boots and mixed commutes that include trains or buses.","The trade-off is that folding bikes usually have smaller wheels, so they can feel less stable and less comfortable than full-size bikes on rough surfaces."],"sections":[{"h":"Who folding e-bikes suit","ul":["People with limited storage at home or work.","Commuters who combine riding with trains, buses or a car.","Riders who want a bike they can carry up stairs, within the weight they can lift."]},{"h":"What to check","ul":["Folded size: does it fit your boot, cupboard or desk space?","Weight: e-bikes are heavy, so check you can lift it when folded.","Fold mechanism: look for a secure latch and a quick, simple fold.","Battery: removable batteries are easier to charge indoors.","Brakes and tyres: small wheels need good brakes and decent tyres."]},{"h":"Folding or full size?","p":["A folding e-bike trades some ride comfort and range for portability. If you ride mostly on flat paths and need to store or carry the bike, a folder works well. If you ride long distances or on rough roads, a full-size commuter may be better: see our [commuter e-bike guide](/blog/commuter-e-bikes/)."]},{"h":"Rules and safety","p":["Pedal-assist e-bikes that meet the 250W standard can use roads and paths, subject to state rules. Wear an approved helmet, and use lights at night: see our [e-bike lights guide](/blog/e-bike-lights/)."]},{"h":"How folding mechanisms differ","p":["Folding bikes use different hinge designs. Mid-frame folds split the frame in the middle and bring the wheels together, which creates a compact package. Vertical folds drop the bars and fold the frame so the bike can be wheeled on small wheels. Some bikes fold in half and then also fold the bars, saddle and pedals. The best design for you depends on where you will store and carry the bike.","Check the latch and hinge strength, because every fold stresses them. Look for designs with a safety lock that stops the bike folding accidentally, and check for play after a few weeks of use."]},{"h":"Choosing wheel size for a folding e-bike","p":["Smaller wheels, such as 16 inch, fold to a smaller package but feel twitchier and are less comfortable on rough roads. Larger wheels, such as 20 inch, ride better but fold bigger. Most e-bike folders use 16 or 20 inch wheels. Choose according to how much you value portability versus ride comfort, and check that tyres and tubes are readily available."]},{"h":"Carrying and storing a folded e-bike","ul":["Know the weight with the battery, and see whether you can remove the battery to lighten the lift.","Check where to lift the bike without damaging cables or the display.","Use a bike bag or cover for public transport.","Store it away from heat and damp, and charge the battery in a safe spot.","Use a wheel kit or roller if the maker supplies one."]},{"h":"Who benefits most from a folding e-bike","p":["People who ride a short leg to a station, who live in flats, who travel by car and bike or who want a spare bike in a caravan or boat benefit most. If you ride mostly on rough ground or carry heavy loads, a different bike will probably serve you better. Browse [folding e-bikes](/shop/folding-e-bikes/) or read the [commuter e-bike guide](/blog/commuter-e-bikes/)."]},{"h":"Comfort upgrades for small-wheeled bikes","p":["Small wheels transmit more vibration than large wheels, so comfort upgrades make a difference. A suspension seat post, ergonomic grips, a slightly wider tyre and a padded saddle all help. Check tyre pressure often, because a small drop makes the ride harsher. If your folder has a front suspension fork, learn how to adjust it. A carrier block or frame bag can help with luggage without affecting the fold."]},{"h":"Traveling with a folding e-bike","p":["Check the airline or train operator’s rules for bikes and batteries. Many operators allow folded bikes as luggage, but batteries have limits. Use a protective bag, and remove the battery if you must carry it separately. Plan how you will lift the bike on and off, and where you will store it on board."]},{"h":"Buying a folding e-bike online","p":["If you buy online, check the folded dimensions, the weight and the delivery arrangements. Ask how the bike arrives (fully assembled, partly assembled or in a box) and what tools you need. Check the return policy and warranty, and whether the seller offers assembly or setup advice. Read reviews of the specific model, and ask the seller about spare parts such as tyres and tubes.","When the bike arrives, check it for damage, tighten bolts and adjust the saddle and bars. Test the fold several times, and check that all latches lock securely before your first ride."]},{"h":"A last word on folding e-bikes","p":["A folding e-bike gives you flexibility. It fits into small spaces, travels with you and still helps you ride further. Choose one that folds easily, weighs what you can lift and has the battery and components you need. If you are not sure which suits you, send us your questions."]},{"h":"Riding comfort and handling","p":["Smaller wheels make a folding bike nimble but twitchier, and they feel bumps more. Check the tyre width, the frame stiffness and the handlebar height, and set the saddle and stem so your position is comfortable. A suspension seat post can add comfort on rough roads."]},{"h":"Taking a folding e-bike on public transport","p":["Rules for taking bikes on trains and buses differ between operators, and some restrict e-bikes or limit battery size. Check the operator’s rules before you travel, and remove the battery if you are allowed to carry it separately."]},{"h":"Care and storage","p":["Keep the hinge and latch clean and lightly lubricated, and check they are fully locked before every ride. Store the battery indoors at a moderate temperature, and fold the bike carefully so cables are not pinched."]},{"h":"Questions to ask before buying a folding e-bike","ul":["How big is it when folded, and will it fit my storage space?","How much does it weigh with the battery, and can I lift it?","Is the battery removable and can it be charged indoors?","What wheel size and tyre width does it use?","How long does the fold take, and does the latch feel secure?","Are replacement parts such as tyres and brake pads easy to get?"]},{"h":"Who should look at a full-size bike instead","p":["If your ride is longer than about 10 to 15 km each way, rough in places, or you carry loads often, a full-size commuter or cargo bike will usually be more comfortable and stable. Folding bikes shine when storage or transport is the main problem, not when you want a longer-range tourer. See the [commuter e-bike guide](/blog/commuter-e-bikes/) for the alternative."]},{"h":"Using a folding e-bike in daily life","p":["A folding e-bike can live under a desk, in a cupboard or in a car boot, and can ride with you on a train. To make it practical, practise folding and unfolding it until it takes a minute or less. Learn where to lift it and how to carry it, remove the battery for charging indoors, and keep a bike bag or cover for dirty wheels."]},{"h":"Servicing small wheels and hinges","ul":["Check the hinge and latch for play before every ride.","Check tyre pressure often, because small tyres lose pressure quickly.","Lubricate the hinge sparingly.","Watch for wear on the chain and cassette, because small wheels spin faster."]},{"h":"Quick answers","ul":["**Are folding e-bikes slower?** Not necessarily, but small wheels feel less stable at speed.","**Can I take one on a train?** Rules differ by operator. Check the operator’s rules for e-bikes and batteries.","**How heavy are they?** Often heavier than a folding bike without a motor, so check the weight before buying.","**Can I use one for long rides?** You can, but a larger bike is usually more comfortable on long trips."]},{"h":"Browse folding e-bikes","p":["See current models in the [folding e-bikes](/shop/folding-e-bikes/) category, and compare prices on the [budget e-bikes](/electric-bikes/budget/) page."]},{"h":"Quick recap","p":["In this guide we covered who folding e-bikes suit; what to check; folding or full size?; rules and safety; how folding mechanisms differ; and choosing wheel size for a folding e-bike. Come back to the section you need when you are deciding, and use the related categories and guides linked above to compare real products in Australian dollars. If you would like help applying any of this to your own bike, your budget and where you ride, message the team on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 or use the contact page, and we will reply with clear, honest advice."]}],"faq":[{"q":"Are folding e-bikes good for commuting?","a":"Yes for many riders. They fold small enough for trains, lifts and cupboards, and the motor helps with hills. They weigh more than a normal folding bike, so check the weight if you carry it up stairs. See the folding e-bikes range for current models and prices."},{"q":"How small does a folding e-bike fold?","a":"Folded size varies by model, so use the dimensions on each product page. Smaller wheels fold more compactly but ride differently from full-size wheels. Measure your storage space and your car boot before ordering. Browse the folding e-bikes category to compare models side by side."},{"q":"Which folding e-bikes does Torque E-Bikes list?","a":"The folding e-bikes category lists models including fat-tyre and step-through folders. Compare prices and weights on each page, and ask the team on WhatsApp if you need help choosing. The team can confirm fitment or availability on WhatsApp. Prices are in Australian dollars and each product page lists its specifications."},{"q":"Is a folding e-bike as good as a full-size one?","a":"It trades some ride comfort and range for portability. If you ride mostly on flat paths and need to store or carry the bike, a folder works well. For long rides or rough surfaces, a full-size e-bike is usually better."},{"q":"Where does Torque E-Bikes list folding e-bikes?","a":"Torque E-Bikes lists folding e-bikes in the folding e-bikes category, with the specification and the price in Australian dollars on each product page. The team can confirm fitment, stock or freight on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 before you order."}],"keywords":{"primary":"electric folding cycle","supporting":["bag for foldable bike","electric folding cycle guide","how to choose electric folding cycle","electric folding cycle explained","electric folding cycle for beginners"]},"words":1322},"electric-trikes-for-adults":{"hook":"An electric trike is a three-wheel e-bike that stays upright without balancing. Adult e-trikes suit riders who want stability, easier starts or load-carrying.","intro":["An electric trike has three wheels, so it stays upright when you stop and is easy to start from a standstill. That stability suits riders with balance concerns, people carrying loads and anyone who wants a relaxed ride.","This guide covers the main types, what to check and when a two-wheel cargo bike might suit better."],"sections":[{"h":"Who electric trikes suit","ul":["Riders who find balance difficult or prefer not to put a foot down.","People carrying shopping, tools or equipment.","Riders who want a stable bike at low speed."]},{"h":"Types of trike","p":["Most adult e-trikes have either two wheels at the back (a delta layout), which is common for cargo and shopping, or two at the front (a tadpole layout), which is more stable when cornering. Each has a different feel, so test a model if you can."]},{"h":"What to check","ul":["Load rating: check the maximum load for the rack, basket and the whole bike.","Motor and battery: choose a battery that covers your usual trips.","Turning circle and width: will it fit through your gates and doors?","Brakes: a heavier bike needs strong brakes.","Storage and charging: where will it live and charge?"]},{"h":"Trike or cargo bike?","p":["Choose a trike if stability and easy starts matter most. Choose a two-wheel cargo bike if you want a narrower bike that handles more like a normal bicycle. Our [cargo e-bike guide](/blog/electric-cargo-bikes/) covers that side of the choice."]},{"h":"Types of adult trike in more detail","p":["Delta trikes have one wheel at the front and two at the rear, and are the classic shopping and recreational design. They are easy to mount, have a rear basket or cargo area and handle slow speeds well. Tadpole trikes put two wheels at the front and one at the rear and sit lower. They corner more stably and suit sportier riding, but they are harder to get on and off.","Folding and compact trikes exist too, and they suit riders with limited storage. Check the folded size and the weight, because they are heavier than they look."]},{"h":"Who benefits most from a trike","ul":["Riders who have balance concerns or limited mobility.","People who want to carry shopping or tools without worrying about tipping.","Those who ride slowly on paths and want a relaxed seating position.","Riders who want to stop and start without putting a foot down."],"p":["A trike is not the best choice for everyone. It is wider than a bike, does not lean into corners and needs more room to turn. Test a trike if you can, and consider whether a two-wheel cargo bike would suit you better."]},{"h":"Riding skills for a trike","p":["Riding a trike is different from riding a bike. You steer rather than lean, so corners need to be taken slowly, particularly with a load. Watch the camber of the road, because a tilted surface can make a trike drift. Practise turning, braking and reversing in an open area, and learn the trike’s width so you can judge gaps.","Because a trike can tip if you turn sharply at speed with a heavy load, slow down before corners and keep the weight low and centred."]},{"h":"Maintenance for a trike","ul":["Check all three tyres regularly, because a flat is more awkward to fix.","Check brakes on every wheel and adjust them evenly.","Lubricate the chain and check the rear axle.","Check the steering linkage and bolts.","Keep the battery charged and stored correctly."]},{"h":"Trike ownership in practice: a week in the life","p":["Picture a typical week with an electric trike. On Monday you ride to the shops, loading groceries into the rear basket. On Wednesday you ride to a friend’s place along a bike path, enjoying the stability when you stop and chat. On Saturday you ride to the market, carrying a larger load. The trike lives in the garage, with the battery charged indoors, and it gets a quick check of the tyres and brakes each weekend.","It is a relaxed, practical way to get around, and many riders find they use the trike more than they expected. The key is to choose one that fits your body, your storage and your trips."]},{"h":"Accessories that make a trike more useful","ul":["A rain cover for the cargo area.","A rear mirror.","A flag or bright lights for visibility.","A strong lock and a ground anchor.","A pump and a spare tube for each wheel size."]},{"h":"Questions to ask before you buy a trike","ul":["What is the load rating and the rider weight limit?","How wide is the trike, and will it fit through my gates and doors?","What is the turning circle?","Does it have a parking brake and good lights?","What is the warranty and who services it?"]},{"h":"Using a trike on paths and in traffic","p":["Trikes are wider than bikes, so check the width of paths, gates and bike lanes on your route. Ride predictably, leave room when passing people and use a bell or a friendly call. In traffic, a trike is lower and less visible than a car, so use bright lights, a flag and reflective clothing. Be aware of slopes and cambers, which can tilt the trike, and slow down before turns.","Check your state’s rules for the use of trikes on paths and roads, because some places treat them differently from bicycles. Learn your route and plan stops where you can park safely."]},{"h":"A last word on trikes","p":["An electric trike can bring independence and joy to riders who want stability. Choose one that fits you, check the load rating and think about where you will store and charge it. Ride within your comfort, and take care on slopes and corners. If you are unsure which trike suits you, tell us about your needs and we will help."]},{"h":"Riding a trike safely","p":["A trike does not lean into corners like a bike, so slow down before turns and avoid sharp turns at speed, especially when loaded or on a camber. Learn the bike’s width so you can judge gaps, and brake smoothly. Practise in an empty car park before you ride in traffic."]},{"h":"Storage and charging","p":["A trike is wider and longer than a bike, so check that it fits your storage space and can be wheeled through doors and gates. Charge the battery in a ventilated area, and take a removable battery inside if the bike lives outdoors."]},{"h":"Who should test first","p":["If you have balance or mobility concerns, try a trike before you buy where you can. Check how easy it is to get on and off, how the brakes feel and whether you can reach the controls comfortably."]},{"h":"Features worth paying for on a trike","ul":["A low step-through for easy mounting and dismounting.","A parking brake so the trike cannot roll away.","A reverse gear or walk-assist on heavier models.","A rear basket or cargo box with a clear load rating.","Good lighting and reflectors, especially if you ride at dusk."]},{"h":"What to check on arrival","p":["Check that all bolts are tight, the brakes work evenly on all wheels, the tyres are inflated and the battery charges properly. Take a short test ride in a quiet area to learn the turning circle and how the steering feels. Keep the manual and receipt for warranty claims."]},{"h":"Choosing the right motor and battery for a trike","p":["Trikes are heavier than bikes and often carry loads, so look for a motor with good low-speed torque and a battery large enough for your usual trips. If your route is hilly or you carry heavy loads, choose a larger battery and check how the motor handles starting on a slope. Check the maker’s range figure and then reduce it for hills and loads."]},{"h":"Where electric trikes are used","ul":["Neighbourhood shopping trips.","Recreational rides on bike paths.","Delivering small loads.","Mobility for riders who prefer stability."]},{"h":"Browse the range","p":["See current models in the [cargo bikes and e-trikes](/shop/cargo-bikes-and-e-trikes/) category, and check [e-bike laws in Australia](/blog/off-road-vs-street-legal-australia/) for where you can ride."]},{"h":"Quick recap","p":["In this guide we covered who electric trikes suit; types of trike; what to check; trike or cargo bike?; types of adult trike in more detail; and who benefits most from a trike. Come back to the section you need when you are deciding, and use the related categories and guides linked above to compare real products in Australian dollars. If you would like help applying any of this to your own bike, your budget and where you ride, message the team on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 or use the contact page, and we will reply with clear, honest advice."]}],"faq":[{"q":"Are electric trikes easy to ride?","a":"Many riders find e-trikes easier than two-wheelers because they stay upright at a standstill and need no balance. They corner differently, so take wide turns and slow down on slopes until you are used to the handling. See the cargo bikes & e-trikes range for current models and prices."},{"q":"Can an electric trike carry a passenger or shopping?","a":"Many e-trikes have a rear basket or cargo bed, but load limits vary. Check the stated maximum load on the product page, and keep weight low and evenly spread for stable handling. Browse the cargo bikes & e-trikes category to compare models side by side."},{"q":"Which electric trikes does Torque E-Bikes sell?","a":"The cargo bikes and e-trikes category lists folding, step-through and cargo trikes from several brands. Prices and features are on each product page, and the team can help match a trike to your needs. The team can confirm fitment or availability on WhatsApp."},{"q":"Should I choose an e-trike or a cargo bike?","a":"Choose a trike if stability and easy starts matter most. Choose a two-wheel cargo bike if you want a more bicycle-like ride and can balance a loaded bike. Think about where you will park it and how narrow your paths are."},{"q":"Where does Torque E-Bikes list electric trikes?","a":"Torque E-Bikes lists electric trikes in the cargo bikes & e-trikes category, with the specification and the price in Australian dollars on each product page. The team can confirm fitment, stock or freight on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 before you order."}],"keywords":{"primary":"3 wheel electric bike","supporting":["3 wheel cycle","adult trike","adult 3 wheel bike","kids 3 wheel bike","mobility trike"]},"words":1318},"electric-cargo-bikes":{"hook":"An electric cargo bike is an e-bike built to carry loads, children or shopping. Longtail and box designs trade length and handling for capacity.","intro":["An electric cargo bike carries shopping, tools, equipment or children with the help of a motor. For short local trips it can replace many car journeys, but it needs more space and a stronger frame than a normal bike.","This guide covers the main types and what to check before you choose."],"sections":[{"h":"Longtail and front-loader types","p":["A longtail has an extended rear rack and handles much like a normal bike, so it is a good first cargo bike. A front-loader carries a box or tray ahead of the rider and suits bulky loads, but it is longer and takes more learning."]},{"h":"Load ratings and safety","ul":["Check the maximum load for the whole bike and for the rack or box.","Fit appropriate child seats and restraints if you carry children.","Check the brakes: loaded bikes need strong, reliable braking.","Check that the stand holds the loaded bike upright."]},{"h":"Motor and battery","p":["Loads and hills use more battery, so choose a larger battery than you would for an unloaded bike. A motor that gives good low-speed torque helps with starting and climbing. Real range is lower with a heavy load."]},{"h":"Is a cargo e-bike a car replacement?","p":["For short local trips with shopping or children, it can be. It will not replace a car for long distances or large loads. Think about your typical week before deciding, and consider a [trike](/blog/electric-trikes-for-adults/) if stability matters more than a narrow bike."]},{"h":"Carrying capacity explained","p":["A cargo bike has several load limits that matter. The total weight limit covers the rider, the load and the bike. The rack or box has its own limit. The child-seat positions each have a weight limit. Add up the rider, passengers and cargo and make sure the total is within the bike’s rating, with margin. Heavier loads need longer braking distances and more battery, so allow for them.","Weight distribution matters too. Keep heavy items low and close to the centre, and avoid overloading the front or rear. If the bike feels unstable, stop and rearrange the load."]},{"h":"Longtail or front-loader: how to decide","ul":["Choose a longtail if you want a bike that handles like a normal one, with space for children or luggage behind you.","Choose a front-loader if you want to carry a box of shopping or children where you can see them.","Choose a trike if stability at a standstill matters most.","Think about storage: a longtail is long but narrow, and a front-loader is wide."]},{"h":"Using a cargo bike with children","p":["Only carry children in seats designed for the bike, and use the harnesses and footrests provided. Children should wear helmets that fit, and the minimum age and weight limits of the seat must be respected. Practise riding with the empty seat, then with a weight, before you carry a child. Keep speeds low and brake early, because a loaded bike takes longer to stop."]},{"h":"Costs of ownership","p":["Allow for child seats, a stand, a strong lock, weather covers, lights and servicing. Brakes and tyres wear faster on a heavy bike, so check them regularly. A good cargo bike is a significant purchase, but it can replace many car trips and pay for itself over time if used often."]},{"h":"Extra questions to ask before buying a cargo bike","ul":["What is the maximum total weight, and how much of it is payload?","Does the frame have mounts for child seats, a rack and a stand?","What brakes are fitted, and what rotor sizes?","Can the bike be locked easily, and what is its length for storage?","Are parts such as tyres and chains standard sizes?"]},{"h":"Accessories for family use","p":["Add a rain cover for passengers, footrests and hand grips for children, a bell and good lights. Choose child seats rated for the bike and fitted by someone who knows how, and check them regularly for wear and loose bolts."]},{"h":"Weights and balance when loading a cargo bike","p":["Load the heaviest items closest to the bike’s centre and as low as you can. Distribute weight evenly left and right, and avoid hanging bags from the handlebars. If you carry children, seat the heavier child closer to the centre. Check that the load does not touch the wheels or block the lights, and secure it with straps. Ride a short loop to check balance before you set off on a longer trip, and adjust if the bike pulls to one side or feels unstable."]},{"h":"Testing a cargo bike before you commit","p":["If you can, ride a cargo bike with a load that resembles your needs. Carry a bag of weights or a child’s seat with a dummy load and ride a loop. Check how the bike steers when loaded, how it brakes and how it feels at low speed. Check that you can park it, lock it and get it through your gate. Think about how you will mount and dismount with a load or a child.","If you cannot test ride, ask the seller for dimensions, weights and user stories. Look at photos and videos, and ask questions about how it handles with loads."]},{"h":"Charging and storing a cargo bike at home","p":["A cargo bike is large, so plan a space for it. A garage, a side passage or a covered area works, with a lock or ground anchor for security. Charge the battery in a ventilated place, away from flammable items, and do not leave it charging unattended. If the bike lives outside, use a cover and a strong lock, and take the battery inside. Check the bike regularly, because it will be used often and carry heavy loads."]},{"h":"Carrying children safely","p":["Only carry children in seats or boxes designed for the purpose, and use the restraints and helmets the maker specifies. Check the minimum age and weight for any child seat, and never exceed the bike’s load rating. Practise riding the loaded bike before you ride in traffic."]},{"h":"Parking, locking and storage","p":["Cargo bikes are long and heavy, so check where you will park and store the bike, and use a strong lock: see the [lock guide](/blog/how-to-lock-an-e-bike/). Many riders fit a robust stand so the bike stays upright while loading."]},{"h":"Costs to plan for","p":["Allow for a good lock, child seats or a box, panniers, lights and servicing. Brake pads and tyres wear faster on a heavy bike, so budget for replacements."]},{"h":"Choosing a longtail or a front-loader","p":["A longtail keeps the load behind the rider and handles much like a normal bike, which makes it a good first cargo bike. A front-loader puts the load ahead of the rider so you can see passengers and cargo, but it is longer and has a steering linkage that takes a little getting used to. Think about where you will park and ride: narrow paths and tight garages suit a longtail better."]},{"h":"Brakes, tyres and maintenance on a heavy bike","ul":["Choose hydraulic disc brakes with rotors large enough for the load.","Check brake pads often, because they wear faster under load.","Use tyres with a good load rating and keep pressure correct.","Check spokes and wheel true regularly.","Keep the chain clean and lubricated."]},{"h":"Planning a week with a cargo e-bike","p":["Think about the trips you make each week: school runs, shopping, errands and visits. Add up the distances and the loads, and check that a cargo bike can handle them. Think about parking and security at each destination, and how you will charge the battery. If most trips are short and local, a cargo bike can replace many car journeys."]},{"h":"Accessories that make a cargo bike more useful","ul":["Rain covers for passengers and loads.","Child seats and harnesses made for the bike.","Strong stands and locks.","Lights and reflectors for visibility.","Panniers and boxes suited to the frame."]},{"h":"Browse and compare","p":["See current models in the [cargo bikes and e-trikes](/shop/cargo-bikes-and-e-trikes/) category and read our [bike racks and trailers guide](/blog/bike-racks-baskets-and-trailers/) for add-ons."]},{"h":"Quick recap","p":["In this guide we covered longtail and front-loader types; load ratings and safety; motor and battery; is a cargo e-bike a car replacement?; carrying capacity explained; and longtail or front-loader: how to decide. Come back to the section you need when you are deciding, and use the related categories and guides linked above to compare real products in Australian dollars. If you would like help applying any of this to your own bike, your budget and where you ride, message the team on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 or use the contact page, and we will reply with clear, honest advice."]}],"faq":[{"q":"How much can an electric cargo bike carry?","a":"Capacity depends on the model, so use the maximum load stated on each product page. Distribute weight low and evenly, and check that brakes and tyres suit the load you plan to carry. See the cargo bikes & e-trikes range for current models and prices."},{"q":"Can I carry children on an electric cargo bike?","a":"Many cargo bikes support child seats or benches, but seat type and age limits vary by model and state. Use seats designed for your bike, fit helmets and follow the manufacturer’s limits. Browse the cargo bikes & e-trikes category to compare models side by side."},{"q":"Where do I find cargo e-bikes at Torque E-Bikes?","a":"Open the cargo bikes and e-trikes category to see every cargo model with Australian-dollar prices. Brand pages list each maker’s range, and the team can help compare models. The team can confirm fitment or availability on WhatsApp. Prices are in Australian dollars and each product page lists its specifications."},{"q":"Is a cargo e-bike a good car replacement?","a":"For short local trips with shopping or children, it can be. It will not replace a car for long distances or heavy goods. Count the trips you make each week and the distances before deciding. Browse the cargo bikes & e-trikes category to compare models side by side."},{"q":"Where does Torque E-Bikes list cargo e-bikes?","a":"Torque E-Bikes lists cargo e-bikes in the cargo bikes & e-trikes category, with the specification and the price in Australian dollars on each product page. The team can confirm fitment, stock or freight on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 before you order."}],"keywords":{"primary":"e bike with cargo trailer","supporting":["e bike with cargo trailer guide","how to choose e bike with cargo trailer","e bike with cargo trailer explained","e bike with cargo trailer for beginners","e bike with cargo trailer tips"]},"words":1301},"what-is-an-e-moto":{"hook":"An e-moto is an electric motorbike-style machine with a throttle and no pedal-assist limit. In Australia, e-motos are for off-road and private property use only.","intro":["An e-moto is an electric motorbike: a battery-powered two-wheeler that looks and rides more like a motorbike than a bicycle. Many have pedals removed or none at all, and they use a throttle rather than pedal assist.","The term covers everything from small kids’ bikes to powerful off-road machines. Understanding how an e-moto differs from an e-bike matters because the rules are different."],"sections":[{"h":"E-moto, e-bike and electric dirt bike","ul":["**E-bike:** a bicycle with a pedal-assist motor. A 250W pedal-assist e-bike can use public roads, subject to state rules.","**E-moto:** an electric motorbike with a throttle and a more powerful motor.","**Electric dirt bike:** an e-moto built for off-road riding."]},{"h":"What the rules say","p":["In Australia the e-motos sold for off-road riding are for off-road and private property use only. They are not road legal and cannot simply be registered for the road. Read our guide to [e-bike laws in Australia](/blog/off-road-vs-street-legal-australia/) before you buy."]},{"h":"Why riders choose e-motos","ul":["Quiet, with no fuel, clutch or gear changes.","Less routine maintenance than a petrol engine.","Instant power delivery, which needs respect."]},{"h":"E-moto or e-bike?","p":["Choose an e-bike if you want to ride on roads and paths with pedal assist. Choose an e-moto if you want to ride off-road on private land. Our [e-bike buying guide](/blog/best-electric-bikes-in-australia/) and [electric dirt bike guide](/blog/best-electric-dirt-bikes-in-australia/) cover each side."]},{"h":"Inside an e-moto: the main parts","p":["An e-moto has a battery pack, a motor, a controller and a drivetrain. The battery stores energy and is usually mounted low in the frame. The motor turns that energy into rotation, and it may be a hub motor in the rear wheel or a mid-mounted motor driving a chain. The controller regulates power, applies riding modes and protects the system. The drivetrain, if there is one, transfers power to the wheel. A suspension system, brakes, wheels and tyres complete the bike.","Because an e-moto has no gearbox or clutch, riding is simple, but the instant torque needs care. The right setup, protective gear and a suitable riding area keep the experience safe and fun."]},{"h":"Common e-moto terms explained","ul":["**Continuous power:** the power a motor can deliver without overheating.","**Peak power:** the maximum for short bursts.","**Voltage and capacity:** battery voltage and energy storage, usually in watt-hours.","**Regenerative braking:** a system that recovers some energy when slowing.","**Riding modes:** preset power and response levels."]},{"h":"How e-motos differ from electric scooters and e-bikes","p":["An e-moto has a motorcycle-style frame, suspension and brakes, and is made for off-road use. An electric scooter is a small vehicle with a deck and small wheels, designed for short urban trips. An e-bike is a bicycle with pedals and a motor assisting your pedalling. Each has different rules and uses, so check which one you want before you buy."]},{"h":"Where to start if you are new","p":["Start with a lower-powered model, a low riding mode and an open private area. Wear full gear, ride with someone experienced and build skills gradually. Read the [electric dirt bike guide](/blog/best-electric-dirt-bikes-in-australia/) and the [kids’ guide](/blog/kids-electric-dirt-bikes/) if you are buying for a young rider."]},{"h":"Charging, range and ride planning with an e-moto","p":["Charging an e-moto is like charging a large e-bike battery. Use the supplied charger, charge in a ventilated spot and keep it away from flammable materials. Charging time depends on battery capacity and charger power, so check the maker’s figures. Range depends on how hard you ride: fast acceleration and steep climbs use more energy than gentle trail riding.","Plan your ride around the battery. Start with a full charge, keep a reserve and think about how far you are from your vehicle or base. If you plan a longer day, consider a second battery or a charging break."]},{"h":"Understanding e-moto prices","p":["E-motos range widely in price, from kids’ models to premium adult bikes. Price reflects battery size, motor power, suspension, brakes and brand. When comparing, look at what you get for the money: battery capacity, quality of components, warranty and parts availability. Remember to add the cost of protective gear, freight for a lithium battery and a safe place to ride."]},{"h":"The future of electric motorbikes","p":["Electric motorbikes are developing quickly. Batteries are getting lighter and more energy dense, motors more efficient and controllers smarter. Riders can expect more range, faster charging and better suspension and brakes. Regulators are also watching, and rules may change. For now, off-road e-motos offer a simple, quiet way to enjoy private land, and a growing community shares tips and events.","If you plan to buy an e-moto, check the latest information, buy from a seller who will support you and choose a bike that suits your skills. As technology improves, there will be more options, but the basics of safety, gear and respect for others will stay the same."]},{"h":"A last word on e-motos","p":["E-motos are exciting, quiet and powerful. They are also serious machines that need respect. Choose one that suits the rider, buy proper gear and ride where it is allowed. Keep the battery in good condition, check the bike before every ride and learn skills gradually. If you want help choosing an e-moto, we are happy to talk."]},{"h":"How an e-moto is built","p":["An e-moto uses a larger battery and a more powerful motor than an e-bike, often with a chain or belt drive to the rear wheel, motorbike-style suspension and a controller that offers several riding modes. Many have hydraulic disc brakes and wide, knobbly tyres for off-road riding."]},{"h":"Choosing your first e-moto","ul":["Pick a lighter bike with a low-power mode.","Check the seat height and rider weight limit.","Buy full protective gear with the bike.","Plan where you will ride before you buy."]},{"h":"Charging and care","p":["Use the supplied charger, charge in a ventilated spot and do not leave it charging unattended. Look after the chain, brakes and tyres as you would on a petrol bike. Read the [battery care guide](/blog/electric-moto-maintenance-lithium-safety/)."]},{"h":"What an e-moto is and is not","p":["An e-moto is not a bicycle with a bigger motor. It is a different class of vehicle, and the way it is built, sold and regulated reflects that. Most are sold for off-road and private-property use. Even if a seller describes a model as “street legal” elsewhere in the world, it does not mean it meets Australian rules, so check before you assume."]},{"h":"Learning to ride an e-moto","ul":["Start in a low-power mode on flat, open ground.","Practise smooth throttle and brake control before riding hills.","Learn how regenerative braking, if fitted, feels.","Always wear a motocross helmet, goggles, gloves, boots and armour.","Consider a rider training course at a licensed venue."]},{"h":"Choosing between e-moto brands and sizes","p":["E-motos range from kids’ models to powerful adult bikes. Compare weight, seat height, motor power and battery capacity, and choose a bike that suits the rider’s size and experience. A lighter bike is easier to handle and pick up, and a heavier one can feel more stable at speed. Think about where you will ride and how you will transport the bike."]},{"h":"Costs and ownership","ul":["Purchase price and freight for a bike with a lithium battery.","Protective gear for each rider.","Replacement chains, sprockets, tyres and brake pads.","A safe place to charge and store the bike.","Insurance, if your policy allows it."]},{"h":"Quick answers","ul":["**Is an e-moto the same as an electric scooter?** No. A scooter is a different, usually smaller vehicle, and e-motos here mean motorbike-style off-road bikes.","**Can I register an e-moto?** Off-road e-motos sold here are for private property use only and are not built to be registered for the road.","**Is an e-moto good for kids?** Kids’ models exist, with power limits, but supervision and protective gear are essential.","**How long does the battery last?** It depends on battery size, mode and terrain."]},{"h":"Safety first","p":["Wear a certified helmet and protective gear, learn on a lower-powered bike and ride only where it is permitted. See the [protective gear guide](/blog/helmets-and-protective-gear-for-e-bikes/)."]},{"h":"Quick recap","p":["In this guide we covered e-moto, e-bike and electric dirt bike; what the rules say; why riders choose e-motos; e-moto or e-bike?; inside an e-moto: the main parts; and common e-moto terms explained. Come back to the section you need when you are deciding, and use the related categories and guides linked above to compare real products in Australian dollars. If you would like help applying any of this to your own bike, your budget and where you ride, message the team on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 or use the contact page, and we will reply with clear, honest advice."]}],"faq":[{"q":"What does e-moto mean?","a":"E-moto is short for electric motorbike. It describes a motorbike-style machine powered by a battery and electric motor, usually without pedals. In Australia, high-powered e-motos are for off-road and private property use only. See the electric dirt bikes range for current models and prices."},{"q":"Is an e-moto the same as an electric dirt bike?","a":"Often, yes. Electric dirt bikes are a type of e-moto designed for off-road riding. Some e-motos are built for tracks, trails or kids, so compare the model and intended use before buying. Browse the electric dirt bikes category to compare models side by side."},{"q":"Does Torque E-Bikes sell e-motos?","a":"Yes. The electric dirt bikes and kids electric dirt bikes categories list e-motos from several brands. Each page states whether the bike is for off-road and private property use only. The team can confirm fitment or availability on WhatsApp. Prices are in Australian dollars and each product page lists its specifications."},{"q":"Should I buy an e-moto or an e-bike?","a":"Choose an e-bike if you want to ride on roads and paths with pedal assist. Choose an e-moto if you want motorbike-style riding on private land or off-road tracks. Consider where you can legally ride before you decide. Browse the electric dirt bikes category to compare models side by side."},{"q":"Where does Torque E-Bikes list e-motos and electric motorbikes?","a":"Torque E-Bikes lists e-motos and electric motorbikes in the electric dirt bikes category, with the specification and the price in Australian dollars on each product page. The team can confirm fitment, stock or freight on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 before you order."}],"keywords":{"primary":"emoto","supporting":["emoto guide","how to choose emoto","emoto explained","emoto for beginners","emoto tips"]},"words":1320},"helmets-and-protective-gear-for-e-bikes":{"hook":"The right helmet depends on how you ride. E-bike commuters need a certified bike helmet, while electric dirt bike riders need a full-face motocross helmet plus goggles, gloves and body armour.","intro":["A certified helmet is the single most important piece of riding gear, and gloves, goggles and armour add protection that matters even more on an electric dirt bike. E-bikes are heavier and faster than normal bikes, so crashes can be more serious.","This guide covers what to wear for each type of riding and how to check that gear fits."],"sections":[{"h":"Helmets: look for a standard and a good fit","p":["Choose a helmet that meets the standard for the type of riding. In Australia, bicycle helmets are expected to meet AS/NZS 2063, and motocross-style helmets for off-road bikes meet their own standards. Check the label, and check that it is the right type for your riding.","A helmet should sit level, snug but not painful, with the straps adjusted so it does not move when you shake your head. Replace a helmet after a significant impact."]},{"h":"What to wear for each type of riding","ul":["**City and commuting:** an approved bicycle helmet, gloves, closed shoes and lights or reflective items.","**Mountain biking:** a helmet with good coverage, gloves, eye protection and knee or elbow pads if the trail is rough.","**Electric dirt bikes:** a motocross helmet, goggles, gloves, boots and body armour or a chest protector."]},{"h":"Gloves, goggles and boots","p":["Gloves protect your hands in a fall and reduce vibration. Goggles keep dust and debris out of your eyes. Boots protect your ankles and feet on an off-road bike. Choose gear that fits well so it does not slip or restrict movement."]},{"h":"Is a smart helmet worth it?","p":["A smart helmet adds features such as lights or audio, which can help visibility and navigation. It still has to meet the same safety standard and fit properly, so choose on safety first and features second."]},{"h":"Understanding helmet standards","p":["Helmets are tested to standards, and the label tells you which one applies. In Australia, bicycle helmets are required to meet the Australian and New Zealand standard for bicycle helmets, and a sticker inside the helmet shows compliance. Motorcycle and motocross helmets are tested to different standards that include tougher impact and retention tests. If you ride an electric dirt bike off-road, choose a helmet designed for that use, not a bicycle helmet.","Standards change over time. If you are unsure, ask the seller which standard the helmet meets and whether it is suitable for your riding. A helmet that meets the right standard and fits properly gives much better protection than an unlabelled helmet."]},{"h":"Goggles, eye protection and sun","p":["Goggles protect your eyes from dust, mud, branches and wind. Choose goggles that fit snugly over your helmet and nose, with a lens suited to the light: clear for low light, tinted for sun. Sunglasses are suitable for road riding if they are impact rated. Keep spare lenses or tear-offs for dusty or muddy rides."]},{"h":"Clothing and footwear","p":["Long sleeves and pants protect your skin from scrapes, and many riders wear a padded jersey or armour under it. Boots with ankle support protect your feet and lower legs on an off-road bike, and sturdy shoes are important on a commuter bike. Avoid loose clothing that can catch on moving parts. Wear gloves to protect your hands and improve grip."]},{"h":"Choosing gear for kids and teenagers","ul":["Buy gear in the right size, not one to grow into.","Check the helmet fits snugly and does not move.","Replace helmets if they are damaged or outgrown.","Choose gear that the child likes, so they wear it.","Lead by example and wear your own gear."]},{"h":"Looking after your gear","p":["Clean helmets with mild soap and water, and keep them out of the sun. Dry gloves and boots after use, and wash jerseys and pants. Check armour for cracks and straps for wear. Store gear in a cool, dry place. Replace items that are worn, damaged or no longer fit."]},{"h":"Budgeting for safety gear","p":["Good gear is an investment, but you do not need to spend everything at once. Buy a certified helmet first, then gloves and eye protection, then boots and armour. For off-road riding, put armour and boots high on your list. Look for sales, but do not compromise on fit or standards. Cheap gear that does not fit or meet the standard is a poor saving.","Remember to budget for replacing gear. Helmets and armour have limited lives, and children outgrow gear quickly. Buying carefully means you ride safely without overspending."]},{"h":"A last word on protective gear","p":["Good gear does not make you invincible, but it does make crashes less costly. Choose gear that fits, meets the standard and suits your riding. Wear it every time, replace it when needed and encourage others to do the same. If you need advice on sizes or types, ask us, and we will help you choose."]},{"h":"Fit and care","ul":["Try helmets on and walk around the shop or at home before you commit.","Adjust the retention system so the helmet does not rock.","Keep the helmet away from heat and chemicals.","Replace it after a significant impact, even if it looks fine."]},{"h":"Gear for kids","p":["Children need gear that fits them now, not gear to grow into. A loose helmet or oversized armour can do more harm than good. See the [kids’ electric dirt bike guide](/blog/kids-electric-dirt-bikes/) for the gear to buy with a first bike."]},{"h":"Visibility on the road","p":["If you ride a road-legal e-bike at dusk or night, add lights and reflective gear so other road users can see you. See the [e-bike lights guide](/blog/e-bike-lights/)."]},{"h":"How to choose between helmet types","p":["A bicycle helmet is light and ventilated, and it is the right choice for road-legal e-bikes and for commuting. A mountain bike helmet adds coverage at the back and sides. A motocross-style helmet with a peak and chin bar offers more protection for off-road riding and is the usual choice on electric dirt bikes. Pick the helmet designed for your type of riding, and do not use a helmet made for a different sport."]},{"h":"Body armour, boots and gloves","ul":["Chest and back protectors reduce injury from falls and flying debris.","Knee and elbow guards help on rough ground.","Boots with ankle support protect your feet and shins.","Gloves with reinforced palms protect your hands and improve grip."]},{"h":"Helmet fit in detail","p":["Measure your head about a finger-width above the eyebrows, and use the maker’s size chart. The helmet should sit level on your head with the front edge about two fingers above your eyebrows. The straps should form a “V” under each ear, and the buckle should sit under the chin with room for one finger between the strap and your chin. When you shake your head, the helmet should move with it rather than shifting.","If a helmet pinches, gives pressure points or moves, try another size or shape. Different brands fit differently, so do not assume a size from one brand will match another."]},{"h":"When to replace gear","ul":["Replace a helmet after a significant impact, even if there is no visible damage.","Replace helmets that are cracked, deformed or very old.","Replace gloves with worn palms or loose stitching.","Replace goggles with scratched lenses.","Replace armour that is cracked or no longer fits."]},{"h":"Quick answers","ul":["**Do I need a different helmet for e-bikes?** A standard bicycle helmet is the legal minimum for road-legal e-bikes, but faster or off-road riding may call for more coverage.","**How often should I replace a helmet?** After an impact, and when it is old or damaged. Check the maker’s guidance.","**Is armour worth it?** For off-road riding, yes. It reduces injury in falls.","**Can I use a motorcycle helmet on a bike?** Check the standard and the rules for where you ride."]},{"h":"Browse gear","p":["See the [helmets, gloves and protection](/shop/helmets-gloves-and-protection/) category, and read the [electric dirt bike guide](/blog/best-electric-dirt-bikes-in-australia/) and [kids’ electric dirt bike guide](/blog/kids-electric-dirt-bikes/) for riders buying a first bike."]},{"h":"Quick recap","p":["In this guide we covered helmets: look for a standard and a good fit; what to wear for each type of riding; gloves, goggles and boots; is a smart helmet worth it?; understanding helmet standards; and goggles, eye protection and sun. Come back to the section you need when you are deciding, and use the related categories and guides linked above to compare real products in Australian dollars. If you would like help applying any of this to your own bike, your budget and where you ride, message the team on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 or use the contact page, and we will reply with clear, honest advice."]}],"faq":[{"q":"What helmet should I wear on an electric dirt bike?","a":"Wear a full-face motocross helmet that fits snugly, plus goggles, gloves, boots and body armour. Check the helmet’s certification marking and replace it after any significant impact. Ride only on private property or approved off-road areas. See the helmets, gloves & protection range for current models and prices."},{"q":"How do I know my helmet fits?","a":"It should sit level, snug but not painful, with no movement when you shake your head. The chin strap should hold it firmly in place. If it moves or leaves pressure points, try another size or shape. Browse the helmets, gloves & protection category to compare models side by side."},{"q":"What helmets does Torque E-Bikes sell?","a":"The helmets, gloves and protection category lists bike helmets, kids’ helmets, motocross helmets, smart helmets, goggles and protective armour. Browse by type and check each page for sizing. The team can confirm fitment or availability on WhatsApp. Prices are in Australian dollars and each product page lists its specifications."},{"q":"Is a smart helmet worth it?","a":"A smart helmet adds features such as lights or audio, which can help visibility and navigation. Fit, certification and comfort still come first. Compare the features you will use against the extra cost. Browse the helmets, gloves & protection category to compare models side by side."},{"q":"Where does Torque E-Bikes list helmets and riding gear?","a":"Torque E-Bikes lists helmets and riding gear in the helmets, gloves & protection category, with the specification and the price in Australian dollars on each product page. The team can confirm fitment, stock or freight on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 before you order."}],"keywords":{"primary":"ebike helmet guide","supporting":["how to choose a bike helmet","bike helmet sizing","what to wear when riding an ebike","dirt bike gear for beginners","ebike protective gear guide"]},"words":1327},"e-bike-lights":{"hook":"E-bike lights make you visible and let you see the path. A front light and a rear light should both be bright enough for the conditions and mounted securely.","intro":["Good lights make you visible to other road users and let you see the path. On an e-bike you travel faster and further than on a normal bike, so lights matter more.","This guide covers brightness, mounting, power and the choice between USB and wired lights."],"sections":[{"h":"Front and rear lights you need","p":["You need a white front light and a red rear light when riding at night, and rules about reflectors and lights vary by state, so check your state’s road authority. A brighter front light helps you see, and a rear light helps others see you."]},{"h":"Brightness and beam","p":["Brightness is measured in lumens. A commuter on lit streets needs less than a rider on dark paths. A beam that spreads evenly and a mount that holds steady on bumps matter as much as raw brightness."]},{"h":"USB or wired lights","ul":["**USB lights** are portable and easy to move between bikes, but need charging.","**Wired lights** run from the bike’s battery, so there is nothing extra to charge, but they need a compatible bike and careful wiring."]},{"h":"Mounting and fitting","p":["Check that the mount suits your handlebar or fork and that the light stays aligned. Fit the front light so it lights the road rather than dazzling oncoming riders."]},{"h":"How to choose lights by the type of riding you do","p":["City riders on lit streets mainly need to be seen, so a compact light with a wide beam and a flashing mode is enough. Suburban and path riders need to see as well, so choose a front light with a medium to high output and a beam that lights the path. Country and off-road riders need strong lights, often with two front lights, one for distance and one for close range, and a helmet light for looking around corners.","Think about the ride you do most. If you ride at dawn and dusk, choose a light that is visible in daylight and has a daytime flash mode. If you ride at night on dark paths, invest in a light that offers a good runtime on its brightest useful setting."]},{"h":"Bike-powered lights versus battery lights","p":["Some e-bikes come with lights wired to the main battery. They are convenient because they turn on with the bike and do not need separate charging. Their disadvantage is that they may not be as bright or as adjustable as stand-alone lights, and a fault can be harder to fix. Stand-alone lights are easy to swap, upgrade and take with you, but you need to charge them. Many riders use both: the built-in lights as a backup, and a good stand-alone light as the main one."]},{"h":"Being visible beyond lights","ul":["Wear reflective or high-visibility clothing.","Fit reflective tape to the frame and helmet.","Use wheel reflectors or reflective tyre sidewalls.","Choose a bag with reflective panels.","Keep your lights clean and aimed correctly."]},{"h":"Maintaining lights and checking rules","p":["Charge lights regularly, check mounts and clean lenses. Carry a spare battery or a small power bank on long rides. Rules about lights, reflectors and flashing modes vary by state, so check your state road authority’s guidance. If your lights do not meet the rules where you ride, upgrade them before you head out at night."]},{"h":"Troubleshooting lights that do not work","ul":["Check that the battery is charged, or that the bike’s main battery is on.","Check the connectors and wiring for loose or corroded contacts.","Check that the switch or the bike’s display is set to lights on.","Clean contacts and replace damaged cables.","If the problem persists, ask a mechanic or the seller."]},{"h":"Choosing lights for a family or group ride","p":["When riding in a group, make sure the last rider has a bright rear light, and the leader has a good front light. Children’s bikes should have bright lights and reflectors, and adults should check them before each ride. Plan rides to finish before dark where possible, and bring spare lights and batteries. Reflective vests and ankle bands add visibility, and a headlamp is useful for repairs.","Teach children why lights matter, and make checking them a habit before every ride."]},{"h":"Choosing between rechargeable and replaceable batteries in lights","p":["Rechargeable lights are convenient and cost less to run, but they need to be charged and can fail if the battery ages. Lights that use replaceable batteries are easy to refresh on a long trip, but they cost more over time and create waste. Many riders use rechargeable lights for daily riding and carry a spare for tours. Check the runtime, the charge time and whether the light can run while charging, which is useful on long rides.","Whichever type you choose, test your lights before each ride and keep a spare small light in your bag."]},{"h":"Daytime running lights and flashing modes","p":["A steady or flashing light during the day improves visibility, and many riders use a low-power daytime mode to save battery. Check your state’s rules about flashing lights, and use a steady front light at night."]},{"h":"Reflectors, bells and other fittings","p":["Reflectors and a bell are required on many bikes in Australia, and rules vary by state. Check your state road authority and add reflective accessories if your bike did not come with them."]},{"h":"Battery life and charging","p":["A USB light needs charging after longer rides, so keep a spare or a small power bank on commutes. Wired lights draw from the main battery and may shorten range slightly."]},{"h":"Choosing the right front light","p":["For lit city streets a light that makes you visible is enough. For dark paths and country roads you need a light that actually lights the way, with a beam wide enough to see the edges of the path and a mount that stays steady. Check the runtime on the mode you will use, because manufacturers often quote the lowest mode."]},{"h":"Rear lights and being seen from the side","p":["A bright red rear light, ideally with a steady and a flashing mode, makes you visible from behind. Side reflectors, reflective tape and ankle bands help you be seen from the side at junctions. Keep lights clean and charged, and carry a spare if you ride regularly at night."]},{"h":"Understanding lumens, beam shape and runtime","p":["Lumens measure how much light a lamp emits, but beam shape matters just as much. A tightly focused beam can reach far but leave dark patches at the sides, and a wide beam lights more of the path but does not reach as far. For commuting on lit streets, a wide, even beam of moderate brightness is usually enough, and for dark roads and paths you want more power and a beam with a clear cut-off so oncoming riders are not dazzled.","Runtime is quoted for the lowest mode, and the maximum mode drains the battery much faster. Choose a light whose useful modes last the length of your ride, and carry a spare or a power bank for long trips."]},{"h":"Fitting and aiming your lights","ul":["Mount the front light firmly so it does not slip on bumps.","Aim it so the brightest part of the beam lands on the path 10 to 15 metres ahead.","Mount the rear light where it is not blocked by bags or mudguards.","Check that lights are visible from the side and not hidden by luggage.","Test the lights before you set off, and clean the lens regularly."]},{"h":"Quick answers","ul":["**How bright should my light be?** Brighter for dark roads, lower for lit streets.","**Do I need a rear light?** Yes, at night, and it helps in the day too.","**Can I use flashing lights?** Check your state’s rules, and use a steady front light at night.","**How do I power my lights?** USB lights need charging, and wired lights run from the bike’s battery."]},{"h":"Browse lights and horns","p":["See the [lights and horns](/shop/lights-and-horns/) category, and for the whole commuting setup read our [commuter e-bike guide](/blog/commuter-e-bikes/)."]},{"h":"Quick recap","p":["In this guide we covered front and rear lights you need; brightness and beam; uSB or wired lights; mounting and fitting; how to choose lights by the type of riding you do; and bike-powered lights versus battery lights. Come back to the section you need when you are deciding, and use the related categories and guides linked above to compare real products in Australian dollars. If you would like help applying any of this to your own bike, your budget and where you ride, message the team on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 or use the contact page, and we will reply with clear, honest advice."]}],"faq":[{"q":"How bright should an e-bike front light be?","a":"Brightness depends on where you ride. Lit streets need less than dark paths or trails. Choose a light with a beam pattern that lights the ground ahead without dazzling others, and check the manufacturer’s stated output. See the lights & horns range for current models and prices."},{"q":"Do I need a rear light on an e-bike?","a":"Yes. A rear light makes you visible to traffic from behind, and many states require lights when riding at night. Use a steady or flashing red light and keep spare batteries or a charged USB light. Browse the lights & horns category to compare models side by side."},{"q":"What lights and horns does Torque E-Bikes sell?","a":"The lights and horns category lists headlights, rear lights and horns from several brands, including USB-rechargeable and bike-powered options. Open a product page for fitment details. The team can confirm fitment or availability on WhatsApp. Prices are in Australian dollars and each product page lists its specifications."},{"q":"Should I choose USB lights or lights wired to the bike?","a":"USB lights are portable and easy to move between bikes, but need charging. Wired lights run off the bike battery and are always on hand. Pick based on how often you ride at night. Browse the lights & horns category to compare models side by side."},{"q":"Where does Torque E-Bikes list bike lights?","a":"Torque E-Bikes lists bike lights in the lights & horns category, with the specification and the price in Australian dollars on each product page. The team can confirm fitment, stock or freight on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 before you order."}],"keywords":{"primary":"led light on bike","supporting":["headlight of bike","dirt bike headlight","led light bike headlight","electric horn for cycle","led light on bike guide"]},"words":1315},"e-bike-and-dirt-bike-brakes":{"hook":"E-bike brakes stop a heavier, faster bike than a normal bicycle. Hydraulic disc brakes give strong, consistent stopping, while mechanical discs are simpler to adjust.","intro":["E-bikes and electric dirt bikes are heavier and faster than normal bikes, so their brakes work harder. Good brakes are one of the most useful safety upgrades you can make.","This guide explains the main brake types, how to choose pads and rotors and when to upgrade."],"sections":[{"h":"Hydraulic or mechanical disc brakes","p":["Mechanical disc brakes use a cable and are simple to adjust. Hydraulic disc brakes use fluid, give stronger and more controllable braking with less effort and are standard on better bikes. Hydraulic systems need correct fluid and occasional bleeding."],"ul":["Never mix brake fluids: mineral oil and DOT fluid are not interchangeable.","Have a bike mechanic bleed or repair hydraulic brakes if you are unsure."]},{"h":"Pads: organic or sintered","p":["Organic (resin) pads are quieter and bite gently. Sintered (metallic) pads last longer and resist fade on long descents or heavy bikes but can be noisier. Choose by riding style and bike weight, and always use pads that match your caliper."]},{"h":"Rotor size","p":["Larger rotors give more braking power and shed heat better. Common sizes are 160 mm, 180 mm and 203 mm. Check the maximum size your fork, frame and caliper mounts allow before you buy."]},{"h":"When to upgrade","p":["If your bike is fast or heavy, upgraded brakes can improve control and reduce fade on long descents. Signs it is time: a spongy lever, long stopping distances, squealing that does not clear, or pads worn to the backing plate."]},{"h":"How to choose pads and rotors as a set","p":["Pads and rotors work together. Organic pads suit smaller rotors and gentle braking, and they are quiet. Sintered pads suit larger rotors and heavier use, and they last longer. Some pads are designed for specific brakes, so match them to your caliper model. Rotors come in different sizes and thicknesses, and many are rated for specific mounting standards. When you replace pads, replace them in pairs and check the rotor for wear and warp.","Bed in new pads and rotors as the maker recommends, with a series of controlled stops. This helps to transfer pad material to the rotor, and it improves braking power and reduces noise."]},{"h":"Brake setups for common e-bike types","ul":["**Commuter e-bikes:** two-piston hydraulic brakes with 160 or 180 mm rotors.","**E-mountain bikes:** four-piston hydraulic brakes with 180 to 203 mm rotors.","**Cargo bikes and trikes:** large rotors and strong calipers, often with a parking brake.","**Fat-tyre bikes:** hydraulic brakes with large rotors for heavier bikes.","**Electric dirt bikes:** motorcycle-style calipers sized for speed and weight."]},{"h":"Brake service you can do yourself","p":["You can check pad wear, clean rotors with rotor cleaner, adjust caliper alignment and replace pads. Remove the wheel, push the pistons back gently with a flat tool, fit new pads and refit the wheel. Squeeze the lever a few times to restore the pad position and check that the wheel spins freely. Do not touch pads or rotors with oily hands. If you are unsure, a mechanic can do the job quickly.","Bleeding hydraulic brakes needs specific tools and fluid, so leave it to a mechanic unless you have experience."]},{"h":"Brake safety checklist","ul":["Test both brakes before every ride.","Check that levers do not touch the grip.","Check hoses and cables for damage.","Replace pads before they reach the backing plate.","Do not ride a bike with weak or noisy brakes."]},{"h":"Brakes for off-road use","p":["Off-road riding exposes brakes to dust, mud and water. Choose pads that suit wet and muddy conditions, clean rotors after muddy rides and check pads more often. Keep the brake lever reach set so you can use two fingers, and check for air in hydraulic lines if the lever becomes soft. If you ride downhill, learn to modulate braking, use both brakes and avoid dragging them on long descents."]},{"h":"Choosing between brake brands and levels","p":["Brake systems vary in power, modulation and maintenance needs. Entry-level hydraulic brakes use two pistons per caliper and work well for commuting. Mid-range brakes use larger calipers and better pads, and provide more control. Top-level brakes use four pistons, large rotors and cooling fins, and suit heavy e-MTBs and cargo bikes. Choose a level that suits your bike’s weight and your riding.","Check parts availability, because pads and rotors wear. Choose a brand with common parts, and check that the maker supports it. Learn the basics of care, and have the system serviced at the intervals the maker recommends."]},{"h":"Brake care for cargo bikes and trikes","p":["Cargo bikes and trikes carry heavy loads, so their brakes work harder. Check pads and rotors more often, and clean them if they squeal. Make sure all wheels brake evenly, particularly on a trike. If you ride with children, test the brakes with the load before each trip. Consider upgrading to larger rotors and better pads if you find the brakes fade, and have the system checked by a mechanic regularly."]},{"h":"Bedding in new pads and rotors","p":["New pads and rotors need bedding in to give full power. Follow the maker’s instructions: it usually involves a series of firm stops from moderate speed without locking the wheel. Avoid contaminating pads or rotors with oil or lubricant, and clean rotors with a suitable rotor cleaner."]},{"h":"Inspecting and adjusting your brakes","ul":["Check pad thickness and replace pads before they reach the backing plate.","Check rotors for warping, scoring and thickness.","Check lever feel: it should be firm, not spongy.","Check cable and hose condition."]},{"h":"Brakes on a high-power bike","p":["A bike that goes faster or weighs more needs more braking power, larger rotors and good pads. Do not upgrade the motor without checking the brakes."]},{"h":"Brake fade, heat and long descents","p":["Braking turns movement into heat, and heavy e-bikes make a lot of it. If pads and rotors overheat, braking power falls: this is brake fade. Larger rotors, sintered pads and a technique of braking in short, firm bursts instead of dragging the lever all help. If you ride long descents, check that your brakes can handle the load, and carry spare pads."]},{"h":"A basic brake service routine","ul":["Check pad thickness and replace pads when they are thin.","Check the rotor for wear, warping and contamination.","Check lever feel and travel before every ride.","Check caliper bolts and wheel axle bolts.","Have hydraulic brakes bled by a mechanic if the lever feels spongy."]},{"h":"Common brake problems and what they mean","ul":["**Squealing:** contaminated pads or rotors, or glazed pads. Clean or replace them.","**Spongy lever:** air in a hydraulic system. Have it bled.","**Weak braking:** worn pads, glazed rotors or a stretched cable.","**Rubbing:** a misaligned caliper or a bent rotor.","**Grinding:** pads worn down to the backing plate. Stop riding and replace them."]},{"h":"Choosing brakes for different kinds of e-bike","p":["A light commuter e-bike may work well with two-piston hydraulic brakes and 160 mm rotors. A heavier cargo bike or a fast e-MTB needs four-piston brakes and larger rotors. Dirt bikes use powerful calipers sized for the speed and weight. Check that your frame and fork allow the rotor size you want, and that the adaptor fits.","If you are unsure about brake upgrades, ask a bike mechanic. Brakes are safety-critical, so correct installation matters. For tyres that match, read the [tyre guide](/blog/e-bike-tyres-and-tubes/)."]},{"h":"Quick answers","ul":["**How often should I replace pads?** Check them regularly and replace them when they are thin. E-bikes wear pads faster than normal bikes.","**Can I fit bigger rotors?** Only if your frame, fork and caliper mounts allow it, using the right adaptors.","**Do I need to bleed hydraulic brakes?** Occasionally. Have it done if the lever feels spongy or the brakes lose power.","**Can I mix brake parts?** Use parts made to work together, and never mix brake fluids."]},{"h":"Browse brakes","p":["See the [brakes](/shop/brakes/) category. If you are adding power with a kit, read the [conversion kit guide](/blog/e-bike-conversion-kits-explained/), and for off-road bikes read the [Sur-Ron speed guide](/blog/how-fast-does-a-sur-ron-go/)."]},{"h":"Quick recap","p":["In this guide we covered hydraulic or mechanical disc brakes; pads: organic or sintered; rotor size; when to upgrade; how to choose pads and rotors as a set; and brake setups for common e-bike types. Come back to the section you need when you are deciding, and use the related categories and guides linked above to compare real products in Australian dollars. If you would like help applying any of this to your own bike, your budget and where you ride, message the team on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 or use the contact page, and we will reply with clear, honest advice."]}],"faq":[{"q":"How often should I replace e-bike brake pads?","a":"Replace pads when the friction material is thin, the brake squeals constantly or stopping power drops. E-bikes wear pads faster because of their weight and speed, so check them regularly and replace in pairs. See the brakes range for current models and prices."},{"q":"Are hydraulic brakes better than mechanical ones?","a":"Hydraulic brakes usually give stronger, more consistent stopping and lighter lever feel, which suits heavier e-bikes. Mechanical brakes are simpler to adjust and repair. Choose by how you ride and how easy servicing needs to be. Browse the brakes category to compare models side by side."},{"q":"What brake parts does Torque E-Bikes sell?","a":"The brakes category lists hydraulic brake sets, pads, rotors, levers, hoses and brake assemblies. Check each product page for the model it fits. The team can confirm fitment or availability on WhatsApp. Prices are in Australian dollars and each product page lists its specifications."},{"q":"Should I upgrade my brakes on a high-power bike?","a":"If your bike is fast or heavy, upgraded brakes can improve control and reduce fade on long descents. Match the upgrade to the bike’s mounting standard and rotor size, and bed the pads in before riding hard. Browse the brakes category to compare models side by side."},{"q":"Where does Torque E-Bikes list brake pads and rotors?","a":"Torque E-Bikes lists brake pads and rotors in the brakes category, with the specification and the price in Australian dollars on each product page. The team can confirm fitment, stock or freight on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 before you order."}],"keywords":{"primary":"bicycle brakes","supporting":["disc brakes bike","disc brake cycle","hydraulic brakes bike","e bike brake pads","ebike brakes"]},"words":1314},"bike-racks-baskets-and-trailers":{"hook":"Racks, baskets, panniers and trailers add carrying capacity to a bike, while towbar and car racks carry the whole bike by vehicle.","intro":["A rear rack, basket or trailer turns an e-bike into a practical vehicle for shopping, commuting and carrying equipment. Because e-bikes are heavy, any rack or trailer has to be strong and securely fitted.","This guide covers the main options and what to check, including a note on moving a heavy e-bike by car."],"sections":[{"h":"Racks, baskets and panniers","ul":["**Rear racks** hold panniers or a box and need mounting points on the frame.","**Front baskets** are convenient for light items but affect steering when loaded.","**Panniers** keep the bike narrow and agile."]},{"h":"Check the load limit and mounting","p":["Check the rack’s maximum load and that the bike’s frame is rated for it. Confirm that your bike has the right mounting points, and that the rack does not interfere with brakes, mudguards or lights."]},{"h":"Trailers","p":["A trailer carries more and bulkier loads than panniers but needs more space and care when turning and parking. Check the trailer’s load limit, its hitch fit and the bike’s ability to pull it safely."]},{"h":"Moving an e-bike by car","p":["Car racks need to carry the bike’s weight, and many e-bikes are heavier than a rack is rated for. Check the rack’s rated load per bike, and remove the battery before lifting where possible. A car-boot or towbar rack is a separate product category from the bike racks and bags we list."]},{"h":"Choosing a rack that fits your bike","p":["Rear racks attach to eyelets near the rear axle and seat stays. Check that your frame has those eyelets, and if it does not, look for a rack that clamps on, remembering that clamp-on racks often have lower load limits. Disc brakes and rear suspension can interfere with rack mounting, so check clearance. Front racks suit smaller loads and affect steering, so they are less common on e-bikes.","Check the rack’s maximum load against the weight you plan to carry, and remember that some bikes have a lower limit than the rack. If your battery mounts on the rack, the load limit may be reduced."]},{"h":"Panniers, baskets and bags compared","ul":["Panniers keep weight low and balanced, are often waterproof and can be removed quickly.","Baskets are convenient for light items and easy to load, but they raise the centre of gravity.","Rack bags sit on top of the rack and suit commuters.","Frame and handlebar bags suit small items and touring.","Trunk-style boxes offer security and weather protection."]},{"h":"Carrying bikes on a car: what to check","p":["E-bikes are heavy, so check the rack’s maximum load per bike and in total. Tow-bar racks are usually the sturdiest option for e-bikes, and some include a ramp so you do not have to lift. Roof racks require lifting a heavy bike overhead, which is risky. Remove the battery before loading, secure the wheels and frame and check that lights and number plates are visible. Check the clearance for the tailgate and the stability of the load after a few minutes of driving.","Never leave a lithium battery in a hot car, and check your car’s tow rating and your state’s rules on towing racks and bike carriers."]},{"h":"Looking after racks and carriers","p":["Check bolts and straps before each trip and tighten loose fittings. Inspect racks for cracks and corrosion, particularly at welds and mounting points. Replace worn straps, and clean and lubricate moving parts. If a rack takes a heavy knock, inspect it before reuse."]},{"h":"Securing cargo and keeping it dry","p":["Wet or loose cargo is a nuisance and a hazard. Waterproof panniers or a rain cover keep items dry, and a rack with a bungee or strap holds loose items. Do not hang loose bags from the handlebars, because they can swing into the wheel or affect steering. Keep heavy items low, and balance weight left and right. Check that nothing hangs near the wheels, brakes or lights."]},{"h":"Choosing a trailer for family or work use","p":["A bike trailer can carry children, cargo or both, depending on the model. Check the weight limit, the hitch design and whether the trailer is stable when turning. Make sure the bike’s brakes can handle the extra mass, and that the trailer has lights and a flag. Practise in a quiet area before you tow in traffic, and check your state’s rules for towing a trailer behind a bike."]},{"h":"Mistakes to avoid when carrying loads","p":["Common mistakes include overloading the rack, carrying heavy loads high on the bike, hanging bags from the handlebars and failing to secure loose straps. Another is ignoring the bike’s total weight limit, which includes the rider and luggage. Avoid carrying fragile or sharp items without protection, and check that nothing blocks your lights or reflectors.","Before a long ride with a load, take a short test ride to check balance and braking. Adjust the load, add straps and check again. A few minutes of care prevents accidents and damage."]},{"h":"A last word on racks and carriers","p":["The right rack or trailer makes an e-bike more useful, and the wrong one can be a hazard. Check the ratings, fit it properly and load it sensibly. When carrying the bike by car, use a rack rated for the weight. If you need advice, ask us."]},{"h":"Choosing panniers and bags","p":["Choose panniers that fit your rack and have a secure clip, and keep the heaviest items low and near the centre of the bike. Waterproof panniers protect the load, and reflective panels help visibility."]},{"h":"Loading and balance","p":["Distribute weight evenly side to side and keep it low. A heavily loaded rear rack changes handling, so brake earlier and take corners more slowly until you are used to it."]},{"h":"Securing the load","p":["Use straps or nets rated for the load, and check them regularly on a long ride. Make sure nothing can fall into the spokes or touch the wheel."]},{"h":"Choosing a rear rack for an e-bike","p":["Check the rack’s load rating, the bike’s own maximum load and whether the frame has the right mounting points. Racks that bolt to eyelets are more secure than clamp-on racks. Remember that the rider’s weight and the load both count towards the bike’s limit, and a heavy battery already uses part of it."]},{"h":"Carrying an e-bike by car","p":["E-bikes are heavy, so a car rack must be rated for the bike’s weight. Remove the battery before lifting, use a rack with a ramp or a tray if you can, and secure the bike with straps in several places. Check the rack, bike and number plate are visible and legal for your car, and never leave a lithium battery inside a hot car."]},{"h":"Baskets, panniers and bags compared","p":["Baskets are convenient for small items you want to reach quickly, but they raise the centre of gravity and can affect steering when loaded at the front. Panniers keep weight low and balanced either side of the rear wheel, and many are waterproof and easy to remove. Bags that strap to the frame or handlebar suit light loads and long rides. Choose based on what you carry most often, and keep heavy items low."]},{"h":"Trailers and when they make sense","ul":["A trailer suits bulky loads such as camping gear or shopping for a family.","Check the trailer’s load rating and the bike’s towing limit.","Fit a flag or lights so other road users can see the trailer.","Learn how it affects braking and turning in a quiet area first.","Check your state’s rules for trailers on bike paths."]},{"h":"Quick answers","ul":["**How much can a rear rack carry?** Check the rack’s and the bike’s load ratings.","**Can I carry a child on a rack?** Only with a seat made for the rack and the bike.","**Do I need a special car rack for an e-bike?** Yes, one rated for the weight.","**Can I leave the battery on when carrying?** Remove it where you can, to lighten the bike and protect it."]},{"h":"Browse racks, bags and trailers","p":["See the [racks, bags and trailers](/shop/racks-bags-and-trailers/) category, and read the [cargo e-bike guide](/blog/electric-cargo-bikes/) if you want a bike built for loads."]},{"h":"Quick recap","p":["In this guide we covered racks, baskets and panniers; check the load limit and mounting; trailers; moving an e-bike by car; choosing a rack that fits your bike; and panniers, baskets and bags compared. Come back to the section you need when you are deciding, and use the related categories and guides linked above to compare real products in Australian dollars. If you would like help applying any of this to your own bike, your budget and where you ride, message the team on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 or use the contact page, and we will reply with clear, honest advice."]}],"faq":[{"q":"Can I put a rack on any e-bike?","a":"Most e-bikes can take a rack if the frame has mounting points or you use a compatible clamp. Check the rack’s maximum load against the bike’s limit and make sure it clears the brakes and battery. See the racks, bags & trailers range for current models and prices."},{"q":"Do e-bikes need a special car rack?","a":"Yes, check the carrier’s weight limit. E-bikes are heavier than normal bikes, so choose a rack rated for the weight and remove the battery before travel where possible. Browse the racks, bags & trailers category to compare models side by side."},{"q":"What racks and bags does Torque E-Bikes sell?","a":"The racks, bags and trailers category lists rear racks, panniers, baskets, crates, trailers and a towbar carrier. Check each page for fit and load limits. The team can confirm fitment or availability on WhatsApp. Prices are in Australian dollars and each product page lists its specifications."},{"q":"Is a trailer better than panniers?","a":"Panniers keep the bike narrow and agile. A trailer carries more and bulkier loads but needs more space and care when turning. Choose based on how much you carry and where you ride. Browse the racks, bags & trailers category to compare models side by side."},{"q":"Where does Torque E-Bikes list bike racks and carriers?","a":"Torque E-Bikes lists bike racks and carriers in the racks, bags & trailers category, with the specification and the price in Australian dollars on each product page. The team can confirm fitment, stock or freight on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 before you order."}],"keywords":{"primary":"basket for cycle","supporting":["bike with bags","bike with a trailer","rear cycle basket","cycle with basket","4 bike carrier"]},"words":1331},"how-to-lock-an-e-bike":{"hook":"An e-bike needs a strong lock because it is worth more than a standard bicycle. A hardened chain or folding lock, used with a tracker, deters most opportunistic theft.","intro":["E-bikes are valuable and attractive to thieves, so a good lock matters more than on a normal bike. No lock is unbreakable, but a strong lock used well makes your bike a harder target.","This guide covers lock types, how to lock your bike and extra protection such as alarms and trackers."],"sections":[{"h":"Lock types","ul":["**D-locks** are compact and strong, and good for the frame and a wheel.","**Chain locks** are flexible and can secure the bike to a fixed object.","**Folding locks** pack small and give a good balance of strength and portability."]},{"h":"How to lock your bike","p":["Lock the frame and a wheel to a fixed object that cannot be lifted or cut, such as a purpose-built bike rail. Keep the lock off the ground, and fill as much of the lock’s space as you can so a thief has less room for tools. Use two different locks if you leave the bike for a long time."]},{"h":"Protect the battery and accessories","p":["Take a removable battery, display and lights with you, or use a lock for the battery if the bike has one. Parts are often stolen separately."]},{"h":"Alarms and trackers","p":["An alarm adds a deterrent and an alert, but it will not stop a determined thief. A tracker can help recover a stolen bike, and works best alongside a strong lock. Record the serial number, take photos of your bike and check your insurance."]},{"h":"How thieves steal e-bikes, and how to make it harder","p":["Thieves look for easy targets: bikes locked with thin cable locks, bikes locked to weak posts, bikes left in the same place for days and bikes with batteries and accessories that can be taken in seconds. They carry cutters, levers and angle grinders, and they work quickly. The aim of security is to make your bike take more time, noise and effort than the next one.","Use a high-rated D-lock or chain, lock the frame and a wheel to a solid fixed object, add a second lock of a different type, remove the battery and accessories and park in busy, well-lit places. At home, use a ground anchor and store the bike out of sight."]},{"h":"Choosing a lock: types compared","ul":["**D-locks:** strong, compact and good value for money.","**Chain locks:** flexible and long, but heavier. Choose hardened links and a good padlock.","**Folding locks:** compact and fairly strong, with many joints that can be targeted.","**Cable locks:** only for securing wheels and accessories, not for protecting the bike.","**Frame and wheel locks:** convenient for short stops, but not a substitute for a main lock."]},{"h":"Marking, registering and recovering a bike","p":["Record the serial number, take photos of the bike and note any marks. Engrave or mark the frame, and register the bike with an online recovery service if one is available. If the bike is stolen, report it to the police with the serial number and photos, tell your insurer and check marketplace listings and local bike shops. Trackers and alarms can help but are not a substitute for a strong lock."]},{"h":"Insurance and what to check","p":["Check whether your home and contents policy covers an e-bike, and what conditions apply, such as lock requirements, away-from-home cover and value limits. If your bike is worth a lot, consider a specialist cycle insurance policy. Keep your receipt and records in a safe place."]},{"h":"Locking different types of e-bike","p":["A folding bike can be taken inside, so lock it in the same way as any bike if you leave it outside, but prefer to bring it in. A cargo bike is long, so use a long chain or two locks to reach a fixed point. A fat-tyre bike has wide wheels, so check that your D-lock fits around the frame and rim. A mountain e-bike can be valuable, so use your best locks and consider taking it inside. In all cases, avoid locking only a wheel, because a thief can remove it and take the bike."]},{"h":"Habits that reduce the risk","ul":["Vary where and when you park.","Take the battery, display and lights with you.","Lock the bike even when leaving it for a minute.","Avoid locking to trees, signposts or weak racks.","Keep an eye on the bike where you can."]},{"h":"Teaching family members to lock properly","p":["Everyone who uses the bike should know how to lock it. Show family members where to lock, how to thread the lock through the frame and wheel and what to avoid. Keep keys or codes safe and share them only with people you trust. If the bike is shared, agree on a routine for taking the battery and locking up.","A habit of locking every time, even at home, builds good security. Many thefts happen because a bike was left unlocked for just a minute."]},{"h":"A last word on bike security","p":["No lock stops a determined thief, but good habits and good locks make theft less likely. Use strong locks, park well, take the battery and record your details. Check your insurance. If your bike is stolen, report it quickly. If you want advice on locks for your bike, ask us."]},{"h":"Where to park","p":["Park in a busy, well-lit place with a purpose-built rail or a fixed object that cannot be lifted or cut. Avoid leaving a bike in the same place overnight where you can, and bring it inside if possible."]},{"h":"What to do if your bike is stolen","ul":["Report it to the police with the serial number and photos.","Tell your insurer.","Check online marketplaces and local bike shops.","Register the bike with a recovery service if one is available."]},{"h":"Before theft happens","p":["Record the serial number, take clear photos of the bike and any marks, and keep the receipt. Consider an engraving or marking kit, and a tracker if the bike is high-value."]},{"h":"Using two locks","p":["A single lock can be defeated, but two different lock types make a thief’s job harder and slower. A D-lock through the frame and rear wheel plus a cable or chain through the front wheel is a common combination. Choose locks of a good grade, and keep them off the ground so they cannot be hit with a hammer against the pavement."]},{"h":"Secure parking at home and work","ul":["Park the bike inside, out of sight, if you can.","Fit a ground anchor in your garage or shed.","Use a lock even for short stops.","Photograph the bike and record its serial number.","Check your home and contents insurance for e-bike cover."]},{"h":"Choosing a lock: what the ratings mean","p":["Many locks carry a security rating from an independent scheme. Higher ratings mean the lock resisted a wider range of tools for longer. A lock rated for high-risk areas is heavier and more expensive, but if your bike is worth thousands of dollars, the cost is small compared with the loss. Choose a lock whose rating matches the value of your bike and where you leave it.","Remember that a lock is only as good as what you attach it to. A thin signpost can be lifted off, and a flimsy rack can be cut. Choose a solid, fixed object that cannot be removed."]},{"h":"Reducing the risk at home","ul":["Keep the bike out of sight, in a locked garage or shed if possible.","Use a ground anchor and a good lock even inside.","Do not leave the battery on the bike if it is parked outside.","Check your insurance and record your serial number.","Consider a tracker for high-value bikes."]},{"h":"Quick answers","ul":["**What lock is best?** A well-rated D-lock or heavy chain, used with a second lock.","**Is a tracker worth it?** For valuable bikes, it can help recovery.","**Should I take the battery?** Yes, if it is removable.","**Does insurance cover theft?** Check your policy."]},{"h":"Browse locks","p":["See the [locks and security](/shop/locks-and-security/) category and compare lock types before you buy. For the full commuting setup, read our [commuter e-bike guide](/blog/commuter-e-bikes/)."]},{"h":"Quick recap","p":["In this guide we covered lock types; how to lock your bike; protect the battery and accessories; alarms and trackers; how thieves steal e-bikes, and how to make it harder; and choosing a lock: types compared. Come back to the section you need when you are deciding, and use the related categories and guides linked above to compare real products in Australian dollars. If you would like help applying any of this to your own bike, your budget and where you ride, message the team on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 or use the contact page, and we will reply with clear, honest advice."]}],"faq":[{"q":"What is the best way to lock an e-bike?","a":"Lock the frame and a wheel to a fixed object using a hardened chain or D-lock, and keep the lock off the ground. Remove the battery or display if you can, and avoid leaving the bike in the same unsecured spot every day."},{"q":"Do bike trackers work on e-bikes?","a":"Trackers and tag mounts can help you find a stolen bike, but they do not stop theft. Hide the tracker well, register it, and combine it with a strong lock rather than relying on it alone. See the locks & security range for current models and prices."},{"q":"What locks does Torque E-Bikes sell?","a":"The locks and security category lists chain locks, folding locks with alarms and a tracker mount. Each page states the lock type and size. Open the locks & security category to see everything that is currently in stock. The team can confirm fitment or availability on WhatsApp."},{"q":"Is an alarmed lock worth it?","a":"An alarm adds a deterrent and an alert, but it will not stop a determined thief. It works best on top of a strong physical lock and good parking habits. See the locks & security range for current models and prices."},{"q":"Where does Torque E-Bikes list bike locks?","a":"Torque E-Bikes lists bike locks in the locks & security category, with the specification and the price in Australian dollars on each product page. The team can confirm fitment, stock or freight on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 before you order."}],"keywords":{"primary":"how to lock an e-bike","supporting":["how to lock an e-bike guide","how to lock an e-bike explained","how to lock an e-bike for beginners","how to lock an e-bike tips","how to lock an e-bike basics"]},"words":1322},"chain-alignment-high-torque-motors":{"hook":"E-bike and electric dirt bike chains and sprockets wear faster under motor torque. Keep the chain clean, lightly lubricated and correctly tensioned, and replace worn sprockets as a set.","intro":["Motor torque stresses a chain and sprockets far more than pedalling does, so e-bike and electric dirt bike drivetrains wear faster. A clean, correctly tensioned and aligned chain lasts longer and runs quieter.","This guide covers cleaning, lubrication, tension and alignment, and when to replace worn parts."],"sections":[{"h":"Why chains wear faster on motorised bikes","p":["Higher torque and heavier loads stretch chains and wear sprocket teeth. A chain that is dirty, dry or misaligned wears faster still. Regular care costs far less than replacing a chain and sprockets early."]},{"h":"Cleaning and lubricating","p":["Clean the chain with a suitable degreaser and brush, rinse and dry it, then apply a lubricant made for chains. Wipe off the excess so it does not attract dirt. Clean and re-lubricate after wet or dusty rides. See the [care, tools and hydration](/shop/care-tools-and-hydration/) category."]},{"h":"Chain tension and alignment","p":["A chain that is too tight stresses the bearings, and one that is too loose can jump off the sprocket or skip. Check tension at the middle of the chain run, as the manufacturer specifies, and make sure the front and rear sprockets are in line. Misalignment wears the chain and sprockets quickly."]},{"h":"When to replace the chain and sprockets","p":["Replace the chain when it stretches past the wear limit or skips under load, and change worn sprockets with it. Fitting a new chain on worn sprockets wears both out quickly, so replace them as a set."]},{"h":"Why alignment matters so much","p":["A chain is designed to run in a straight line between the front and rear sprockets. If the sprockets are out of line, the chain runs at an angle, wears the sides of the teeth and plates and can jump off. On a motorised bike the torque is higher, so misalignment does damage faster. Check alignment whenever you change a wheel, adjust the chain or replace a sprocket.","To check, look along the chain from behind and see whether it runs straight. Use a straight edge if you can, and adjust the wheel position in small steps. Recheck after tightening the axle nuts, because they can move the wheel slightly."]},{"h":"Tools you need for drivetrain care","ul":["A chain wear gauge or ruler.","A chain breaker or quick-link pliers.","A torque wrench for axle and sprocket bolts.","A degreaser, brush and chain lubricant.","A set of hex keys and spanners."]},{"h":"Replacing a chain and sprockets step by step","p":["Measure the new chain against the old one and cut it to length if required. Remove the old chain and sprockets, clean the area and fit the new sprockets with thread locker if the maker recommends it. Torque the bolts to the specified setting. Fit the new chain, join it with the correct link and set the tension. Check alignment, spin the wheel and listen for noise.","Ride gently for the first few kilometres and then recheck the tension, because a new chain stretches slightly as it beds in."]},{"h":"Belts and other alternatives","p":["Some bikes use belt drives instead of chains. Belts are quiet, clean and need little lubrication, but they require a compatible frame and are more expensive. They cannot be repaired roadside as easily as a chain. If your bike uses a belt, follow the maker’s tension and inspection advice carefully."]},{"h":"Drivetrain care in dusty, wet and sandy conditions","p":["Conditions change how you care for the drivetrain. In dust, use a dry lubricant, wipe the chain more often and avoid over-lubricating. In wet conditions, use a wet lubricant, clean the chain after the ride and dry the bike. In sand, rinse gently, brush away grit and re-lubricate, because sand wears chains and sprockets very fast. In all cases, avoid applying lubricant to brake rotors and pads."]},{"h":"When to ask for help","p":["If you hear grinding that does not clear after cleaning, if the chain keeps jumping or if you find cracks or bent teeth in a sprocket, stop riding and have the bike checked. A mechanic can diagnose alignment, bearing and wheel problems that are hard to see."]},{"h":"Building a simple drivetrain maintenance log","p":["Keep a notebook or a note on your phone with the date and distance when you clean, lubricate, adjust and replace drivetrain parts. Record the chain brand and the sprocket sizes. A log helps you predict when parts are due, spot problems early and show a mechanic what has been done. It also helps when you sell the bike, because a documented history adds value and confidence."]},{"h":"Tools and spares worth carrying on a ride","p":["Carry a quick-link, a small chain tool, a multi-tool with the right hex keys and a spare tube. A few zip ties and a small rag can solve many roadside problems. If you ride in a group, share tools. For longer rides, carry a spare chain link and consider a spare derailleur hanger if your bike uses one. Learn to fit a quick-link before you need to do it on the trail."]},{"h":"How often to service your drivetrain","p":["Servicing frequency depends on how much you ride and in what conditions. As a guide, wipe and lubricate the chain every couple of weeks, check wear monthly and replace chains and sprockets when they reach their wear limits. In dusty, wet or sandy conditions, clean and lubricate more often. A well-kept drivetrain lasts much longer and costs less over time than one that is neglected.","Set reminders in your phone, and keep a log of what you do. A few minutes of care each week is better than a big, expensive repair later."]},{"h":"A last word on drivetrain care","p":["A clean, aligned and lubricated drivetrain is quiet, efficient and long-lasting. A few minutes each week saves money and avoids breakdowns. If you are not sure how to adjust or replace parts, a mechanic can help, and so can we."]},{"h":"Checking chain wear","p":["A chain wear gauge shows when a chain has stretched past its limit. For a quick check, lift the chain from the front chainring: if you can see a lot of the tooth through the gap, the chain or chainring is worn. Replace a stretched chain promptly to protect the sprockets."]},{"h":"Common drivetrain problems","ul":["Chain skipping under load: worn chain or sprockets.","Noise: dry or dirty chain.","Chain dropping off: misalignment or loose tension.","Rapid wear: dirt, poor lubrication or incorrect tension."]},{"h":"Cleaning without damaging the motor or battery","p":["Avoid high-pressure water around the motor, battery and electrical connectors. Use a damp cloth and a soft brush, and let the bike dry before you charge it."]},{"h":"A monthly drivetrain routine","ul":["Wipe the chain and check for dirt and wear.","Clean and lubricate the chain, then wipe off excess.","Check chain tension and sprocket alignment.","Inspect sprocket teeth for hooking or sharp edges.","Check that axle and sprocket bolts are tight."]},{"h":"Choosing the right chain lube","p":["Wet lube sticks to the chain and lasts longer in rain, but picks up dirt. Dry lube stays cleaner in dust, but needs reapplying more often. Pick one made for chains, apply a thin coat and wipe off the excess. Keep lubricant away from brake rotors and pads."]},{"h":"Setting chain tension and alignment by eye and by measurement","p":["On bikes with a rear derailleur, a tensioner keeps the chain taut. On single-speed or motorbike-style drives, tension is set by moving the rear wheel. Aim for a small amount of up-and-down play in the middle of the chain run, as specified by the maker. Too tight stresses bearings, and too loose lets the chain jump.","To check alignment, look along the chain from the rear and see whether the chain runs in a straight line between the front and rear sprockets. A straight edge held against the sprockets can help. Make small adjustments and recheck after tightening the axle."]},{"h":"Signs that you need a new chain or sprockets","ul":["The chain skips under load.","Teeth are hooked or shark-fin shaped.","The chain has stretched past the wear limit.","You hear grinding or crunching that does not clear after cleaning.","Adjustment range has run out."]},{"h":"Parts and related guides","p":["Browse chains, sprockets and drivetrain parts in the [motors, drivetrain and sprockets](/shop/motors-drivetrain-and-sprockets/) category. Read about [conversion kits](/blog/e-bike-conversion-kits-explained/) and [Sur-Ron spares](/shop/sur-ron-spares/) if you are upgrading."]},{"h":"Quick recap","p":["In this guide we covered why chains wear faster on motorised bikes; cleaning and lubricating; chain tension and alignment; when to replace the chain and sprockets; why alignment matters so much; and tools you need for drivetrain care. Come back to the section you need when you are deciding, and use the related categories and guides linked above to compare real products in Australian dollars. If you would like help applying any of this to your own bike, your budget and where you ride, message the team on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 or use the contact page, and we will reply with clear, honest advice."]}],"faq":[{"q":"How often should I lubricate an e-bike chain?","a":"Clean and lubricate the chain regularly, and after wet or dusty rides. Wipe off excess lube so it does not attract dirt. Motor torque stresses the chain, so check wear and tension often. See the motors, drivetrain & sprockets range for current models and prices."},{"q":"When should I replace the chain and sprockets?","a":"Replace the chain when it stretches past the maker’s wear limit or skips under load, and change worn sprockets with it. Fitting a new chain on worn sprockets wears both out quickly. Browse the motors, drivetrain & sprockets category to compare models side by side."},{"q":"What drivetrain parts does Torque E-Bikes sell?","a":"The motors, drivetrain and sprockets category lists drive chains, sprockets, bottom bracket assemblies, motors and torque arms. The care category lists degreasers and rotor cleaners. The team can confirm fitment or availability on WhatsApp. Prices are in Australian dollars and each product page lists its specifications."},{"q":"Is a belt drive better than a chain?","a":"Belts are quiet and clean but need a compatible frame and cost more. Chains are cheaper, easier to find and simple to repair. Most motorised bikes use chains, so check your bike’s drivetrain before choosing. Browse the motors, drivetrain & sprockets category to compare models side by side."},{"q":"Where does Torque E-Bikes list chains, sprockets and drivetrain parts?","a":"Torque E-Bikes lists chains, sprockets and drivetrain parts in the motors, drivetrain & sprockets category, with the specification and the price in Australian dollars on each product page. The team can confirm fitment, stock or freight on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 before you order."}],"keywords":{"primary":"cycle sprocket","supporting":["sprocket in bike","new bike chain","ebike maintenance","cycle sprocket guide","how to choose cycle sprocket"]},"words":1343},"pedal-assist-vs-throttle":{"hook":"A pedal-assist e-bike adds motor power as you pedal, while a throttle e-bike adds power when you twist or press a throttle. Some bikes offer both.","intro":["A pedal-assist e-bike adds motor power as you pedal, while a throttle e-bike adds power when you twist or press a throttle. Some bikes offer both.","The difference affects how the bike feels, how far it goes and how Australian rules treat it."],"sections":[{"h":"How pedal assist works","p":["Sensors detect that you are pedalling, and the motor adds power in proportion to the assist level you choose. The bike feels like a bicycle with a helping hand, and battery range is usually longer than with throttle use."]},{"h":"How a throttle works","p":["A throttle gives power on demand without pedalling. It is convenient for starting from a standstill and for riders who cannot pedal continuously. A throttle uses battery faster, and it changes how the bike is classed."]},{"h":"Which is road legal?","p":["In Australia a pedal-assist e-bike that meets the 250W standard can use public roads, subject to state rules. A throttle that drives the motor at higher speeds changes how the bike is treated. Check your state’s road authority and read our [e-bike laws guide](/blog/off-road-vs-street-legal-australia/)."]},{"h":"Adding a throttle to a bike","p":["Some controllers accept a throttle and some do not, and adding one can change the bike’s classification. Check compatibility with your controller and your state’s rules first. See the [controls, grips and pedals](/shop/controls-grips-and-pedals/) category."]},{"h":"How assist systems feel in real riding","p":["On paper, the difference between pedal assist and throttle seems simple. On the road, the feel matters. A well-tuned torque-sensing pedal-assist system feels like having a stronger version of yourself, with power that rises as you push harder and fades smoothly as you ease off. A cadence-sensing system can feel more abrupt, with power switching on after a moment of pedalling and continuing for a moment after you stop. A throttle feels direct and immediate, like a small scooter, with no connection to your effort.","Many riders try both and find they prefer one. If you can test ride, try each system, and pay attention to how each behaves when you start from a stop, on a hill and when you ease off."]},{"h":"Starting from a standstill and walking pace","p":["Starting is where throttles shine. A tap of the throttle gets a heavy bike moving without a wobble, which helps on slopes, in traffic and with cargo. Pedal-assist bikes can handle this too, particularly those with a start-assist or walk mode, and torque-sensing systems feel more natural at low speed. If you often start on slopes or carry loads, think about how the bike will behave at the first pedal stroke."]},{"h":"Safety considerations for both systems","ul":["Learn the bike’s response in a safe area before riding in traffic.","Keep fingers over the brake levers when using a throttle in traffic.","Check that brake cut-offs work, so the motor stops when you brake.","Be cautious in wet conditions, because sudden power can cause a wheel to slip.","Keep assist levels low when learning a new bike."]},{"h":"How the rules affect the choice","p":["In Australia, the pedal-assist definition is what allows an e-bike to be treated as a bicycle. A throttle that works only at very low speed is generally accepted, but a throttle that drives the bike at higher speeds changes the classification. If you want a bike you can use on roads and paths, check the bike’s throttle behaviour and rated power against your state’s page, and read our [e-bike laws guide](/blog/off-road-vs-street-legal-australia/)."]},{"h":"Choosing the right assist level while riding","p":["Most pedal-assist e-bikes have three to five assist levels. A good habit is to use the lowest level that keeps you comfortable, and to step up only on hills, into headwinds or when carrying a load. Low levels stretch the battery and give more exercise, while high levels are useful when you are in a hurry or tired. Change gears first, then change assist, because the motor works best when you pedal at a steady cadence.","Develop a routine for your regular route: which levels you use on flat sections, climbs and descents. After a few weeks you will know how much battery your route uses and which settings suit you."]},{"h":"Common questions about throttles","ul":["**Will a throttle drain my battery faster?** Often yes, because you ask the motor to do more of the work.","**Can I use a throttle all the time?** It depends on the bike and the rules for where you ride.","**Can I add a throttle later?** Only if the controller supports it, and it can change how the bike is classified.","**Is a twist or thumb throttle better?** It is a matter of preference, but make sure the control suits your hands and does not interfere with your brakes."]},{"h":"Talking to the seller about assist systems","p":["When you buy, ask the seller to explain how the assist system works. Is the sensor cadence or torque? Does the bike have a throttle, and how does it behave? What is the maximum assist speed? Can the settings be changed? Ask for a demonstration or a video if you cannot test the bike. A good seller will answer clearly and help you choose."]},{"h":"Cadence sensors and torque sensors","p":["Pedal-assist systems use one of two sensors. A cadence sensor detects that you are pedalling and gives a set amount of assist, which feels on and off. A torque sensor measures how hard you pedal and scales the assist to match, which feels more natural and responsive. Torque-sensor bikes usually cost more."]},{"h":"Choosing by how you ride","ul":["Commuting with frequent stops: a throttle or a responsive pedal-assist system helps you start.","Fitness and longer rides: pedal assist keeps you working and stretches range.","Off-road riding: throttle control is common on dirt bikes."]},{"h":"Display, assist levels and range","p":["Assist levels trade range for effort. Use a lower level on flat ground and a higher one on hills. Check the display for battery and assist information, and learn how the bike behaves in each level before you rely on it."]},{"h":"Examples of when each system makes sense","ul":["A rider who wants fitness and range: pedal assist.","A rider with a bad knee who needs a boost at start-up: pedal assist with walk-assist, or a throttle.","A rider who rides a lot in stop-start traffic: a throttle or a responsive torque sensor.","An off-road rider on private land: throttle control is typical."]},{"h":"What to check in the product description","p":["Look for the words “pedal assist”, “PAS”, “throttle” or “twist throttle”, and whether the throttle works only up to a set speed or at any speed. Check the maximum assist speed and the rated motor power. If a seller does not state them, ask before you buy."]},{"h":"Understanding assist levels and modes","p":["Pedal-assist bikes usually offer several levels, from a gentle boost to maximum help. A low level stretches the battery and keeps you working, while a high level helps on hills and with loads. Some bikes also offer a walk mode that moves the bike slowly while you walk beside it, which is useful on stairs and ramps. Learn what each level does on a quiet road before you rely on it in traffic.","On a bike with a throttle, check whether the throttle works at all speeds or only at low speed, and whether it needs you to pedal. These details change how the bike feels and how it is treated by the rules."]},{"h":"Choosing by comfort and fitness goals","ul":["If you want exercise, use pedal assist at lower levels.","If you want to arrive fresh, use higher assist levels.","If you have health concerns, ask your doctor about the right level of effort.","If you ride with others, choose a level that lets you keep pace comfortably."]},{"h":"Which should you choose?","p":["A throttle is convenient, while pedal assist feels more like cycling and usually stretches range. For commuting see the [commuter e-bike guide](/blog/commuter-e-bikes/), and for how to pick a bike overall see [how to choose an e-bike](/blog/how-to-choose-an-e-bike/)."]},{"h":"Quick recap","p":["In this guide we covered how pedal assist works; how a throttle works; which is road legal?; adding a throttle to a bike; how assist systems feel in real riding; and starting from a standstill and walking pace. Come back to the section you need when you are deciding, and use the related categories and guides linked above to compare real products in Australian dollars. If you would like help applying any of this to your own bike, your budget and where you ride, message the team on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 or use the contact page, and we will reply with clear, honest advice."]}],"faq":[{"q":"What is the difference between pedal assist and a throttle?","a":"Pedal assist adds motor power only while you pedal, and the assist level is set on the display. A throttle gives power on demand without pedalling. Rules about throttles vary by bike type and state. See the controls, grips & pedals range for current models and prices."},{"q":"Can I add a throttle to a pedal-assist e-bike?","a":"Some controllers accept a throttle, but not all, and adding one can change how the bike is classed. Check compatibility with your controller and your state’s rules before fitting one. Browse the controls, grips & pedals category to compare models side by side."},{"q":"What throttles and displays does Torque E-Bikes sell?","a":"The controls, grips and pedals category lists twist and thumb throttles, displays and switches. Each page states the models it fits. The team can confirm fitment or availability on WhatsApp. Prices are in Australian dollars and each product page lists its specifications."},{"q":"Is a throttle better than pedal assist?","a":"A throttle is convenient and good for starting from a standstill, while pedal assist feels more like cycling and usually stretches range. The best choice depends on how you ride and which rules apply to your bike. Browse the controls, grips & pedals category to compare models side by side."},{"q":"Where does Torque E-Bikes list throttles and controls?","a":"Torque E-Bikes lists throttles and controls in the controls, grips & pedals category, with the specification and the price in Australian dollars on each product page. The team can confirm fitment, stock or freight on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 before you order."}],"keywords":{"primary":"throttle bike","supporting":["throttle of bike","throttle bike guide","how to choose throttle bike","throttle bike explained","throttle bike for beginners"]},"words":1317},"commuter-e-bikes":{"hook":"A commuter e-bike is a pedal-assist bike built for daily city riding, with mudguards, lights and a rack. Step-through frames make mounting easy.","intro":["A commuter e-bike is built for daily city riding. It usually has an upright or step-through frame, mudguards, a rear rack and lights, and a pedal-assist motor that makes hills and headwinds easier.","This guide covers what to look for and how to set the bike up for the daily ride."],"sections":[{"h":"Frame style and fit","p":["Step-through frames are easy to get on and off, and upright frames suit most riders. Choose a frame size that matches your height, and test the reach to the handlebars for comfort over longer rides."]},{"h":"Range and battery for your route","p":["Measure your round trip, and add a margin for hills and weather. Charge the battery at home or at work. Larger batteries add range and weight, so choose the capacity that suits your distance."]},{"h":"Practical features","ul":["Mudguards keep you clean in the wet.","A rear rack carries a bag or panniers.","Integrated lights help you be seen: read the [e-bike lights guide](/blog/e-bike-lights/).","Reliable brakes matter more on a heavier bike."]},{"h":"Is an e-bike cheaper than commuting by car?","p":["For short trips, an e-bike can cost much less to run because electricity costs are low and there is no fuel or parking cost, but you still pay for the bike, servicing and a good lock. Work out your own numbers before deciding."]},{"h":"Choosing a frame type for commuting","p":["Commuter e-bikes come in step-through, diamond-frame and low-step designs. Step-through frames are easy to mount, which helps with bags, skirts and quick stops, and they suit riders of all heights. Diamond frames are stiffer and often lighter, and they suit riders who want a sportier feel. Low-step frames offer a compromise. Choose the one that feels comfortable, and test the standover height if you can.","Think about how you will carry things. A rear rack with panniers, a basket or a bag all work, but the frame needs mounting points. If you want to fit a child seat later, check the frame and rack rating."]},{"h":"Gearing and drive options for commuters","p":["Many commuter e-bikes use derailleur gears, which offer a wide range and are easy to service. Hub gears, enclosed in the rear wheel, are low maintenance and handle stop-start traffic well. Some bikes use a single speed with a motor, which keeps things simple but limits hill climbing. Belt drives are quiet and clean, but they need compatible frames. For most riders, a derailleur or a hub gear offers the best balance."]},{"h":"Security and parking at work","p":["Ask your employer about secure bike parking, showers and charging. If none is available, choose a lock rated for your risk, park in a visible place and take the battery with you. A commuter e-bike is a valuable item, so treat it that way. See the [lock guide](/blog/how-to-lock-an-e-bike/) for details."]},{"h":"Dealing with weather and seasons","ul":["In rain, use mudguards, a jacket and longer braking distances.","In heat, ride in the cooler parts of the day and carry water.","In cold, wear layers and gloves, and keep the battery warm.","In wind, choose a lower position and a lower assist.","In low light, use bright lights and reflective clothing."]},{"h":"Building a commuting routine","p":["Plan your route, check for bike lanes and paths and ride it once on a weekend. Charge the battery the night before. Keep a spare tube, a pump and a multi-tool in your bag. Learn how to deal with common problems such as a flat tyre or a loose chain. Over time, commuting by e-bike becomes simple, reliable and often enjoyable."]},{"h":"A first-week checklist for a new commuter e-bike","ul":["Adjust the saddle and handlebars to fit you.","Learn the assist levels and the display.","Ride your route on a quiet day and note battery use.","Fit lights, a bell and a lock.","Check bolts, tyres and brakes after the first few rides."]},{"h":"Making the case for an e-bike commute","p":["If you are weighing up an e-bike commute, add up the costs and benefits for your own situation. Consider parking costs, fuel, public transport fares, travel time and the cost of the bike and accessories. Consider the benefits too: exercise, fresh air, predictable travel time and less stress in traffic. Many commuters find that an e-bike is competitive on time over short and medium trips, and that the savings add up.","Try it for a week by borrowing or hiring a bike, if you can. Practical experience is worth more than any calculation."]},{"h":"A last word on commuting by e-bike","p":["A commuter e-bike can change your day. It can make travel faster, cheaper and more enjoyable, and it can help you stay active. Choose a bike that suits your route, equip it for the weather and keep it secure. Ride with care and courtesy, and look after the bike. We are happy to help you choose."]},{"h":"Weather, clothing and comfort","p":["Mudguards, a chain guard and a rain jacket keep you clean and dry. Choose gloves for cold mornings and ventilated clothing for hot days, and carry spare layers in a pannier. A comfortable saddle and grips make long commutes easier."]},{"h":"Charging at work and at home","p":["A removable battery is easier to charge indoors. Charge in a ventilated area, and do not leave it charging unattended. Ask your workplace if there are secure bike parking and charging options."]},{"h":"Servicing a commuter bike","p":["Daily use wears tyres, brake pads and the chain. Check tyre pressure weekly, and have the brakes and drivetrain checked regularly. Keep the battery contacts clean and dry."]},{"h":"Setting up a commuter bike properly","ul":["Fit mudguards and a chain guard to keep clothes clean.","Add a rear rack and panniers or a bag.","Fit front and rear lights and a bell.","Choose puncture-resistant tyres.","Carry a small repair kit and a pump."]},{"h":"Planning your route and your charge","p":["Check that bike paths and roads on your route allow e-bikes, plan for hills and headwinds, and start with a full charge. If your trip is close to your battery’s range, charge at work or choose a larger battery. Check your state’s rules for helmets and lights before you set off."]},{"h":"A sample commute: what to pack and how to ride","p":["Imagine a 12 km ride to work with a couple of hills. You would pack a small repair kit, a rain jacket, a lock and a change of clothes, set the assist to a medium level on the flat and higher on hills, and plan to arrive with spare charge. Keep your lights on in low light, use a bell on shared paths, and leave extra room when you brake, because e-bikes weigh more and take longer to stop.","In the first few weeks, note how much battery you use each day. That tells you whether your battery is big enough, and whether you can ride without charging at work. Over time, you will learn to choose the assist level that gets you there comfortably without draining the battery."]},{"h":"Common commuting problems and fixes","ul":["**Punctures:** use puncture-protected tyres and carry a spare tube.","**Sweaty arrival:** use a lower assist on flat ground and a higher one on hills, and wear breathable clothes.","**Theft worries:** lock the frame and wheel to a fixed rail, and take the battery with you.","**Wet weather:** fit mudguards, wear a waterproof jacket and brake earlier.","**Battery range anxiety:** choose a bigger battery or charge at work."]},{"h":"Quick answers","ul":["**Do I need to pedal?** On a pedal-assist e-bike, yes, but the motor helps.","**Can I ride in the rain?** Yes, with mudguards and care, and avoid pressure washing electrical parts.","**How do I stop the bike being stolen?** Use a strong lock, park in busy places and take the battery.","**How fast can I go?** On a road-legal e-bike, assistance stops at about 25 km/h, and you can pedal faster than that on your own."]},{"h":"Rules, locks and gear","p":["Pedal-assist e-bikes at 250W can use roads and paths, subject to state rules: see [e-bike laws in Australia](/blog/off-road-vs-street-legal-australia/). Wear an approved helmet, and read the [lock guide](/blog/how-to-lock-an-e-bike/). Browse [commuter and urban e-bikes](/shop/commuter-and-urban-e-bikes/)."]},{"h":"Quick recap","p":["In this guide we covered frame style and fit; range and battery for your route; practical features; is an e-bike cheaper than commuting by car?; choosing a frame type for commuting; and gearing and drive options for commuters. Come back to the section you need when you are deciding, and use the related categories and guides linked above to compare real products in Australian dollars. If you would like help applying any of this to your own bike, your budget and where you ride, message the team on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 or use the contact page, and we will reply with clear, honest advice."]}],"faq":[{"q":"What is a step-through e-bike?","a":"A step-through e-bike has a low frame section so you can mount without lifting your leg over a top tube. It suits riders who want easy mounting, carry loads or ride in everyday clothes. See the commuter & urban e-bikes range for current models and prices."},{"q":"How far can I commute on an e-bike?","a":"It depends on the battery, rider weight, terrain and assist level, so check the stated range and allow a margin. Most daily commutes sit well within a typical battery, and charging at work or home resets the next trip. Browse the commuter & urban e-bikes category to compare models side by side."},{"q":"Which commuter e-bikes does Torque E-Bikes list?","a":"The commuter and urban e-bikes category lists step-through, hybrid and city models from several brands. Open a product to see its motor, battery and price. The team can confirm fitment or availability on WhatsApp. Prices are in Australian dollars and each product page lists its specifications."},{"q":"Is an e-bike cheaper than commuting by car?","a":"For short trips, an e-bike can cost much less to run because electricity costs are low and there is no fuel or parking. Compare your own trip distances and the purchase price to work out the break-even point. Browse the commuter & urban e-bikes category to compare models side by side."},{"q":"Where does Torque E-Bikes list commuter e-bikes?","a":"Torque E-Bikes lists commuter e-bikes in the commuter & urban e-bikes category, with the specification and the price in Australian dollars on each product page. The team can confirm fitment, stock or freight on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 before you order."}],"keywords":{"primary":"retro cruiser electric bike","supporting":["retro cruiser electric bike guide","how to choose retro cruiser electric bike","retro cruiser electric bike explained","retro cruiser electric bike for beginners","retro cruiser electric bike tips"]},"words":1329},"best-phone-mounts-for-e-bikes":{"hook":"A good bike phone mount holds the phone firmly over rough surfaces. Look for a secure lock, a handlebar clamp that fits your bars and a design that reduces vibration.","intro":["A phone mount lets you use maps and ride data without holding your phone. It needs to hold the phone securely over bumps, fit your handlebar and be easy to remove.","This guide covers the main mount types and what to check."],"sections":[{"h":"Clamp mounts and case-based mounts","p":["A clamp mount grips the edges of any phone and works across phones. A case-based system holds the phone more securely and removes quickly, but needs a compatible case. Choose by how often you change phones and how rough your rides are."]},{"h":"Handlebar fit","p":["Check the mount’s clamp size against your handlebar diameter, and make sure it does not clash with brake levers, lights or a display. A stem mount can be a good alternative if the bar is crowded."]},{"h":"Vibration and your phone camera","p":["Motor and road vibration can damage some phone camera stabilisation systems. Consider a mount with vibration damping if you ride on rough surfaces often."]},{"h":"Weather and security","p":["A waterproof case or cover protects the phone in rain. Remove the phone when you park, and secure the mount so it cannot be taken easily."]},{"h":"Mount types in detail","p":["Clamp mounts use spring-loaded arms to grip the phone. They are quick to use and fit most phones, but they can obscure part of the screen and may not hold a heavy phone over bumps. Case-based mounts use a twist-lock or magnetic system that holds the phone securely, and they often feel more premium, but they need a compatible case. Stem and bar-end mounts keep the bars tidy and place the phone in a good viewing position."]},{"h":"Battery and charging while riding","p":["Navigation uses a lot of battery. Use a power bank in a frame bag or a bike-mounted USB port if your e-bike has one. Make sure cables are routed so they do not catch on the bars, and protect connectors from rain. Do not leave the phone charging in direct sun, because heat can damage the battery."]},{"h":"Vibration, cameras and phone safety","p":["Motorbike and e-bike vibration can damage some phone camera stabilisation systems over time. Choose a mount with vibration damping if you ride a lot. Check your phone maker’s guidance, and consider using a dedicated cycling computer for long rides on rough ground."]},{"h":"Rules and good habits","p":["Riders are generally not allowed to hold a phone while riding. A mount lets you view navigation hands-free, but you should not operate the phone while moving. Plan your route first, use voice prompts and stop in a safe place if you need to change anything."]},{"h":"Quick comparison of mount features","ul":["Security: case-based and locking mounts hold best over rough ground.","Convenience: clamp mounts are quick and flexible.","Position: stem and bar-end mounts keep the bars tidy.","Protection: look for a cover or case that protects against rain.","Charging: some mounts include wireless or USB charging."]},{"h":"Taking care of your phone and your mount","p":["Check the mount’s fittings regularly and tighten them, and clean the contacts and clamps. Replace worn rubber parts, because a loose mount can drop your phone. If you use your phone for navigation on long rides, consider a power bank and a waterproof pouch."]},{"h":"Comparing mounts for e-bikes with displays","p":["E-bikes often have a display on the handlebar, which takes up space. A phone mount must fit alongside it, so check the available bar length and the position of the controls. Some riders mount the phone on the stem or at the bar end to avoid clutter, and others use a mount that attaches to the display bracket. Whichever you choose, check that it does not block the display or your view of the road.","If your bike has a suspension fork, think about how the phone will move. A mount fixed to the stem moves with the steering, while a mount fixed to the handlebar moves with the bars. Either works, but test both for comfort and visibility."]},{"h":"When a dedicated cycling computer is better","p":["A phone is convenient, but a dedicated cycling computer has advantages. It is built for sunlight readability, battery life and rough use. It may link to your e-bike’s data, show your speed and battery in one place and keep going all day. It does not distract you with messages. If you ride long distances, in rain or on rough tracks, a cycling computer is worth considering.","If you use a phone, protect it with a case and a waterproof cover, and keep a power bank handy. Whichever you choose, set up your route before you leave, and use audio prompts where possible."]},{"h":"Final checks before you ride with a phone mount","p":["Before you set off, check that the phone is secure by gently shaking the bars, that the screen is visible without blocking the display and that cables are clear of moving parts. Set your route, turn on do-not-disturb, and make sure the phone is charged. If it starts to rain, cover or remove the phone. If the mount feels loose, stop and fix it, because a falling phone can cause a crash.","A little preparation makes navigation helpful and safe, and keeps your attention on the road."]},{"h":"A last word on phone mounts","p":["A good mount makes navigation easier and safer, and a poor one risks your phone. Choose a secure mount that suits your bars, protect your phone from weather and use it responsibly. If you need help finding a mount that fits your bike, ask us."]},{"h":"Using navigation safely","p":["Set your route before you start, and use voice directions where you can. Glance at the screen only when it is safe, and keep your eyes on the road. Check that the mount does not block your view or the display on the bike."]},{"h":"Power and charging","p":["Navigation drains a phone battery quickly. Some mounts include wireless or USB charging, and a small power bank can keep the phone going on a long ride. Protect charging ports from rain."]},{"h":"Mirrors and audio accessories","p":["A handlebar mirror improves awareness behind you, and a small speaker or bone-conduction headphones can add audio without blocking traffic noise. Check your state’s rules on headphones while riding. See the [phone mounts, mirrors and audio](/shop/phone-mounts-mirrors-and-audio/) category."]},{"h":"Choosing between mount styles","ul":["Clamp mounts fit most phones and are easy to swap.","Case-based mounts are more secure and quick to remove.","Stem mounts keep the handlebar uncluttered.","Bar-end and top-cap mounts suit specific setups."]},{"h":"Keeping your phone safe","p":["Use a mount with a secure lock, add a strap or tether if you ride rough ground, and remove the phone when you park. Keep an eye on temperature, because phones can overheat in direct sun while charging."]},{"h":"Choosing a mount that suits your handlebar","p":["Handlebars come in several diameters, and e-bikes often have extra fittings such as displays, bells and light mounts that take up space. Before you buy a mount, measure your bar and look at where it will sit. Many mounts use rubber shims to fit different diameters, and some clamp to the stem instead, which can be cleaner on a busy cockpit.","Check that the mount does not block your view of the display or your reach for the brakes and shifters. Fit it where you can see the phone with a glance down, not a long stare."]},{"h":"Protecting your phone from vibration and weather","ul":["Use a mount with some vibration damping, especially on rough surfaces.","Use a waterproof case or cover in the rain.","Keep the phone out of direct sun when charging, to avoid overheating.","Take the phone with you when you park the bike.","Check that the mount grips firmly before you ride, and recheck after the first few rides."]},{"h":"Using your phone safely while riding","p":["A phone mount is for navigation and ride data, not for texting or calling. Set up your route before you leave, use voice prompts if you can, and pull over in a safe place if you need to change anything. Check your state’s rules about using phones while riding."]},{"h":"Browse mounts","p":["See the [phone mounts, mirrors and audio](/shop/phone-mounts-mirrors-and-audio/) category, and for the full commuting setup read the [commuter e-bike guide](/blog/commuter-e-bikes/)."]},{"h":"Quick recap","p":["In this guide we covered clamp mounts and case-based mounts; handlebar fit; vibration and your phone camera; weather and security; mount types in detail; and battery and charging while riding. Come back to the section you need when you are deciding, and use the related categories and guides linked above to compare real products in Australian dollars. If you would like help applying any of this to your own bike, your budget and where you ride, message the team on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 or use the contact page, and we will reply with clear, honest advice."]}],"faq":[{"q":"How do I stop vibration damaging my phone camera?","a":"Strong vibration can affect some phone cameras. Use a mount with a vibration damper or a stem or bar-end position that sees less shake, and remove the phone on very rough ground. See the phone mounts, mirrors & audio range for current models and prices."},{"q":"Will a phone mount fit my handlebars?","a":"Most clamps fit common handlebar diameters, but check the range on the product page. Thick grips, cables or controls can block placement, so measure your bars first. Browse the phone mounts, mirrors & audio category to compare models side by side."},{"q":"What phone mounts does Torque E-Bikes sell?","a":"The phone mounts, mirrors and audio category lists handlebar mounts, out-front mounts and phone cases from several brands. Each page states the phone and handlebar fit. The team can confirm fitment or availability on WhatsApp. Prices are in Australian dollars and each product page lists its specifications."},{"q":"Is a case-based mount better than a clamp?","a":"A case-based system holds the phone more securely and removes quickly, but it needs a compatible case. A universal clamp fits more phones but can be less secure on rough ground. Browse the phone mounts, mirrors & audio category to compare models side by side."},{"q":"Where does Torque E-Bikes list phone mounts?","a":"Torque E-Bikes lists phone mounts in the phone mounts, mirrors & audio category, with the specification and the price in Australian dollars on each product page. The team can confirm fitment, stock or freight on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 before you order."}],"keywords":{"primary":"phone mounts for e-bikes guide","supporting":["phone mounts for e-bikes guide","how to choose phone mounts for e-bikes","phone mounts for e-bikes explained","phone mounts for e-bikes tips","phone mounts for e-bikes basics"]},"words":1338},"how-to-choose-an-e-bike":{"hook":"Choosing an e-bike comes down to where you ride, how much power you need and how far you go. Motor power, battery capacity and frame size matter most.","intro":["Choosing an e-bike comes down to six decisions: where you ride, how much motor power you need, how far you need to go, what size and style of bike fits you, what brakes and parts you want, and what you can spend. Answer them in that order and the shortlist makes itself.","This guide walks through each step in plain language, with the questions to ask before you buy."],"sections":[{"h":"Step 1: Where will you ride?","p":["Write down your three most common trips. A flat ride to the station, a hilly commute, a weekend trail and a trip with shopping each point to a different bike.","Roads and bike paths point to a pedal-assist e-bike that meets the 250W rule. Private land and rough trails point to a mountain bike or an off-road bike. See our [e-bike laws guide](/blog/off-road-vs-street-legal-australia/) before you decide."]},{"h":"Step 2: Motor power and how it is delivered","p":["On a road-legal e-bike the motor is limited to 250W, so choose on quality and feel rather than watts. Mid-drive motors give natural power on hills, and hub motors are simple and common on lower-priced bikes. For off-road bikes, higher power means more speed and more risk, so match it to the rider. Our [pedal assist vs throttle guide](/blog/pedal-assist-vs-throttle/) explains how power is delivered."]},{"h":"Step 3: Battery capacity and range","p":["A larger battery gives more range but adds weight and cost. Quoted range is a best case: hills, wind, heavy riders and high assist levels shorten it. Choose a battery with margin for your longest regular trip, and read the [battery care guide](/blog/electric-moto-maintenance-lithium-safety/) so it lasts.","If you want long range, look at models with larger batteries or a removable second battery."]},{"h":"Step 4: Frame style and size","ul":["Step-through frames are easy to mount and suit mixed-ability riders.","Folding frames suit small spaces: see [folding e-bikes](/blog/folding-electric-bikes/).","Cargo frames carry loads: see [electric cargo bikes](/blog/electric-cargo-bikes/).","Mountain and fat-tyre frames suit rough ground: see [electric mountain bikes](/blog/electric-mountain-bike-buying-guide/) and [fat-tyre e-bikes](/blog/fat-tyre-e-bikes/)."],"p":["Check the maker’s size chart against your height and inside leg, because an incorrect size spoils the ride however good the bike is."]},{"h":"Step 5: Brakes, tyres and components","p":["Heavier, faster bikes need good brakes: look for hydraulic disc brakes on hilly or fast bikes, and see the [brakes guide](/blog/e-bike-and-dirt-bike-brakes/). Check tyre width for your surfaces, and make sure lights, mudguards and a rack are included if you commute."]},{"h":"Step 6: Budget, warranty and support","p":["Set a budget, and compare bikes of the same type in the same price band. The [budget e-bikes](/electric-bikes/budget/) page lists the lowest-priced models. Look at the warranty, who handles claims and how easy it is to get parts. Ask the team on WhatsApp if you want help comparing two bikes.","To see every type in one place, browse [all electric bikes](/electric-bikes/)."]},{"h":"Understanding motor types in plain language","p":["There are two common motor layouts. A hub motor sits in the centre of the front or rear wheel and pushes the wheel directly. It is simple, quiet, cheap to make and easy to fit to a frame, which is why it is common on commuter and budget bikes. A mid-drive motor sits at the pedals and drives the chain, so it works through the bike’s gears. That gives strong, efficient climbing and a natural feel, at a higher price and with more wear on the chain and cassette.","For flat commuting, a good hub motor is hard to fault. For hilly commuting, touring and trail riding, a mid-drive often feels better, because you can drop to a low gear on a steep climb and let the motor spin at an efficient speed. Whichever you choose, the controller, sensor and battery matter as much as the motor."]},{"h":"Sensors: cadence or torque","p":["A cadence sensor detects that you are pedalling and applies a preset level of assist. It feels like an on-off boost, and it is common on lower-priced bikes. A torque sensor measures how hard you push and adds assist in proportion, so the bike feels like a stronger version of you. Torque sensors are smoother, more efficient and better on technical terrain, but they cost more.","If you are choosing between two similar bikes, the one with a torque sensor will usually feel more natural, especially at low speed and on hills. If you only ride short, flat trips, a cadence sensor is fine."]},{"h":"Battery capacity, range and charging explained","p":["Battery capacity is quoted in watt-hours (Wh). A 36V 10Ah battery is 360Wh, and a 48V 14Ah battery is 672Wh. Many riders consume somewhere in the region of 10 to 25 Wh per kilometre depending on assist level, hills, wind, tyres and rider weight. At that rate, a 500Wh battery might carry you 20 to 50 km. That is a wide range, which is why you should plan with margin and learn your own consumption in the first few weeks.","Charging is simple, but it deserves care. Use the charger that came with the bike, charge somewhere ventilated and away from flammable material, and avoid charging unattended or overnight. A removable battery is convenient because you can charge it indoors or at your desk. Read our [battery care guide](/blog/electric-moto-maintenance-lithium-safety/) for storage and safety advice."]},{"h":"Frames, sizes and body position","p":["A frame that fits you matters more than any component. Upright positions such as a step-through commuter suit short, relaxed trips and make mounting easy. A more forward position suits fitness and speed but loads your wrists and neck. Mountain bikes put you in an active position, with the saddle lower and the bars wider. Cargo bikes and trikes are built for stability and carrying capacity.","Take your measurements before you shop: height, inside leg and, if you can, reach. Compare them with the maker’s size chart. If you are between sizes, choose the one that gives you a comfortable reach and a saddle you can adjust to the right height, and ask the seller for advice."]},{"h":"Brakes, tyres and suspension on a heavier bike","p":["An e-bike weighs more than a normal bike and travels faster, so braking, tyre grip and suspension matter more. Hydraulic disc brakes are the sensible choice for most e-bikes. Tyres should have a good load rating, a tread that suits your surfaces and puncture protection if you ride in town. Suspension is useful on rough ground, but cheap suspension forks can add weight without adding comfort, so a rigid fork with a suspension seat post is often better on a budget commuter."]},{"h":"Accessories that make an e-bike practical","ul":["Mudguards and a chain guard for wet weather and clean clothes.","A rear rack and panniers or a basket for carrying.","Front and rear lights, plus a bell.","A strong D-lock and a second lock for high-value bikes.","A phone mount and a spare tube with a pump.","A helmet that meets the standard, and gloves."],"p":["Read the guides for [e-bike lights](/blog/e-bike-lights/), [locks](/blog/how-to-lock-an-e-bike/), [racks and trailers](/blog/bike-racks-baskets-and-trailers/) and [phone mounts](/blog/best-phone-mounts-for-e-bikes/) before you spend money on extras."]},{"h":"Where to buy and what to do after you buy","p":["You can buy from a local bike shop, directly from an online seller or through a marketplace. A local shop offers a test ride and local service. An online seller often offers a wider range and lower prices, but you should check shipping, returns and support. For either, ask who handles warranty and spare parts, and whether the seller has stock of batteries, chargers and wear items.","When the bike arrives, check for transport damage, tighten all bolts, set tyre pressure, adjust the saddle and bars, charge the battery and test the brakes in a safe place before riding. Keep the manual, receipt and serial number somewhere safe. After the first few rides, check bolts again, because new bikes settle."]},{"h":"Ownership costs over three years","p":["Think about the total cost, not only the price. Over three years you will probably spend money on tyres, brake pads, chains and sprockets, a lock, lights and possibly a replacement charger. Electricity is cheap, but bike shop labour is not. If you expect to ride a lot, budget for a service every six to twelve months, and for a replacement battery in the longer term, because batteries wear gradually.","A well-chosen bike with common parts, from a seller who supports it, usually costs less to own than a cheaper bike with unusual parts that you cannot get. That is why this guide keeps coming back to service, parts and warranty alongside price."]},{"h":"Making the final choice","p":["Write down your top three bikes, and compare them on five measures: fit, motor and sensor, battery capacity, brakes and warranty with support. Weigh price last, because a good bike at a fair price beats a cheap bike you stop using. If you are still unsure, message the team with your height, budget, typical trip and where you ride, and we will point you to the types and models that fit. Browse every type on the [electric bikes](/electric-bikes/) page."]},{"h":"Choosing for a hilly area","p":["Hills change what you need. A bike that is fine on flat ground can struggle on a steep climb, especially with a heavy rider or load. For hills, look for a motor with good low-speed torque, a mid-drive if the budget allows, a wide gear range, a battery with extra capacity and brakes that do not fade on descents. Keep an eye on the maker’s climb claims, and ask owners who ride similar hills.","Technique helps too. Shift to a low gear before the climb, keep your cadence up and use a higher assist level only where you need it. On the way down, use both brakes smoothly and avoid dragging them for long periods."]},{"h":"Choosing for a long commute","p":["If your trip is longer than about 15 km each way, battery capacity and comfort become the priority. Choose a bike with a battery that can cover a round trip with margin, or one that lets you charge at work. A comfortable saddle, ergonomic grips, a suspension seat post and wider tyres reduce fatigue. Lights, mudguards and a lock with a good rating become daily essentials.","A mid-drive or a good hub motor with a torque sensor will feel smoother over a long ride. Keep your assist in a level that does not drain the battery too early, and use the lowest level that keeps you comfortable."]},{"h":"Choosing for an older or less confident rider","p":["A step-through frame, a low standover height, a comfortable upright position and a smooth, predictable motor are the key features. Look for a bike with good brakes, a low-speed start that is not abrupt, and a saddle height that lets the rider put a foot down at stops. A bike that feels stable at low speed gives confidence, and a bike with a throttle or walk-assist can help when starting on a slope.","Consider a test ride and ask about adjustable stems and seat posts. If balance is a concern, a trike can be a good alternative. See the [electric trike guide](/blog/electric-trikes-for-adults/)."]},{"h":"Choosing for a teenager or younger rider","p":["Choose a bike that fits the rider now, with brakes they can operate easily, a motor limited to the pedal-assist rules and lights and a helmet that meets the standard. Set rules about where and when to ride, and check that the bike meets the rules for the places they will use it. For off-road bikes on private land, see the [kids’ electric dirt bike guide](/blog/kids-electric-dirt-bikes/) and make sure there is supervision."]},{"h":"Mistakes to avoid","ul":["Buying a bike for the best-case range rather than the real range.","Choosing a frame that is too large because it looks better.","Spending the whole budget on the bike and nothing on a helmet, lock and lights.","Assuming any e-bike is legal on any road.","Not checking how the battery will be delivered and who will service the bike."]},{"h":"A worked example: choosing between two real-world scenarios","p":["Imagine two riders. The first lives in a flat suburb, commutes 8 km each way, owns a unit with limited storage and wants to avoid arriving sweaty. A folding or compact commuter with a 400 to 500Wh battery, mudguards, a rack and good lights suits them, with a removable battery they can charge inside. The second lives in a hilly area, commutes 18 km each way and carries a laptop and a change of clothes. A commuter or trekking e-bike with a torque-sensing motor, a larger battery of 600Wh or more, hydraulic brakes and a wide gear range suits them.","Notice that neither rider needs the most powerful bike. They need a bike matched to the route, the storage and the habits. That is the pattern to follow: describe your use first, then choose features."]},{"h":"How to compare two bikes that look similar","ul":["Compare the battery in watt-hours, not volts or amp-hours alone.","Compare the brakes by brand, type and rotor size.","Compare the motor type and sensor, because they change how the bike feels.","Compare the weight with the battery, and check the rider limit.","Compare the warranty terms and who handles claims.","Compare the cost of replacement parts such as batteries and chargers."],"p":["A bike with slightly lower specifications but better support and common parts is often the better buy over several years."]},{"h":"Planning where the bike will live","p":["Before you order, decide where the bike will be stored and charged. A secure, dry spot, a power point you can use safely and a lock or anchor reduce risk and stress. If you live in a unit or share a building, check the building’s rules about e-bikes and lithium batteries, because some buildings restrict charging indoors or in shared areas."]},{"h":"A final word on patience","p":["Buying an e-bike is a large purchase, so take your time. Read reviews of the model you are considering, ask the seller questions and compare at least two alternatives. A bike chosen carefully will serve you for years, and a bike chosen in a hurry often sits unused."]},{"h":"Key takeaways","ul":["Choose the type of bike for how and where you ride, then compare features.","Match battery capacity to your longest trip with a margin.","Fit, brakes and support matter as much as the motor.","Budget for a helmet, lock, lights and servicing.","Check the rules for where you plan to ride before you buy."]},{"h":"Using this guide with the team","p":["If you have worked through this guide and still have questions, bring your answers to the team. Tell us where you ride, how far, your height, your budget and what you want to carry. We will suggest types and models, explain the trade-offs and say honestly if we do not have a bike that suits you. You can reach us on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 or through the contact page, and we will reply with clear advice rather than a sales pitch.","Buying an e-bike should be an exciting step, not a stressful one. Take your time, ask questions and choose a bike you will enjoy riding for years."]},{"h":"A last word on choosing","p":["Choosing an e-bike is easier when you know what you need. Use this guide as a checklist, and ask for help when you want it. The right bike will make riding easier and more enjoyable."]},{"h":"Common mistakes when choosing an e-bike","ul":["Buying for the best-case range and not the real one.","Choosing the wrong frame size because the price was good.","Ignoring brakes, which matter more on a heavier bike.","Assuming a powerful bike is road legal.","Forgetting about charging, storage and weight."]},{"h":"Test, compare and ask","p":["If you can, test ride bikes of the type you are considering, and compare two or three in the same price band. Ask the seller about warranty, parts and servicing, and check how you will get help if something goes wrong. If you buy online, ask about returns and delivery before you order."]},{"h":"Buying for another person","p":["If you are buying for someone else, ask about their height, strength and riding experience, and check the rules for where they will ride. For a gift, a bike with a lower step-through frame, good brakes and a modest motor is often the safest choice."]},{"h":"After you buy","p":["Read the manual, check the bolts and brakes, set up the saddle and handlebars, and charge the battery as the maker advises. Wear a helmet, learn the controls in a quiet place and service the bike regularly."]},{"h":"Match the bike to your life with five quick scenarios","ul":["**Daily commute of 10 km, mostly flat:** a commuter e-bike with a mid-size battery, mudguards, a rack and lights.","**Hilly commute of 20 km:** a commuter or trekking bike with a larger battery and a motor with good climbing torque.","**Weekend trail riding:** an e-MTB, hardtail or full suspension depending on budget and trail roughness.","**Small flat and a train commute:** a folding e-bike you can carry and store easily.","**Kids, shopping and errands:** a cargo e-bike or trike with a high load rating and strong brakes."]},{"h":"How to test fit and comfort","p":["A good fit makes a bigger difference than any component. Stand over the frame with your feet flat: you should have clearance. Sit on the saddle with a slight bend in your knee at the bottom of the pedal stroke. Reach for the handlebars with a slight bend in your elbows and no strain in your shoulders. If anything feels stretched or cramped, try a different frame size or ask about adjustable stems and seat posts.","Test the controls too. You should be able to reach the brake levers and display without letting go of the bars, and the controls should be easy to use with gloves on."]},{"h":"Costs beyond the price tag","ul":["A helmet, lights, a lock and possibly panniers or a rack.","Servicing: brake pads, tyres, chains and eventually a battery.","Insurance: check whether your policy covers an e-bike and what it excludes.","Charging: electricity is cheap, but you need a safe place to charge.","Delivery: freight for a heavy bike with a lithium battery costs more than for a normal bike."]},{"h":"Questions to ask the seller","ul":["What is the motor’s continuous power, and does the bike meet the pedal-assist definition in my state?","What is the battery’s capacity in watt-hours, and what range do riders actually get?","What does the warranty cover, for how long and who handles a claim?","Are spare batteries, chargers and brake pads available separately?","What are the delivery arrangements for a lithium battery?"]},{"h":"Quick answers from first-time buyers","ul":["**How much range do I need?** Add up your longest regular trip, then add a margin of about 30 to 50 per cent.","**Should I choose a mid-drive or a hub motor?** Mid-drives feel natural on hills, and hubs are simpler and cheaper.","**Can I fit a child seat?** Check the frame, the rack and the maker’s guidance, and use only seats made for the bike.","**How do I transport it?** Check the bike’s weight and the rack’s rating, and remove the battery before lifting.","**What if I do not like it?** Check the return policy before you order, especially for online purchases."]},{"h":"A final decision checklist","ul":["I know where and how I will ride, and the rules there.","I know my budget, including accessories.","The frame size fits me.","The battery is big enough for my longest trip.","The brakes suit the bike’s weight.","I know who will service and support the bike."]},{"h":"A quick checklist before you buy","ul":["Does it meet the rules for where I will ride?","Is the battery big enough for my longest trip?","Does the frame size suit my height?","Are the brakes strong enough for the bike’s weight?","Is there warranty and parts support in Australia?"]},{"h":"Quick recap","p":["In this guide we covered step 1: Where will you ride?; step 2: Motor power and how it is delivered; step 3: Battery capacity and range; step 4: Frame style and size; step 5: Brakes, tyres and components; and step 6: Budget, warranty and support. Come back to the section you need when you are deciding, and use the related categories and guides linked above to compare real products in Australian dollars. If you would like help applying any of this to your own bike, your budget and where you ride, message the team on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 or use the contact page, and we will reply with clear, honest advice."]}],"faq":[{"q":"How fast does a 250W e-bike go?","a":"In Australia a road-legal 250W pedal-assist e-bike’s motor assistance cuts out at 25 km/h, though you can ride faster by pedalling. Speed on the flat also depends on rider weight, terrain and wind. See the electric bikes (all e-bikes hub) range for current models and prices."},{"q":"How much motor power do I need?","a":"A 250W motor suits road and path riding and meets the Australian standard for pedal-assist e-bikes. Higher-power motors are for off-road and private property use only. Choose by where you will ride. Browse the electric bikes (all e-bikes hub) category to compare models side by side."},{"q":"Can Torque E-Bikes help me choose?","a":"Yes. Message the team on WhatsApp with your riding plans and budget and they will suggest models from the range. Each category page also lists the models with prices. The team can confirm fitment or availability on WhatsApp. Prices are in Australian dollars and each product page lists its specifications."},{"q":"Is a bigger battery always better?","a":"A bigger battery adds range but also weight and cost. If your trips are short, a smaller battery may be enough. Match capacity to your normal trip with some margin. Browse the electric bikes (all e-bikes hub) category to compare models side by side."},{"q":"Where does Torque E-Bikes list e-bikes for every type of rider?","a":"Torque E-Bikes lists e-bikes for every type of rider in the electric bikes (all e-bikes hub) category, with the specification and the price in Australian dollars on each product page. The team can confirm fitment, stock or freight on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 before you order."}],"keywords":{"primary":"long range ebikes","supporting":["how fast does an electric bicycle go","how fast do electric bikes go","how fast can an e bike go","electric bicycle range","how fast does a n ebike go"]},"words":3178},"what-is-an-e-bike":{"hook":"An e-bike is a bicycle with an electric motor and rechargeable battery that assists the rider. Most e-bikes add power as you pedal, and some also have a throttle.","intro":["An e-bike is a bicycle with an electric motor and battery that helps you pedal. You still ride the bike, but the motor adds power so hills, headwinds and longer trips take less effort.","This guide explains how an e-bike works, the main types and how it differs from an electric motorbike."],"sections":[{"h":"How an e-bike works","p":["A battery supplies power to a motor, and a controller manages how much power the motor gives. On a pedal-assist e-bike, a sensor detects pedalling and the controller adds power in proportion to the assist level you choose. The bike has the same brakes, gears and tyres as a normal bike, but built for the extra weight and speed."]},{"h":"The main parts","ul":["The motor: in the front or rear wheel (hub) or at the cranks (mid-drive).","The battery: a rechargeable lithium pack, usually on the frame or rack.","The controller and display: manage power and show assist level and battery.","The sensor: reads pedalling (cadence or torque)."]},{"h":"Types of e-bike","p":["Commuter, mountain, folding, cargo, fat-tyre and road e-bikes all use the same basic parts. See the main types in our [e-bike buying guide](/blog/best-electric-bikes-in-australia/), or browse every category on the [electric bikes](/electric-bikes/) page."]},{"h":"Is an e-bike better than a normal bike?","p":["An e-bike makes hills and longer trips easier and suits riders who want less effort. A normal bike is lighter and cheaper to own. If distance or hills put you off riding, an e-bike usually pays for itself in use."]},{"h":"A short guide to e-bike terms","ul":["**Pedal assist (PAS):** the motor helps while you pedal.","**Throttle:** a control that gives power without pedalling.","**Hub motor:** a motor in the wheel.","**Mid-drive:** a motor at the pedals that drives the chain.","**Watt-hours (Wh):** the amount of energy a battery stores.","**Torque sensor and cadence sensor:** devices that detect your pedalling.","**Walk assist:** a low-speed mode that helps when walking beside the bike."]},{"h":"How an e-bike differs to ride","p":["An e-bike feels like a normal bike with a strong tailwind. The first time you ride one, the push from the motor is noticeable, and you will want to start in a low assist level. The bike is heavier, so it feels solid and stable, but it takes more care when wheeling, parking or lifting. Braking distances are longer, and you may reach speeds you are not used to, so keep a margin and look further ahead.","Most riders adapt in a few rides. Practise in a quiet area, learn the controls and then move on to busier routes."]},{"h":"Why people buy e-bikes","ul":["To commute without arriving tired or sweaty.","To tackle hills and headwinds.","To carry loads or children.","To ride further with friends or family of different fitness levels.","To keep riding as fitness or health changes.","To replace some car trips."]},{"h":"Where to learn more","p":["When you are ready to choose, read our [guide to choosing an e-bike](/blog/how-to-choose-an-e-bike/) and our [guide to the best types of e-bike](/blog/best-electric-bikes-in-australia/). If you are unsure about rules, read the [e-bike laws guide](/blog/off-road-vs-street-legal-australia/). Our team can also help you decide which type suits your riding."]},{"h":"What an e-bike can and cannot do for your fitness","p":["Studies and riders consistently report that e-bike riders often ride more frequently and for longer than they would on a normal bike, which can improve fitness and wellbeing. You still pedal, so you still exercise, and you can choose how much help you receive. An e-bike is not a substitute for all exercise, but it can make activity easier to start and sustain.","If you want more workout, use a low assist level and ride longer routes. If you want a gentle ride, use a higher level. It is your choice, and that flexibility is one of the e-bike’s strengths."]},{"h":"Questions to ask when you first try one","ul":["How does the assist feel when I start from a stop?","Is the display easy to read and use?","Do the brakes feel strong and controllable?","Is the bike comfortable for my height and position?","How does the weight feel when I wheel or lift it?"]},{"h":"A short buying guide for beginners","p":["If you are new to e-bikes, start by deciding where you will ride and what you will carry. Then choose a type, set a budget and compare two or three bikes. Check the battery size, the brakes and the warranty, and make sure the bike meets the rules for your riding. Add a helmet, lights and a lock to the budget. When you are ready, our [e-bike buying guide](/blog/best-electric-bikes-in-australia/) walks through the options in detail."]},{"h":"A note on e-bike safety for new riders","p":["New riders should take care in the first few rides. The bike is heavier and faster than it feels, so brake earlier and keep your hands on the bars. Start in a low assist level, learn how the bike responds and avoid busy areas until you are confident. Wear a helmet, use lights and follow the road rules. If you are returning to cycling after a long break, consider a short skills session or a ride with an experienced friend.","Take your time and enjoy getting used to the bike. Within a few weeks, most riders feel natural and comfortable."]},{"h":"How far can an e-bike go?","p":["Range depends on battery capacity, assist level, rider weight, terrain, wind, tyre pressure and temperature. A typical pedal-assist e-bike can cover many kilometres on a charge, but the maker’s figure is a best case. Choose a battery with margin for your longest trip."]},{"h":"Do you still need to pedal?","p":["On a pedal-assist e-bike, yes: the motor helps while you pedal. Some bikes also have a throttle, which gives power without pedalling. See the [pedal assist vs throttle guide](/blog/pedal-assist-vs-throttle/)."]},{"h":"Charging and cost to run","p":["An e-bike battery charges from a normal power point, and a full charge uses a small amount of electricity. Running costs are low compared with a car, though you still pay for servicing and replacement parts such as tyres, brake pads and eventually a battery."]},{"h":"Common e-bike myths","ul":["“E-bikes do all the work.” On a pedal-assist bike you still pedal.","“E-bikes are only for older riders.” Riders of every age use them for commuting, trails and errands.","“E-bikes can go anywhere.” Rules and trail access still apply.","“The battery is the only thing to worry about.” Brakes, tyres and the drivetrain also wear faster on a heavier bike."]},{"h":"What you need to get started","ul":["A helmet that meets the standard.","A lock and lights.","A safe place to charge.","A bit of practice in a quiet area."]},{"h":"The different kinds of e-bike","ul":["**City and commuter bikes** with upright positions, mudguards and racks.","**Mountain bikes** with suspension and strong brakes.","**Folding bikes** for small spaces and transport.","**Cargo bikes and trikes** for loads and passengers.","**Fat-tyre bikes** for sand, gravel and snow.","**Road and gravel bikes** with a light motor."]},{"h":"Is an e-bike right for you?","p":["An e-bike suits you if hills, distance or headwinds put you off riding, if you want to commute without arriving sweaty, or if you want to carry loads. It may not suit you if you only ride short, flat trips and prefer the lightest possible bike. If you are unsure, hire or test ride one first, and read our [guide to choosing an e-bike](/blog/how-to-choose-an-e-bike/)."]},{"h":"Quick answers","ul":["**How fast can an e-bike go?** On a road-legal e-bike, assistance stops at about 25 km/h.","**How long does a battery last on a ride?** It depends on the size, assist level, terrain and rider.","**Is an e-bike hard to maintain?** The bike needs the same care as a normal bike plus battery care.","**Do I need a licence?** Not for a road-legal pedal-assist e-bike, but check your state."]},{"h":"E-bike or electric motorbike?","p":["An e-bike is a bicycle with pedal assist. An electric motorbike, or e-moto, has a throttle and a much more powerful motor, and is for off-road use in Australia. Read [what an e-moto is](/blog/what-is-an-e-moto/) and the [e-bike laws guide](/blog/off-road-vs-street-legal-australia/)."]},{"h":"Quick recap","p":["In this guide we covered how an e-bike works; the main parts; types of e-bike; is an e-bike better than a normal bike?; a short guide to e-bike terms; and how an e-bike differs to ride. Come back to the section you need when you are deciding, and use the related categories and guides linked above to compare real products in Australian dollars. If you would like help applying any of this to your own bike, your budget and where you ride, message the team on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 or use the contact page, and we will reply with clear, honest advice."]}],"faq":[{"q":"How does an e-bike work?","a":"A sensor detects pedalling or a throttle input, the controller draws power from the battery, and the motor adds drive to the wheel or cranks. The rider still steers, brakes and, on pedal-assist bikes, pedals. See the electric bikes (all e-bikes hub) range for current models and prices."},{"q":"How do I charge an e-bike?","a":"Plug the charger into the battery or the bike and a standard wall outlet. Use only the charger supplied for your battery, charge in a safe, ventilated spot and do not leave it charging unattended. Browse the electric bikes (all e-bikes hub) category to compare models side by side."},{"q":"Where can I see all the e-bike types?","a":"The electric bikes hub links to every category: commuter, mountain, folding, cargo, road and fat-tyre e-bikes. Pick a category to compare models and prices. The team can confirm fitment or availability on WhatsApp. Prices are in Australian dollars and each product page lists its specifications."},{"q":"Is an e-bike better than a normal bike?","a":"An e-bike makes hills and longer trips easier and suits riders who want less effort. A normal bike is lighter and cheaper to own. It depends on how far, how hilly and how often you ride. Browse the electric bikes (all e-bikes hub) category to compare models side by side."},{"q":"Where does Torque E-Bikes list e-bikes for first-time riders?","a":"Torque E-Bikes lists e-bikes for first-time riders in the electric bikes (all e-bikes hub) category, with the specification and the price in Australian dollars on each product page. The team can confirm fitment, stock or freight on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 before you order."}],"keywords":{"primary":"what is an ebike","supporting":["what is a ebike","what is an ebike guide","how to choose what is an ebike","what is an ebike explained","what is an ebike for beginners"]},"words":1322},"fat-tyre-e-bikes":{"hook":"Fat-tyre e-bikes use wide tyres for grip and comfort on sand, gravel and rough ground. Scrambler styles add a moto-inspired frame and motor.","intro":["A fat-tyre e-bike uses very wide tyres for grip and comfort on sand, gravel, dirt and rough tracks. Add a motor and battery and you have a bike that is easy to ride on surfaces that stop a normal bike.","This guide covers what they suit, what to check and how they compare with an electric mountain bike."],"sections":[{"h":"What fat-tyre e-bikes are good for","ul":["Sand and beach tracks.","Gravel roads and unsealed tracks.","Relaxed riding with a comfortable, stable feel.","Scrambler-style bikes that pair wide tyres with a moto-inspired frame."]},{"h":"Fat-tyre or e-MTB?","p":["A fat-tyre bike suits sand, gravel and relaxed riding, while an e-MTB suits technical trails with more suspension and agile handling. Fat tyres add weight and rolling resistance on hard surfaces, which can shorten range. Read the [e-MTB buying guide](/blog/electric-mountain-bike-buying-guide/) to compare."]},{"h":"What to check","ul":["Tyre width and tread for your surfaces.","Motor type and power, and whether it is pedal assist.","Battery capacity for the range you want.","Brakes: heavier bikes with wide tyres need strong brakes.","Frame size and rider limit."]},{"h":"Rules for riding","p":["A bike with a motor above 250W is for off-road and private property use only. Check the product page and read our [e-bike laws guide](/blog/off-road-vs-street-legal-australia/) before you ride on public roads."]},{"h":"Choosing between fat-tyre brands and models","p":["The fat-tyre category is crowded, with many models that look similar. Compare the details: motor power and type, battery capacity, brakes, frame material, suspension, tyre width and weight. A model with a quality hydraulic brake and a large battery will usually be a better long-term choice than one with mechanical brakes and a small battery, even if the price is similar.","Read the specification for the rider weight limit, because fat-tyre bikes are used by a wide range of riders, and make sure the frame size suits you. Check the warranty terms and who supplies parts. For models we list, see the [fat-tyre and scrambler e-bikes](/shop/fat-tyre-and-scrambler-e-bikes/) category."]},{"h":"Riding techniques for soft and rough surfaces","ul":["In soft sand, keep your weight back, keep pedalling smoothly and avoid sudden steering.","On gravel, brake early and avoid grabbing the front brake.","On wet ground, reduce tyre pressure slightly for grip and slow down.","On hills, use a low gear and moderate assist rather than a high assist in a high gear.","Look ahead and pick a line, because wide tyres are forgiving but not magic."]},{"h":"Storage, transport and security for a heavy bike","p":["Fat-tyre e-bikes are heavy, so plan how you will store and move them. A garage floor with a wheel chock, a wall mount rated for the weight or a ground anchor are common options. For transport, use a rack rated for the bike’s weight and wide tyres, and take the battery out before lifting. For security, lock the frame and a wheel to a fixed object, and take the battery with you. Fat-tyre bikes are valuable and conspicuous, so do not leave them unattended in public places overnight."]},{"h":"Seasonal care","p":["In summer, avoid leaving the battery in a hot car or in direct sun. In winter, keep the battery warm before charging, and check tyre pressure more often, because pressure drops in cold weather. After riding on salty or sandy ground, rinse the bike gently and dry it. Lubricate the chain and check for grit around the brake pads and rotors."]},{"h":"Tyre pressure by surface: a practical starting point","p":["Fat-tyre pressure is much lower than normal bikes, and small changes matter. On soft sand, riders often run very low pressure so the tyre spreads and floats. On gravel and dirt, a little more pressure gives better rolling and protects the rim. On hard roads, higher pressure reduces drag and protects against pinch flats. Always stay within the range printed on the tyre sidewall, and use a gauge that is accurate at low pressures.","Change pressure when conditions change, and check it before every ride. If you ride from road to beach in one trip, carry a small pump or a CO2 inflator so you can top up afterwards."]},{"h":"Common problems and simple fixes","ul":["**Slow punctures:** check the valve, rim tape and for thorns inside the tyre.","**Squeaking brakes after beach rides:** clean pads and rotors, and replace contaminated pads.","**Rusty chain:** rinse, dry and lubricate after salt exposure.","**Loose spokes:** have the wheel trued and tension checked.","**Reduced range:** check tyre pressure, drag from brakes and battery condition."]},{"h":"Choosing a fat-tyre e-bike for different budgets","p":["At lower prices, fat-tyre bikes often use hub motors, mechanical brakes and steel or basic aluminium frames. They can be good value for casual riding, but check the brakes and the battery. At mid-range prices, you find hydraulic brakes, better batteries and improved suspension. At higher prices, you find stronger frames, quality components and better support.","Decide how hard you will use the bike. If you ride rarely and on gentle terrain, a simpler bike may do. If you ride often on rough ground, spend more on the parts that matter: brakes, frame and battery."]},{"h":"A last word on fat-tyre e-bikes","p":["Fat-tyre e-bikes are a good choice for sand, gravel and rough tracks. Choose one that suits your weight, your rides and your budget, and look after tyres, brakes and the battery. If you want to talk through the options, we are happy to help."]},{"h":"Tyre pressure and surfaces","p":["Fat tyres run at much lower pressures than normal tyres. Lower pressure gives more grip and float on sand and soft ground, and higher pressure rolls easier on hard surfaces. Use a gauge made for low pressures and follow the maker’s range. See the [tyre guide](/blog/e-bike-tyres-and-tubes/)."]},{"h":"Weight and handling","p":["Wide tyres and larger batteries add weight, so these bikes feel solid on rough ground but heavier to lift and store. Check the weight and the rider limit, and consider a stand or rack that suits a heavier bike."]},{"h":"Where fat-tyre bikes work well","p":["They suit beach tracks, gravel roads, fire trails and relaxed riding. Check local rules about riding on beaches and trails, because they vary and some areas restrict e-bikes."]},{"h":"Accessories that suit fat-tyre bikes","p":["Wide mudguards, a sturdy rack, a bright light and a good lock make a fat-tyre bike far more practical. Check that accessories are sized for the wide tyres and the frame, and confirm the rack’s load rating before you carry anything heavy."]},{"h":"Riding on sand and gravel safely","ul":["Let some air out of the tyres for soft sand and check pressure before you head home.","Brake early and gently, because wide tyres can slide.","Rinse salt off the bike and dry it after beach riding.","Check local rules before riding on beaches and trails."]},{"h":"Understanding the trade-offs of wide tyres","p":["Wide tyres give grip, comfort and flotation on soft ground. The price is weight, rolling resistance and sometimes a less precise feel on hard roads. On a fat-tyre e-bike the motor covers the extra effort, but the battery pays for it: expect shorter range than a slimmer-tyred e-bike with the same battery. If you ride mostly on sealed roads, a narrower tyre may suit you better."]},{"h":"Fitting and servicing fat tyres","ul":["Use tubes made for the tyre width and check the valve type.","Use a gauge that reads low pressures accurately.","Check that mudguards, racks and kickstands are rated for wide tyres.","Check the wheel bearings and spokes regularly, because heavier bikes stress them."]},{"h":"Who should and should not buy a fat-tyre e-bike","p":["A fat-tyre bike suits riders who ride on sand, gravel, farm tracks and soft ground, who value comfort and stability, and who do not mind extra weight. It suits less well if your riding is mostly fast sealed roads, if you need a light bike to carry upstairs or if storage space is tight. If you are unsure, compare it with a standard commuter or e-MTB in the same price band."]},{"h":"Accessories to add","ul":["Wide mudguards.","A rear rack rated for the load.","A strong lock.","Lights.","A tyre pressure gauge made for low pressures."]},{"h":"Models and brands to browse","p":["See the [fat-tyre and scrambler e-bikes](/shop/fat-tyre-and-scrambler-e-bikes/) category, and the [Flight Risk](/blog/flight-risk-fr505-and-fr555/) and [DiroDi Rover](/blog/dirodi-rover-gen-6/) guides for popular models. Maintain tyres with the [tyre guide](/blog/e-bike-tyres-and-tubes/)."]},{"h":"Quick recap","p":["In this guide we covered what fat-tyre e-bikes are good for; fat-tyre or e-MTB?; what to check; rules for riding; choosing between fat-tyre brands and models; and riding techniques for soft and rough surfaces. Come back to the section you need when you are deciding, and use the related categories and guides linked above to compare real products in Australian dollars. If you would like help applying any of this to your own bike, your budget and where you ride, message the team on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 or use the contact page, and we will reply with clear, honest advice."]}],"faq":[{"q":"Are fat-tyre e-bikes good on sand?","a":"Wide, low-pressure tyres spread the load and give better grip on soft ground than narrow tyres. Check the tyre size and pressure guidance, and expect more rolling resistance on hard surfaces. See the fat-tyre & scrambler e-bikes range for current models and prices."},{"q":"How heavy are fat-tyre e-bikes?","a":"They are heavier than standard e-bikes because of the larger tyres and stronger frames, so check the weight on each product page if you need to lift or carry the bike. Browse the fat-tyre & scrambler e-bikes category to compare models side by side."},{"q":"Which fat-tyre e-bikes does Torque E-Bikes list?","a":"The fat-tyre and scrambler e-bikes category lists models from Fatboy, Cruzr, DiroDi, Aventon and others, with prices on each product page. The team can confirm fitment or availability on WhatsApp. Prices are in Australian dollars and each product page lists its specifications."},{"q":"Is a fat-tyre e-bike better than a mountain e-bike?","a":"A fat-tyre bike suits sand, gravel and relaxed riding, while an e-MTB suits technical trails with suspension. Pick by the surface you ride on most. Browse the fat-tyre & scrambler e-bikes category to compare models side by side. Message the team on WhatsApp if you want advice on the fat-tyre & scrambler e-bikes range."},{"q":"Where does Torque E-Bikes list fat-tyre e-bikes?","a":"Torque E-Bikes lists fat-tyre e-bikes in the fat-tyre & scrambler e-bikes category, with the specification and the price in Australian dollars on each product page. The team can confirm fitment, stock or freight on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 before you order."}],"keywords":{"primary":"fat tyre mountain bike","supporting":["bolzzen super street","electric scrambler bike","bolzzen atom pro","cruzr stoke af","bolzzen phoenix"]},"words":1350},"electric-moto-maintenance-lithium-safety":{"hook":"An e-bike battery is a rechargeable lithium pack rated in volts and amp-hours. Voltage affects power delivery and capacity affects range.","intro":["The battery is the most valuable and most safety-critical part of an e-bike or electric dirt bike. Looking after it extends its life, and following simple safety rules reduces fire risk.","This guide covers charging, storage, signs of trouble and how lithium batteries are shipped."],"sections":[{"h":"Charging","ul":["Use the charger supplied with the battery, or one the maker recommends.","Charge in a ventilated spot, away from flammable items, and do not leave it charging unattended or overnight.","Stop using a battery that is swollen, hot, leaking or damaged.","Unplug when the battery is full."]},{"h":"Storage and winter care","p":["Store the battery somewhere cool and dry, away from heat and direct sun. If the bike will sit for weeks, leave the battery partly charged rather than full or empty, and check it every month or two. Follow the maker’s manual for the recommended storage level."]},{"h":"Getting more life from a battery","p":["Avoid fully draining the battery regularly, avoid extreme heat and cold, and keep contacts clean and dry. A battery gradually loses capacity over its life, so range falls slowly even with good care."]},{"h":"Shipping lithium batteries","p":["Lithium batteries are Class 9 dangerous goods and travel only with carriers approved for dangerous goods freight. This is why delivery of a bike or battery needs extra planning, and why Torque E-Bikes confirms freight with you before payment."]},{"h":"How lithium-ion batteries work, in plain language","p":["An e-bike battery is a pack of lithium-ion cells, controlled by a battery management system (BMS). The cells store energy chemically and release it as electricity. The BMS monitors voltage, current and temperature, balances the cells and protects the pack from over-charging, over-discharging and over-current. A good pack and a good BMS make a battery safer and longer lasting.","Heat, deep discharge, high charge rates and physical damage all shorten battery life. Treat the battery gently and it will repay you with years of service."]},{"h":"What affects how long a battery lasts","ul":["The number of charge cycles, which vary by use and cell quality.","Heat and cold, especially heat during charging and storage.","Keeping the battery at very high or very low charge for long periods.","Fast charging and high discharge currents.","Impacts and water ingress."]},{"h":"Storing a battery over a long break","p":["If the bike will not be used for weeks or months, leave the battery partly charged, around the level the maker recommends, and store it indoors in a cool, dry place. Check it every month or two, and top up if the charge has dropped. Do not store the battery on the charger, and keep it away from heat sources and flammable items."]},{"h":"Lithium battery safety at home","p":["Choose a charging spot that is not near exits, bedding or fuel. Use a smoke alarm in the area. Never charge a swollen, damaged or wet battery. If a battery smells, hisses, smokes or gets hot, move it outdoors if you can do so safely and call emergency services if there is a fire. Dispose of old batteries through a recycling drop-off."]},{"h":"Choosing a replacement battery","p":["When a battery wears out, replace it with one that matches your bike. The voltage, connector, size, mounting system and communication protocol must be compatible. Using the wrong battery can damage the controller or cause a safety hazard. Buy from the bike maker or a trusted supplier, and check that the battery includes protection and certification. If a seller offers a much larger capacity for the same price, be cautious.","Check the age and condition of the battery before you replace it. A battery that is a few years old may still have useful life, and a service by a specialist might extend it."]},{"h":"Travelling with batteries","p":["Airlines and couriers restrict lithium batteries. Check the rules before you fly or ship a battery. For driving, secure the battery in the vehicle and avoid leaving it in the heat. If you ship a battery, use a carrier that handles dangerous goods, and package it according to the rules."]},{"h":"Monitoring battery health over time","p":["Keep an eye on your battery’s range and charge time. If range falls by a lot over a few months, or if charging takes much longer, the battery may be ageing or faulty. Note the date of purchase and the number of charges, and keep a record of how it behaves. Some displays show battery health or cycle count.","If you suspect a problem, ask the seller or the maker to test the battery. Never open the pack or try to repair it yourself. A specialist can check the cells and the management system and tell you whether it can be repaired or must be replaced."]},{"h":"A last word on batteries","p":["A battery is the most valuable part of an e-bike, and looking after it pays off. Charge safely, store it properly and watch for signs of trouble. Choose replacements carefully, and recycle old batteries responsibly. If you have questions about your battery or need a replacement, contact us with your bike’s details."]},{"h":"Signs a battery needs attention","ul":["Swelling, a bulging case or a hot spot.","A smell of chemicals or smoke.","A sudden, large drop in range.","The battery not holding charge or cutting out under load."],"p":["If you see any of these, stop using the battery, keep it away from anything flammable and contact the maker or a specialist."]},{"h":"Charging habits that help","p":["Charge when convenient rather than letting the battery run flat. Avoid charging a hot battery straight after a hard ride, and let it cool first. In very cold weather, let the battery warm to room temperature before charging."]},{"h":"End of life and disposal","p":["When a battery no longer holds a useful charge, do not put it in the household bin. Take it to a battery recycling drop-off point, or ask the maker or a bike shop how to dispose of it safely."]},{"h":"Understanding volts, amp-hours and watt-hours","p":["Voltage tells you the pressure, amp-hours tell you the charge capacity, and watt-hours (volts multiplied by amp-hours) tell you the energy stored. A 48V 10Ah battery holds 480Wh. When you compare batteries, compare watt-hours, because voltage on its own does not tell you how long the bike will run."]},{"h":"Safe charging habits at home","ul":["Charge on a hard, non-flammable surface.","Keep the charger and battery away from curtains, beds and fuel.","Fit a smoke alarm near where you charge.","Do not charge a damaged battery.","Use the charger that came with the battery."]},{"h":"Winter, summer and storage temperature","p":["Batteries work best at moderate temperatures. In cold weather, range falls and charging can be slower, so bring the battery to room temperature before charging. In hot weather, avoid leaving the battery in a closed car or in direct sun, because heat shortens its life and increases risk. Store the battery indoors, away from heaters and out of direct sunlight."]},{"h":"What to do if a battery is damaged","ul":["Stop using it and do not charge it.","Move it outside, away from anything flammable, if it is hot or smoking.","Contact the maker or a battery specialist.","Do not try to open or repair the pack yourself.","Dispose of dead batteries through a recycling drop-off, not the household bin."]},{"h":"Quick answers","ul":["**How long does an e-bike battery last?** Many last several years, but capacity falls gradually with use and age.","**Can I leave the battery on charge overnight?** Avoid it. Charge while you are awake and nearby, and unplug when full.","**Can I buy a battery from a different brand?** Only if the voltage, connector and communication system match your bike. Check with the seller.","**Can I fly with an e-bike battery?** Airlines restrict lithium batteries, so check the airline’s rules before you travel."]},{"h":"Buying a replacement","p":["Match the voltage, connector and size to your bike. See the [batteries, chargers and electronics](/shop/batteries-chargers-and-electronics/) category, and read the [conversion kit guide](/blog/e-bike-conversion-kits-explained/) if you are building a kit."]},{"h":"Quick recap","p":["In this guide we covered charging; storage and winter care; getting more life from a battery; shipping lithium batteries; how lithium-ion batteries work, in plain language; and what affects how long a battery lasts. Come back to the section you need when you are deciding, and use the related categories and guides linked above to compare real products in Australian dollars. If you would like help applying any of this to your own bike, your budget and where you ride, message the team on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 or use the contact page, and we will reply with clear, honest advice."]}],"faq":[{"q":"What is the difference between 48V and 72V e-bike batteries?","a":"Higher voltage delivers power more efficiently to a compatible motor and controller, but the system must be designed for it. Never swap voltages without confirming the controller and motor support it. See the batteries, chargers & electronics range for current models and prices."},{"q":"How should I store an e-bike battery?","a":"Store it in a cool, dry place away from flammable items, partly charged if left for long periods. Do not leave it on charge unattended or store it in extreme heat. Browse the batteries, chargers & electronics category to compare models side by side."},{"q":"What batteries and chargers does Torque E-Bikes sell?","a":"The batteries, chargers and electronics category lists batteries, chargers, controllers and wiring harnesses for several bikes. Each page names the models it fits. The team can confirm fitment or availability on WhatsApp. Prices are in Australian dollars and each product page lists its specifications."},{"q":"Should I buy a bigger battery or a second battery?","a":"A bigger battery adds range in one pack but adds weight. A second battery lets you swap and keep riding, and spreads wear. Choose based on how long you ride and whether your bike supports two packs. Browse the batteries, chargers & electronics category to compare models side by side."},{"q":"Where does Torque E-Bikes list e-bike batteries and chargers?","a":"Torque E-Bikes lists e-bike batteries and chargers in the batteries, chargers & electronics category, with the specification and the price in Australian dollars on each product page. The team can confirm fitment, stock or freight on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 before you order."}],"keywords":{"primary":"e-bike batteries guide","supporting":["e-bike batteries guide","how to choose e-bike batteries","e-bike batteries explained","e-bike batteries for beginners","e-bike batteries tips"]},"words":1317},"kids-electric-dirt-bikes":{"hook":"A kids’ electric dirt bike is a small battery-powered off-road bike with limited power. Match the bike to your child’s height, ability and the manufacturer’s age guidance.","intro":["A kids’ electric dirt bike is a small battery-powered off-road bike for young riders. It is quiet, easy to start and has no clutch, which many children find less intimidating than a petrol bike.","Choosing the right one depends on your child’s size and ability more than their age. These bikes are for off-road and private property use only in Australia."],"sections":[{"h":"Age, height and ability","p":["Age is a poor guide on its own. Check the rider height and weight range on the product page, and think about your child’s confidence and coordination. A bike that is too big or too powerful is unsafe."]},{"h":"Power limiter and speed settings","p":["Look for a bike with power modes or a speed limiter so you can start slowly and increase power as your child improves. Check that the brakes are easy for small hands to operate."]},{"h":"Safety gear","p":["A fitted helmet, goggles, gloves, boots and protective armour are essential every ride. See the [protective gear guide](/blog/helmets-and-protective-gear-for-e-bikes/) and browse [helmets, gloves and protection](/shop/helmets-gloves-and-protection/)."]},{"h":"Balance bike or mini dirt bike first?","p":["An electric balance bike is a gentler first step for younger children learning balance. A mini dirt bike suits older, more confident riders. See the [electric balance bike guide](/blog/electric-balance-bikes/), and our [Yozma IN10 guide](/blog/yozma-in10-electric-mini-dirt-bike/) for one popular mini bike."]},{"h":"A safe set-up for a family riding area","ul":["A fenced or clearly bounded area away from roads, water and buildings.","A flat, firm surface free of rocks, holes and debris.","A clear entry and exit, with a place for adults to stand and watch.","First aid kit, water and shade nearby.","A rule that only one child rides at a time, and that others stay behind a line."]},{"h":"Teaching a child to fall and to recover","p":["Falls are part of learning, so teach children what to do. Release the throttle, let go of the bars if the bike is going to fall and keep arms and legs away from the bike. Practise at low speed on soft ground, and teach your child to stay down until an adult says it is safe to move. Check for injuries after a fall, and stop the session if your child is upset or hurt."]},{"h":"Choosing the bike’s power level as skills grow","p":["Start at the lowest setting and increase one step at a time, only after your child has shown they can control the bike at the current level. Many bikes allow you to limit top speed with a key or a controller setting. Keep records of your child’s progress, and do not be tempted to move up because other children are riding faster."]},{"h":"Costs to plan for when buying a kids’ electric bike","ul":["The bike and a spare battery if you want longer rides.","Protective gear in the correct size.","A suitable place to ride, which may mean venue fees.","Replacement tyres, brake pads and chains.","Transport and storage."]},{"h":"Choosing between brands and models for a child","p":["Kids’ electric dirt bikes come in a wide range of sizes and power levels. Compare the seat height, weight, speed limits and battery capacity. Check whether the bike has a speed limiter, a key or switch to reduce power and a lanyard kill switch. Look at the brakes and the ease of operating them. If the bike has pedals or a footrest, check the position and make sure it suits your child’s legs.","Check the quality of the tyres and suspension, because children ride in many conditions. Look at parts availability, because tyres, brake pads and chains wear. If you can, try the bike with your child before buying, or ask the seller for guidance on size."]},{"h":"Building confidence over time","p":["Confidence is built through small wins. Praise good habits such as releasing the throttle, braking smoothly and wearing gear without being asked. Increase the challenge slowly, and let your child lead when they feel ready. Avoid comparing children, because every child progresses at their own pace. If your child loses interest or becomes frightened, take a break and return later."]},{"h":"A parent’s checklist before every ride","ul":["Helmet and gear fitted and fastened.","Bike checked: tyres, brakes, chain, battery.","Riding area clear and boundaries agreed.","Water and first aid nearby.","Power mode set for the child’s skill level."]},{"h":"Choosing a venue for a child’s first rides","p":["A good venue is safe, flat and welcoming to children. Many motocross clubs have beginner areas or kids’ days, with supervision and rules. A private paddock can work if it is fenced and clear. Check that the venue accepts electric bikes, and ask about age rules, insurance and gear requirements. Visit without the bike first, if you can, to see the layout and meet the staff.","Look for a place where your child can ride without pressure from faster riders. Confidence grows best when the environment is calm and supportive."]},{"h":"A last word for parents","p":["The best thing you can do for a young rider is to be there: supervise, encourage and set a good example. Choose a bike that fits, buy gear that fits and pick a safe place to ride. Keep sessions fun and short, and stop before your child is tired. If you have questions, ask us. We are happy to help you choose a bike and gear that suit your child."]},{"h":"Learning to ride: a simple progression","ul":["Start in an open, flat area on a low-power setting.","Practise starting, stopping and turning slowly.","Add gentle slopes and obstacles as confidence grows.","Increase the power setting only when your child is ready."]},{"h":"Supervision and riding rules","p":["Always supervise. Set clear rules about where to ride, staying away from other people and stopping when told. Check the bike before every ride, and keep the area free of vehicles and animals."]},{"h":"Maintenance and growing out of the bike","p":["Check brakes, tyres, chain and the battery regularly. Children grow quickly, so check the rider size on the bike and plan the next step. A well-kept bike can often be sold when your child outgrows it."]},{"h":"What to look for in a first bike for a child","ul":["A speed limiter or low-power mode.","A seat height the child can reach with both feet.","Brakes with levers sized for small hands.","A throttle that returns to idle when released.","A kill switch or lanyard."]},{"h":"Teaching safe riding habits","p":["Start with the basics: how to start, stop and fall, and how to stay away from others. Set boundaries on where the child can ride and practise emergency stops. Supervise every ride, and stop sessions before the child gets tired."]},{"h":"Sizing a bike for a child","p":["Seat height and weight matter more than age. A child should be able to sit on the bike with both feet reaching the ground, or at least the balls of their feet, and operate the brake levers comfortably. Check the maker’s rider height and weight guidance and, if possible, have your child sit on the bike before you buy.","Avoid buying a bigger bike for your child to grow into. A bike that is too large is harder to control and can lead to falls. Buy for the size now, and plan to move up when the child outgrows it."]},{"h":"Choosing protective gear for children","ul":["A helmet that fits snugly and meets the standard for the type of riding.","Goggles to protect against dust and branches.","Gloves, long sleeves and long pants.","Boots with ankle support.","Chest and knee protection for off-road riding."]},{"h":"Quick answers","ul":["**What age can a child ride?** It depends on size and ability. Check the maker’s guidance and supervise.","**Do kids need a licence?** On private property there is generally no road licence, but check venue and landowner rules.","**Is electric safer than petrol?** It is simpler to operate, but it can still cause injury. Gear and supervision matter.","**How long should a first ride be?** Short sessions of 10 to 15 minutes are plenty at first."]},{"h":"Where children can ride","p":["Use private property or an approved off-road area, with adult supervision. Check your state’s rules and the land owner’s conditions, and read our [e-bike laws guide](/blog/off-road-vs-street-legal-australia/). Browse the [kids’ electric dirt and balance bikes](/shop/kids-electric-dirt-and-balance-bikes/) category."]},{"h":"Quick recap","p":["In this guide we covered age, height and ability; power limiter and speed settings; safety gear; balance bike or mini dirt bike first?; a safe set-up for a family riding area; and teaching a child to fall and to recover. Come back to the section you need when you are deciding, and use the related categories and guides linked above to compare real products in Australian dollars. If you would like help applying any of this to your own bike, your budget and where you ride, message the team on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 or use the contact page, and we will reply with clear, honest advice."]}],"faq":[{"q":"What age is a kids’ electric dirt bike suitable for?","a":"Suitable age depends on the model, your child’s height and ability, so follow the manufacturer’s guidance on each product page. Start with the smallest, lowest-power setting and supervise every ride. See the kids electric dirt & balance bikes range for current models and prices."},{"q":"Do kids need protective gear on an electric dirt bike?","a":"Yes. A fitted helmet, goggles, gloves, boots and body armour reduce injury risk. Check that gear is sized for children and replace helmets after a hard impact. Browse the kids electric dirt & balance bikes category to compare models side by side."},{"q":"Which kids’ electric bikes does Torque E-Bikes list?","a":"The kids’ electric dirt and balance bikes category lists mini dirt bikes and electric balance bikes from several brands, and the youth category lists youth e-bikes. Each page shows size and price. The team can confirm fitment or availability on WhatsApp."},{"q":"Should I buy a balance bike or a mini dirt bike first?","a":"An electric balance bike is a gentler first step for younger children learning balance and control. A mini dirt bike suits older children with more experience. Match the bike to your child’s size and confidence. See the kids electric dirt & balance bikes range for current models and prices."},{"q":"Where does Torque E-Bikes list kids’ electric bikes?","a":"Torque E-Bikes lists kids’ electric bikes in the kids electric dirt & balance bikes category, with the specification and the price in Australian dollars on each product page. The team can confirm fitment, stock or freight on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 before you order."}],"keywords":{"primary":"kids’ electric dirt bikes guide","supporting":["kids’ electric dirt bikes guide","how to choose kids’ electric dirt bikes","kids’ electric dirt bikes explained","kids’ electric dirt bikes for beginners","kids’ electric dirt bikes tips"]},"words":1333},"electric-vs-petrol-dirt-bikes":{"hook":"Electric dirt bikes are quieter and need less maintenance than petrol bikes, but ride time is limited by the battery. Petrol bikes refuel in minutes and suit very long days.","intro":["Electric and petrol dirt bikes both ride off-road, but they feel and work differently. Electric bikes are quiet, simple and instant, while petrol bikes offer long ride times and quick refuelling.","This guide compares them on the points that matter when choosing, without pretending one is right for everyone."],"sections":[{"h":"Noise and where you can ride","p":["Electric bikes are far quieter, which can make it easier to find places to ride and keep neighbours happy. Whichever you choose, ride only on private property or approved off-road areas and follow local rules."]},{"h":"Maintenance","p":["Electric bikes have no engine oil, air filter, spark plug or clutch to maintain, so routine servicing is simpler. They still need brake, chain, tyre and battery care: see our [chain care guide](/blog/chain-alignment-high-torque-motors/) and [battery care guide](/blog/electric-moto-maintenance-lithium-safety/)."]},{"h":"Power delivery and learning","p":["Electric motors deliver torque instantly and smoothly with no clutch or gear changes, which many beginners find easier. Instant power also needs respect, so start in a low mode and wear full protective gear."]},{"h":"Ride time and refuelling","p":["A petrol bike refuels in minutes, while an electric bike needs charging, which takes longer. Ride time on an electric bike depends on battery capacity and how hard you ride, so for long days consider a second battery or a bike with a larger pack."]},{"h":"Cost comparison in more detail","p":["A petrol dirt bike costs fuel, oil, filters, spark plugs and regular engine servicing. An electric dirt bike costs electricity, and needs less routine servicing, but its battery is expensive and wears over time. Over a few years the running costs of an electric bike are usually lower, but the purchase price can be higher, and a replacement battery is a significant cost. When comparing, add the purchase price, five years of running costs, protective gear and the likely resale value.","Resale values for electric bikes are still developing, because the technology changes quickly. Petrol bikes have a long history of resale. If you plan to sell in a few years, take that into account."]},{"h":"Learning curve and skills","p":["Electric bikes remove the clutch and gears, which simplifies riding for beginners. Power is instant and smooth, and it can be limited with riding modes. Petrol bikes teach clutch control and engine braking, skills that are useful on technical terrain. Riders who want to progress to racing or long enduro events may prefer to learn on a petrol bike, and riders who want to enjoy trails quietly may prefer electric."]},{"h":"Environmental and community factors","p":["Electric bikes produce no exhaust emissions at the point of use and are far quieter. Quiet matters for access: some venues and neighbours accept electric bikes more readily than petrol. Whichever you choose, take your rubbish home, stay on tracks and respect other users."]},{"h":"Which should you choose?","p":["If you ride short sessions near home, want low maintenance and low noise or are buying for a young rider, an electric bike is a strong choice. If you ride all day far from power, want the familiar feel of an engine or race, a petrol bike may suit you better. Many riders end up owning both. Browse [electric dirt bikes](/shop/electric-dirt-bikes/) and read the [buyer’s guide](/blog/best-electric-dirt-bikes-in-australia/)."]},{"h":"Maintenance differences you will notice","p":["A petrol bike needs regular oil changes, air filter cleaning, spark plug replacement, valve checks and carburettor or fuel injection care. An electric bike removes most of those jobs, but adds battery care, connector checks and, if relevant, motor and controller inspections. Both need chain, sprockets, brakes, tyres and suspension care. If you enjoy working on engines, a petrol bike gives you more to do. If you want to ride more and wrench less, electric has an advantage."]},{"h":"Noise, access and neighbours","p":["Noise limits restrict where petrol bikes can ride and at what hours. Electric bikes are quieter, which opens up more locations and lets you ride closer to home. They do not remove all rules, so check venue and landowner requirements, and always be considerate of neighbours, livestock and wildlife."]},{"h":"A simple way to decide","p":["If you cannot decide, imagine your best day of riding in a year’s time. Where are you, who is with you and how long are you riding? If you picture a few hours on private land close to home with family, an electric bike is likely to suit you. If you picture a long weekend away with no power, a petrol bike or a spare battery plan is more realistic. Try to ride both types before you buy, even for ten minutes, because feel matters more than any spec sheet.","Also think about what you enjoy. Some riders love the sound, smell and tinkering of petrol bikes. Others love the quiet, simplicity and instant response of electric. Neither is right, and many riders end up owning both."]},{"h":"A last word on electric and petrol","p":["Both kinds of bike offer a lot of fun. Choose the one that fits your riding, your location and your preferences. Whichever you choose, wear proper gear, ride in the right places and look after the bike. If you are leaning towards electric, we can help you choose a model."]},{"h":"Cost of ownership","p":["Electric bikes use electricity rather than fuel and need fewer routine services, but batteries are expensive and wear over time. Petrol bikes cost more to service and fuel but have cheaper replacement parts in many cases. Compare the whole running cost over a few years, not just the purchase price."]},{"h":"Power, weight and handling","p":["Electric bikes often feel lighter and more instant, while petrol bikes provide a familiar engine feel and engine braking. Weight distribution differs, with battery weight low and central on an electric bike. Try both if you can, because feel is personal."]},{"h":"Environment and neighbours","p":["Electric bikes produce no exhaust and little noise, which helps with neighbours and some riding areas. Whichever you choose, ride only where it is permitted and follow local rules."]},{"h":"Choosing based on how you ride","ul":["Short sessions near home: electric is quiet and convenient.","All-day riding far from power: petrol offers faster refuelling.","Learning or riding with kids: electric power delivery is easier to manage.","Racing: check which class and rules apply at your venue."]},{"h":"Environmental and neighbour considerations","p":["Electric bikes produce no exhaust and are much quieter, which helps with neighbours and some venues. Both types still need to be ridden only where allowed and with respect for others."]},{"h":"Practical differences you notice on the day","p":["Starting an electric bike is a matter of switching it on, while a petrol bike needs fuel, a kick or button start and sometimes a warm-up. An electric bike is almost silent, so you hear the tyres and the trail rather than an engine, which many riders enjoy. A petrol bike has a long-established parts and service network, while an electric bike needs fewer routine services.","On the trail, an electric bike’s torque is instant and smooth, which helps on technical climbs. A petrol bike’s engine braking and power band feel different, and many riders prefer them for long, fast days. Neither is better for everyone."]},{"h":"Questions to ask yourself before choosing","ul":["How long do I ride in a typical session?","Where will I ride, and is noise a problem there?","How much maintenance am I happy to do?","Who will ride the bike, and how experienced are they?","Where will I charge or refuel, and how do I transport the bike?"]},{"h":"Quick answers","ul":["**Which is cheaper to run?** Electric bikes have lower running costs, but batteries wear over time.","**Which is quieter?** Electric bikes, by a wide margin.","**Which has more range?** Petrol bikes refuel quickly, and electric bikes depend on battery size.","**Which is better for kids?** Electric is simpler to operate, with no clutch or gears."]},{"h":"Which is better for beginners?","p":["Electric bikes are easier to start and have smoother, clutch-free power, which many beginners prefer. Browse [electric dirt bikes](/shop/electric-dirt-bikes/) and read the [electric dirt bike guide](/blog/best-electric-dirt-bikes-in-australia/) to compare models."]},{"h":"Quick recap","p":["In this guide we covered noise and where you can ride; maintenance; power delivery and learning; ride time and refuelling; cost comparison in more detail; and learning curve and skills. Come back to the section you need when you are deciding, and use the related categories and guides linked above to compare real products in Australian dollars. If you would like help applying any of this to your own bike, your budget and where you ride, message the team on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 or use the contact page, and we will reply with clear, honest advice."]}],"faq":[{"q":"Are electric dirt bikes quieter than petrol bikes?","a":"Yes. Electric motors are much quieter than petrol engines, which can make them easier to ride near neighbours and on land with noise limits. Check local rules about riding on your property. See the electric dirt bikes range for current models and prices."},{"q":"Do electric dirt bikes need less maintenance?","a":"They have no engine oil, air filter or spark plugs to service, so routine maintenance is mostly chains, brakes, tyres and the battery. They still need regular checks and cleaning. Browse the electric dirt bikes category to compare models side by side."},{"q":"What electric dirt bikes does Torque E-Bikes sell?","a":"The electric dirt bikes category lists Sur-Ron, Talaria, Segway, KTM, Husqvarna and more, with prices in Australian dollars. Kids’ models are in a separate category. The team can confirm fitment or availability on WhatsApp. Prices are in Australian dollars and each product page lists its specifications."},{"q":"Is electric or petrol better for beginners?","a":"Electric bikes are easier to start and have smoother, clutch-free power, which many beginners prefer. Petrol bikes teach clutch and gear use. Either way, start slowly and wear full protective gear. Browse the electric dirt bikes category to compare models side by side."},{"q":"Where does Torque E-Bikes list electric dirt bikes and the petrol comparison?","a":"Torque E-Bikes lists electric dirt bikes and the petrol comparison in the electric dirt bikes category, with the specification and the price in Australian dollars on each product page. The team can confirm fitment, stock or freight on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 before you order."}],"keywords":{"primary":"electric vs petrol dirt bikes guide","supporting":["electric vs petrol dirt bikes guide","how to choose electric vs petrol dirt bikes","electric vs petrol dirt bikes explained","electric vs petrol dirt bikes for beginners","electric vs petrol dirt bikes tips"]},"words":1328},"electric-balance-bikes":{"hook":"An electric balance bike is a pedal-free kids’ bike with a small motor. It helps children learn balance and throttle control with limited power.","intro":["An electric balance bike is a pedal-free bike with a small motor, designed to help young children learn balance and throttle control. Many parents see it as a stepping stone to a mini dirt bike or a bicycle.","This guide explains who it suits and what to check before you buy."],"sections":[{"h":"How it differs from a pedal balance bike","p":["A pedal-free balance bike teaches balance very simply. An electric one adds a throttle, so the child also learns speed control. That makes supervision and protective gear more important."]},{"h":"Who it suits","ul":["Children who already balance well on a push bike.","Families riding on private property.","Children moving up towards mini dirt bikes."]},{"h":"What to check","ul":["Rider height and weight range.","A speed limiter or low-power mode.","Brakes that small hands can operate.","Battery run time and charging.","Tyre type and suspension."]},{"h":"Safety and where to ride","p":["Always supervise, use a fitted helmet and protective gear, and ride only on private property or an approved off-road area. Read the [kids’ electric dirt bike guide](/blog/kids-electric-dirt-bikes/) and the [e-bike laws guide](/blog/off-road-vs-street-legal-australia/)."]},{"h":"Understanding the controls on a child’s electric balance bike","p":["Electric balance bikes are simple, but each control matters. A twist or thumb throttle gives power, and it should spring back to zero when released. A brake lever, or two, stops the bike, and the levers should be sized for small hands. Some bikes have a power switch or key, which lets you turn off the motor when the child wants to push along. A speed mode switch limits top speed, and some bikes have a parent control.","Before the first ride, show the child each control while the bike is switched off, then switch it on and let them feel the throttle with the wheels off the ground. This helps them understand what each control does."]},{"h":"Choosing a bike by wheel size and weight","p":["Wheel size affects stability and handling. Larger wheels roll over bumps more easily, and smaller wheels suit smaller children. Weight matters because a child needs to lift or steady the bike. Choose the lightest bike that has the features you need, and check that the child can pick it up from the ground."]},{"h":"Tyres and terrain","p":["Air-filled tyres offer better grip and comfort than foam tyres, and are better on grass, gravel and uneven ground. Foam tyres do not puncture, which is convenient, but they can be slippery. Choose according to where your child will ride, and check tyre condition regularly."]},{"h":"Transitioning to the next bike","p":["When your child is steady at higher speeds, uses brakes smoothly and wants more challenge, it is time to consider a mini dirt bike or a pedal bike. Choose the next bike for the size and skills they have now. Keep the protective gear that still fits and replace what does not. See the [kids’ electric dirt bike guide](/blog/kids-electric-dirt-bikes/) and the [Yozma IN10 guide](/blog/yozma-in10-electric-mini-dirt-bike/)."]},{"h":"Choosing safety gear for a toddler or preschooler","p":["A child’s helmet must fit snugly and sit level, with straps adjusted so it does not move. Choose a helmet designed for the child’s head size, not an adult helmet. Add knee and elbow pads, gloves and closed shoes. A bright jacket helps adults see the child. Check gear before each ride and replace anything that is worn or too small."]},{"h":"Teaching a child to stop safely","p":["Stopping is the most important skill. Teach your child to release the throttle first and then squeeze the brake gently. Practise from a slow speed, and increase gradually. Use cones to mark a stopping point and praise when they stop in time. If your child grabs the brake too hard, practise with a lighter touch. Make it a game, and keep it positive.","If the bike has a parental speed limit or remote stop, learn how to use it, but do not rely on it. The child’s own control is the safest tool, and it builds confidence."]},{"h":"Why balance bikes help children later","p":["Balance skills learned on a balance bike carry over to pedal bikes, mini dirt bikes and other riding. Children who learn balance early often move to a pedal bike without training wheels and gain confidence quickly. The electric version adds throttle control, which helps when they move to motorised bikes later. It is also fun, which keeps children interested and active. Choose a bike that suits their size, and let them progress at their own pace."]},{"h":"Moving up to a pedal bike or mini dirt bike","p":["Once a child balances confidently, steers well and uses the brakes without thinking, they are ready to move on. A pedal bike builds fitness and road skills, while a mini dirt bike builds throttle control. Choose the next bike for the size and ability your child has now."]},{"h":"Care and charging","p":["Charge the battery with the supplied charger under adult supervision, and store the bike indoors at a moderate temperature. Check the tyres, brakes and bolts before each ride."]},{"h":"Safety gear for small riders","p":["A properly fitted helmet is essential, and gloves, knee and elbow pads add protection. Supervise every ride and ride on a safe surface away from traffic."]},{"h":"Choosing the right size","p":["Check the seat height and the child’s inside leg. They should be able to put both feet flat on the ground while seated. A bike that is too tall is unsafe, and one that is too small is uncomfortable. Many models have an adjustable seat, which helps as the child grows."]},{"h":"First rides and progression","ul":["Start at the lowest speed setting.","Practise on flat, soft ground.","Teach braking and stopping first.","Increase speed only when the child is confident."]},{"h":"How an electric balance bike teaches balance and throttle control","p":["A normal balance bike teaches a child to balance first, because they push along with their feet and lift them as they gain confidence. An electric balance bike adds a motor, so the child also learns to manage speed with a throttle. The sequence matters: balance first, then power. A child who can already glide on a push bike with their feet up will adapt to a motor quickly, while a child who still wobbles may be frightened by the first surge of power.","Look for a bike that lets a parent limit speed, ideally with a clearly marked low setting that keeps the bike at walking or jogging pace. Some bikes have a parent-controlled remote or a physical limiter. Whichever system you choose, test it yourself before your child rides, so you know how the bike responds to the throttle."]},{"h":"Safety rules for a first-time young rider","ul":["Choose a flat, open and safe area away from roads, driveways and water.","Fit a properly sized helmet every time, plus gloves and pads if the child will accept them.","Teach the child to release the throttle and stop before you teach them to go faster.","Keep rides short, because young children tire quickly and make mistakes when tired.","Check the bike before each ride: tyres, brakes, bolts and battery.","Stay close enough to reach the child, and agree on simple signals such as “stop” and “slow”."],"p":["The aim of the first few weeks is confidence, not distance. When your child is comfortable at low speed, you can move up a setting, but do it one step at a time, and go back a step if confidence drops."]},{"h":"Battery, charging and storage for a child’s bike","p":["Use the charger supplied with the bike, charge it where you can see it and unplug it when it is full. Do not let children charge the battery themselves. Store the bike indoors in a cool, dry place, and take the battery out if the manual recommends it. If the bike will not be used for a few weeks, leave the battery partly charged. See our [battery care guide](/blog/electric-moto-maintenance-lithium-safety/) for the full routine."]},{"h":"Browse the range","p":["See the [kids’ electric dirt and balance bikes](/shop/kids-electric-dirt-and-balance-bikes/) category and [helmets, gloves and protection](/shop/helmets-gloves-and-protection/)."]},{"h":"Quick recap","p":["In this guide we covered how it differs from a pedal balance bike; who it suits; what to check; safety and where to ride; understanding the controls on a child’s electric balance bike; and choosing a bike by wheel size and weight. Come back to the section you need when you are deciding, and use the related categories and guides linked above to compare real products in Australian dollars. If you would like help applying any of this to your own bike, your budget and where you ride, message the team on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 or use the contact page, and we will reply with clear, honest advice."]}],"faq":[{"q":"How is an electric balance bike different from a normal one?","a":"An electric balance bike adds a small motor and throttle to the pedal-free frame, so a child learns balance and throttle control together. Look for a speed limiter and good brakes. See the kids electric dirt & balance bikes range for current models and prices."},{"q":"What size electric balance bike does my child need?","a":"Match the wheel size and seat height to your child’s inseam so their feet touch the ground. Check each product’s recommended height range, and choose a size they can grow into slightly. Browse the kids electric dirt & balance bikes category to compare models side by side."},{"q":"Which electric balance bikes does Torque E-Bikes list?","a":"The kids’ electric dirt and balance bikes category lists electric balance bikes in several wheel sizes from multiple brands. Prices and size guidance are on each page. The team can confirm fitment or availability on WhatsApp. Prices are in Australian dollars and each product page lists its specifications."},{"q":"Is an electric balance bike better than a pedal one?","a":"A pedal-free balance bike teaches balance more simply. An electric one adds throttle control and is suited to riders ready for a bit more speed. Choose by your child’s age and confidence. Browse the kids electric dirt & balance bikes category to compare models side by side."},{"q":"Where does Torque E-Bikes list electric balance bikes?","a":"Torque E-Bikes lists electric balance bikes in the kids electric dirt & balance bikes category, with the specification and the price in Australian dollars on each product page. The team can confirm fitment, stock or freight on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 before you order."}],"keywords":{"primary":"electric balance bikes guide","supporting":["electric balance bikes guide","how to choose electric balance bikes","electric balance bikes explained","electric balance bikes for beginners","electric balance bikes tips"]},"words":1315},"talaria-x3-mx3-and-mx4":{"hook":"Talaria makes a range of electric off-road bikes with different frames, motors and batteries. Check each model’s official specification, because details change between model years.","intro":["Talaria makes several families of electric off-road bikes, and model names such as X3, MX3 and MX4 refer to different bikes with different frames, motors and batteries. People search for these names a lot, so it helps to know how they fit together before you choose.","A note before we start: the Talaria X3, MX3 and MX4 are not currently listed at Torque E-Bikes. This guide explains how to compare them and points you to the Talaria models we do list, and you can ask the team what can be sourced."],"sections":[{"h":"How the Talaria range is organised","p":["Talaria sells model families under names such as X3, MX3, MX4, Sting and XXX. They differ in frame size, motor power, battery capacity and intended rider. Because specifications change between model years, use the manufacturer’s official page as the source for exact figures."]},{"h":"What to compare between models","ul":["Motor power and the riding modes available.","Battery capacity and charging time.","Weight, seat height and suspension travel.","The rider size and experience the model suits.","Spare parts and upgrades available in Australia."]},{"h":"Talaria models we list","p":["The Talaria models in our catalogue include Sting and XXX bikes. See the full list on the [Talaria brand page](/brands/talaria/) and in the [electric dirt bikes](/shop/electric-dirt-bikes/) category. For a wider comparison read the [Talaria range guide](/blog/talaria-electric-dirt-bikes/) and the [Sur-Ron models guide](/blog/sur-ron-models-explained/)."]},{"h":"Off-road use and Australian rules","p":["Talaria bikes are for off-road and private property use only in Australia. They are not road legal. Read our [e-bike laws guide](/blog/off-road-vs-street-legal-australia/) and wear full protective gear: see the [gear guide](/blog/helmets-and-protective-gear-for-e-bikes/)."]},{"h":"How to research a model you cannot find locally","p":["If a model you want is not listed by a local seller, start with the maker’s own website and manual. Read the specification, the rider limits and the maintenance schedule. Search for owner reviews, but look at the date and the model year. Check whether the model is sold in Australia, whether it meets Australian electrical and battery freight rules and whether parts are available.","Be careful with grey imports. A bike that is cheaper because it was imported without support may cost more in the long run if parts or warranty are unavailable."]},{"h":"What to check on any electric dirt bike before you ride","ul":["Wheel bearings and axle nuts are tight.","Brake pads, rotors and levers are in good condition.","The chain and sprockets are aligned and not worn.","The battery is secure and the connectors are clean.","Tyres are properly inflated and undamaged.","Suspension moves smoothly and does not leak."]},{"h":"Choosing between lighter and heavier models","p":["A lighter bike is easier to learn on, easier to pick up after a fall and kinder on a smaller rider. A heavier bike with more suspension and a bigger battery can be more stable at speed and more capable in rough terrain. If you are unsure, start with the lighter option and move up when you know what you want from the bike."]},{"h":"Safety gear checklist for an adult rider","ul":["A motocross helmet that meets the standard.","Goggles and gloves.","Boots with ankle support.","Chest and back protection.","Knee and elbow guards.","Long pants and a long-sleeved jersey."],"p":["Read the [protective gear guide](/blog/helmets-and-protective-gear-for-e-bikes/) for help with fit and standards."]},{"h":"Frequently misunderstood points about Talaria models","ul":["Model names do not tell you power or battery size on their own.","Specifications can differ between markets and years.","A cheaper price can reflect missing accessories or limited support.","Reviews of one version do not always apply to another.","Parts compatibility can differ between models, even in the same family."],"p":["When in doubt, ask the seller to confirm the exact specification and what is included before you pay."]},{"h":"Ask the team","p":["If you are trying to decide between the Talaria models we list and one we do not, message the team on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 with your height, weight, riding experience and budget. We will suggest the model from our range that suits you, or tell you if we think a different bike would be better."]},{"h":"Planning your first electric dirt bike purchase","p":["Buying your first electric dirt bike involves more than choosing a model. Plan for a place to ride, protective gear for each rider, a way to transport the bike and a safe place to store and charge it. Think about whether you want a spare battery, a bike stand and tools. Budget for freight, because lithium batteries must travel with approved carriers, and for the first set of wear items such as tyres and brake pads.","Take your time to compare models, ask questions and make sure the bike suits the rider’s size. If you need help, message the team with your height, weight, experience and budget."]},{"h":"Understanding warranty and support for imported electric bikes","p":["Warranty on an electric bike covers specific parts for a set period, usually with conditions about use, maintenance and modifications. Read the warranty carefully: check what is covered, what is excluded and how to make a claim. Ask who will handle the claim, whether you must ship the bike and who pays freight. For imported bikes, find out whether the maker or the seller provides support in Australia.","Keep your receipt, serial numbers and records of maintenance. If you modify the bike, check how this affects the warranty. A clear warranty and a responsive seller are worth more than a small saving in price."]},{"h":"Keeping your expectations realistic","p":["Popular models attract hype, and online discussions can make a bike sound perfect. Remember that every bike has trade-offs, and that your experience will depend on your size, skill and where you ride. Read widely, talk to owners and test ride if you can. A bike that is not the most talked about may suit you better. Take your time, and buy with your own needs in mind."]},{"h":"How to check a model before you buy anywhere","ul":["Confirm the exact model name and year on the maker’s page.","Compare motor, battery and weight with other models you are considering.","Ask what is included, such as the charger and tools.","Check warranty terms and how claims are handled in Australia.","Make sure parts are available locally."]},{"h":"Why model names cause confusion","p":["The same model can have different versions, colours and market-specific changes. Check that the version you are looking at matches the one reviewed or discussed online, and ask the seller to confirm the specification."]},{"h":"Choosing an alternative from the range","p":["If you cannot find the model you want, think about what you were drawn to: lightweight handling, power, price or brand. Then compare the Talaria Sting and XXX models and other bikes in the [electric dirt bikes](/shop/electric-dirt-bikes/) category on those measures. The team can help narrow the choice."]},{"h":"What the model names usually tell you","p":["Model names such as X3, MX3 and MX4 are the maker’s way of separating its bikes by frame, power level and intended rider. They are not interchangeable, and the differences can be large. Names also change between model years, which is why reading the official specification matters more than relying on a nickname or a forum thread.","If you are comparing models, make a short table with motor power, battery capacity, weight, seat height, suspension and price. Fill it from the maker’s own information, and check each figure at least twice if it will decide the purchase."]},{"h":"Questions to ask the seller before you buy any Talaria","ul":["Is this the exact model and year I have been reading about?","What is included: charger, tools, spare parts and manual?","What is the warranty and how are claims handled in Australia?","Are parts and upgrades available locally?","What are the delivery times and freight costs for a bike with a lithium battery?"]},{"h":"Asking about a model that is not listed","p":["Message the team on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 with the model and year you want. We will tell you what is available, the price in Australian dollars and the freight cost before you pay."]},{"h":"Quick recap","p":["In this guide we covered how the Talaria range is organised; what to compare between models; talaria models we list; off-road use and Australian rules; how to research a model you cannot find locally; and what to check on any electric dirt bike before you ride. Come back to the section you need when you are deciding, and use the related categories and guides linked above to compare real products in Australian dollars. If you would like help applying any of this to your own bike, your budget and where you ride, message the team on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 or use the contact page, and we will reply with clear, honest advice."]}],"faq":[{"q":"What should I compare between Talaria models?","a":"Compare motor power, battery capacity, weight, suspension and the rider size each model suits. Use the maker’s official specification for each model year, because details change, and ask the team on WhatsApp if you want help comparing two bikes. See the talaria range for current models and prices."},{"q":"Can I ride a Talaria X3, MX3 or MX4 on the road?","a":"No. Electric dirt bikes and e-bikes above 250W are for off-road and private property use only, and are not road legal or registrable. Check your state or territory road authority and wear full protective gear whenever you ride. Browse the talaria category to compare models side by side."},{"q":"How is the Talaria range organised?","a":"Talaria sells several model families with different frames and power levels, and names such as X3, MX3, MX4, Sting and XXX refer to different bikes. Check the official page for each model before comparing prices or parts. Message the team on WhatsApp if you want advice on the talaria range."},{"q":"Does Torque E-Bikes list the Talaria X3, MX3 or MX4?","a":"The Talaria hub lists the Talaria models currently in the catalogue, including Sting and XXX models, and the X3, MX3 and MX4 are not among them at the time of writing. If the model you want is not listed, message the team and they will tell you what can be sourced and when."},{"q":"Can I ask Torque E-Bikes about a Talaria model that is not listed?","a":"Yes. Message the team on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 or use the contact form with the model and year. They will confirm what is available, the price in Australian dollars and the freight cost before you pay. Prices are in Australian dollars and each product page lists its specifications."}],"keywords":{"primary":"talaria x3","supporting":["talaria x3 pro","talaria mx4","talaria mx3","talaria x3 australia","talaria 3x"]},"words":1317},"yozma-in10-electric-mini-dirt-bike":{"hook":"The Yozma IN10 is an electric mini dirt bike for young riders. Check the rider height and age guidance, wear full protective gear and ride only on private property or approved off-road areas.","intro":["The Yozma IN10 is an electric mini dirt bike for young riders. It is quiet and easy to start, and it suits children who are ready to move up from a balance bike or a smaller bike.","This guide explains who it suits, how to set it up and how to ride it safely. It is for off-road and private property use only."],"sections":[{"h":"What the Yozma IN10 is","p":["The Yozma IN10 Electric Mini Dirt Bike is listed in our [kids’ electric dirt and balance bikes](/shop/kids-electric-dirt-and-balance-bikes/) and [electric dirt bikes](/shop/electric-dirt-bikes/) range. Open the [Yozma IN10 product page](/shop/electric-dirt-bikes/yozma-in-10-electric-mini-dirt-bike/) for its specification and price in Australian dollars."]},{"h":"Who it suits","p":["Suitability depends on your child’s height, weight and ability, not age alone. Check the rider size on the product page, and the manufacturer’s manual. A child should be able to reach the ground and operate the brakes and throttle comfortably."]},{"h":"Setup, charging and first ride","ul":["Read the manual and check every bolt and fitting before the first ride.","Charge the battery with the supplied charger, in a ventilated spot, and never leave it charging unattended.","Start in a low-power mode in an open, flat area.","Check brakes, tyres and chain before each ride."]},{"h":"Protective gear","p":["A fitted helmet, goggles, gloves, boots and armour are essential every ride. See the [protective gear guide](/blog/helmets-and-protective-gear-for-e-bikes/) and browse [helmets, gloves and protection](/shop/helmets-gloves-and-protection/)."]},{"h":"What “mini dirt bike” means for a first-time family buyer","p":["A mini dirt bike is a small off-road motorcycle sized for children and smaller teenagers. Electric versions replace the petrol engine with a motor and battery, which makes them quieter, easier to start and simpler to maintain. For families, that means less noise for neighbours, no fuel to store and fewer moving parts to go wrong. For children, it means they can concentrate on steering, balance and throttle control rather than clutches and gears.","The trade-offs are weight, because batteries are heavy, and ride time, because a battery runs down. A typical session for a young rider is short anyway, so many families find battery life is not a problem. If you plan long days, think about a spare battery or a break for charging."]},{"h":"A first-day plan for a new rider","ul":["Read the manual together, and show your child the controls with the bike switched off.","Check that the helmet and gear fit properly before the child sits on the bike.","Start in a flat, open, safe area on the lowest power setting.","Practise starting, stopping and turning at walking pace.","Add one new skill each session, such as wider turns or gentle slopes.","End each session before your child gets tired or frustrated."]},{"h":"Teaching throttle control and braking","p":["Throttle control is the main new skill. Teach your child to roll the throttle on gradually and release it fully to slow down. Practise stopping by releasing the throttle and squeezing both brakes smoothly, in a straight line. Use markers such as cones to teach a stopping point. If the bike has a lanyard kill switch, teach your child why it is attached and to clip it on every time.","Avoid practising on loose gravel or wet grass at first, because those surfaces are slippery. Move to varied terrain only when your child is confident on flat, firm ground."]},{"h":"When your child outgrows the bike","p":["Growth spurts are quick. Check the fit every few months: can your child reach the ground, operate the levers and sit comfortably with a slight bend in the knees? When the answer is no, it is time to move up. A bike that is too small makes the rider cramped and less in control, and it may be unsafe. Sell or pass on the bike while it is in good condition, and keep the protective gear in line with the new size."]},{"h":"Questions parents ask about electric mini bikes","p":["Parents often ask whether an electric mini bike is safer than a petrol one. The honest answer is that it is simpler, but not risk-free. There is no clutch to stall or gear to miss, and the bike is quiet enough for you to talk to your child while they ride, which helps with coaching. However, electric torque is instant, so a nervous twist of the throttle can launch the bike. Speed limiting, supervision and good gear matter more than the power source.","Another common question is how long the bike lasts on a charge. That depends on battery size, rider weight and terrain. For a young rider doing short sessions, a charge usually covers a few sessions. If you ride for hours, a spare battery or a break for charging is wise. Ask the seller for the manufacturer’s figures and treat them as best-case."]},{"h":"Keeping records and planning the next step","p":["Keep the receipt, serial number and manual in one place. Note the date you first charge the battery, and keep a simple log of rides and any problems. When your child grows out of the bike, these records help you sell it and show the next owner that it has been looked after. Think ahead: a child who enjoys riding will want a bigger bike in a year or two, so plan your gear purchases to carry over where you can, such as goggles and gloves."]},{"h":"Safety checks before every ride","ul":["Check tyres for pressure and damage.","Test both brakes before moving.","Check the chain and bolts.","Check the battery is secure and charged.","Make sure your child is wearing full gear."]},{"h":"Growing with the bike","p":["Rider size is the limit, not age. When your child reaches the maximum height or weight, move up to a larger bike. See the [kids’ electric dirt bikes](/shop/kids-electric-dirt-and-balance-bikes/) range."]},{"h":"Choosing protective gear for a young rider","ul":["A helmet that fits snugly and meets the standard.","Goggles for dust and sun.","Gloves, boots and long sleeves.","Knee and elbow guards and a chest protector."]},{"h":"Questions to ask before ordering","ul":["What is the maximum rider weight and height?","Does it have speed modes?","What is the battery run time?","What does the warranty cover?"]},{"h":"Understanding the Yozma IN10 as a first off-road bike","p":["Electric mini dirt bikes like the Yozma IN10 are designed to make off-road riding less intimidating for young riders. There is no clutch to stall, no fuel to mix and no loud engine to startle a nervous child. The controls are simple: a throttle, a brake and, on some models, a speed setting. That simplicity helps children focus on balance, steering and body position.","The bike is still a motorised vehicle, and a young rider will push its limits quickly. Start in a low-power setting, keep sessions short and make sure the child understands that the throttle must be released to slow down. If the bike has a lanyard or kill switch, use it so the motor stops if the child falls off."]},{"h":"Choosing where and how to ride","ul":["A fenced yard, a paddock or a motocross track with permission from the owner.","Flat, firm ground with no steep slopes or deep ruts for the first sessions.","No traffic, animals or people in the riding area.","A clear boundary the child understands, and an adult who can see the whole area."],"p":["Do not ride on public roads, footpaths or in parks. The bike is for private property and approved off-road areas only. Our [e-bike laws guide](/blog/off-road-vs-street-legal-australia/) explains why."]},{"h":"Looking after the bike between rides","p":["Wipe dirt off the frame and wheels, check the tyres and chain, and look for loose bolts. Keep the battery dry and charge it after riding. Replace worn brake pads and tyres early, because a child will not tell you when the brakes are getting weak. Store the bike out of the sun and away from moisture."]},{"h":"Where to ride","p":["Use private property or an approved off-road area with adult supervision. Read the [kids’ electric dirt bike guide](/blog/kids-electric-dirt-bikes/) and the [e-bike laws guide](/blog/off-road-vs-street-legal-australia/) for where and how children can ride."]},{"h":"Quick recap","p":["In this guide we covered what the Yozma IN10 is; who it suits; setup, charging and first ride; protective gear; what “mini dirt bike” means for a first-time family buyer; and a first-day plan for a new rider. Come back to the section you need when you are deciding, and use the related categories and guides linked above to compare real products in Australian dollars. If you would like help applying any of this to your own bike, your budget and where you ride, message the team on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 or use the contact page, and we will reply with clear, honest advice."]}],"faq":[{"q":"What age is the Yozma IN10 suitable for?","a":"Suitability depends on the child’s height, ability and the manufacturer’s guidance rather than age alone. Check the rider size on the product page, supervise every ride, and fit a helmet and protective gear before the first ride. See the kids electric dirt & balance bikes range for current models and prices."},{"q":"Can the Yozma IN10 be ridden on the road?","a":"No. Electric mini dirt bikes are for off-road and private property use only in Australia, and they are not road legal or registrable. Use a fenced yard, a private property or an approved off-road area. Browse the kids electric dirt & balance bikes category to compare models side by side."},{"q":"How should I charge and store the Yozma IN10 battery?","a":"Use the supplied charger, charge in a ventilated spot and never leave it charging unattended. Store the bike somewhere cool and dry, away from flammable items, and follow the manufacturer’s manual for battery care. Message the team on WhatsApp if you want advice on the kids electric dirt & balance bikes range."},{"q":"Does Torque E-Bikes list the Yozma IN10?","a":"Yes. The Yozma IN10 Electric Mini Dirt Bike is listed with its price in Australian dollars on its product page, and the team can confirm stock and freight on WhatsApp before you order. Dirt bikes are for off-road use only."},{"q":"Where can I see other kids’ electric bikes at Torque E-Bikes?","a":"The kids’ electric dirt and balance bikes category lists mini dirt bikes and electric balance bikes, and the kids and youth e-bikes category lists larger models. Each product page shows the specification and price. Prices are in Australian dollars and each product page lists its specifications."}],"keywords":{"primary":"yozma in10","supporting":["yozma in 10 australia","yozma in10 electric bike","yozma in 10 electric bike","yozma in 10 pro electric dirt bike","yozma in10 guide"]},"words":1326},"dirodi-rover-gen-6":{"hook":"The DiroDi Rover is a fat-tyre electric bike. Check the motor, battery and rider limits on the product page, and confirm that the bike meets the rules where you plan to ride.","intro":["The DiroDi Rover is a fat-tyre electric bike, and the Gen 6 is one of the versions people search for most. Wide tyres give grip on sand, gravel and rough tracks, and the motor and battery make longer rides easier.","This guide explains what to check before you buy, how range works and where the Rover fits in the fat-tyre range."],"sections":[{"h":"What to check on a DiroDi Rover","ul":["Motor power and whether it is pedal assist.","Battery capacity, which decides range.","Weight and the rider limit.","Brakes, tyres and frame size."],"p":["See the [DiroDi Rover Gen 6 product page](/shop/fat-tyre-and-scrambler-e-bikes/dirodi-rover-gen6/) if it is available, and the [DiroDi brand page](/brands/dirodi/) for every DiroDi model we list, including the Rover Pro."]},{"h":"Range on a fat-tyre bike","p":["Range depends on battery capacity, rider weight, terrain, tyre pressure and assist level, so treat the maker’s figure as a best case. Soft sand and hills reduce it, and a larger battery adds range and weight. Our [tyre guide](/blog/e-bike-tyres-and-tubes/) explains how tyre pressure affects it."]},{"h":"Rules for where you can ride","p":["Rules depend on the bike’s motor power and your state. Pedal-assist bikes at 250W or less can use public roads, while more powerful bikes are for off-road and private property use only. Check the product page and read the [e-bike laws guide](/blog/off-road-vs-street-legal-australia/)."]},{"h":"Accessories and upgrades","p":["Lights, a rack and a good lock make a Rover more useful for commuting and touring. See the [lights guide](/blog/e-bike-lights/), the [racks guide](/blog/bike-racks-baskets-and-trailers/) and the [lock guide](/blog/how-to-lock-an-e-bike/)."]},{"h":"Understanding the Rover range","p":["Torque E-Bikes lists three DiroDi Rover models: the Rover Gen 6, the Rover Pro and the Rover Plus Gen 6. They share a fat-tyre approach but differ in specifications and price. Open each product page, compare the motor, battery and components and decide which fits your riding. The [DiroDi brand page](/brands/dirodi/) shows all three together.","When comparing, note the battery capacity, because it determines range, and the motor rating, because it determines climbing ability and the rules that apply. Check the brakes and the frame weight limit, and think about whether you will carry loads."]},{"h":"Planning rides on a fat-tyre bike","p":["Fat-tyre bikes suit rides on beaches, fire trails, bush tracks and gravel roads. Plan your route and check that e-bikes are allowed. Take water, a repair kit and a charged phone, and consider the battery range with a margin. Soft surfaces and hills drain a battery faster, and wind affects range on exposed routes.","Ride within your limits and with others when exploring new areas. Tell someone your route, and know where you can stop or turn back if the battery runs low."]},{"h":"Keeping a Rover in good condition","ul":["Check tyre pressure before each ride.","Wipe the bike down after dusty or sandy rides.","Lubricate the chain regularly.","Check brake pads and rotors for wear and grit.","Keep the battery charged and stored correctly.","Check bolts, spokes and bearings at regular intervals."]},{"h":"Accessories to consider","p":["A good light set, a rear rack, wide mudguards, a bell and a strong lock make the bike more practical. If you carry gear, choose panniers that fit the rack and keep the load low. A pump and a spare tube sized for wide tyres are essential. See the guides to [lights](/blog/e-bike-lights/), [racks](/blog/bike-racks-baskets-and-trailers/) and [locks](/blog/how-to-lock-an-e-bike/)."]},{"h":"Who we think the Rover Gen 6 suits best","p":["The Rover suits riders who want a comfortable, stable bike for varied surfaces and who are happy with a heavier machine. It makes sense for riders who live near beaches or trails, for those who want a bike that will handle gravel roads and for riders who like the look and feel of a fat-tyre bike. It is less suited to riders who need a very light bike to lift or who ride mostly on smooth roads where a slimmer-tyre e-bike would be more efficient.","If you are unsure, tell us where you plan to ride and how far, and we will say whether we think a Rover or a different type of bike would suit you better."]},{"h":"Seasonal and regional riding tips for a Rover","p":["In hot northern conditions, ride in the cooler parts of the day, carry water and keep the battery out of direct sun when parked. Check tyre pressure often, because heat raises it. On coastal routes, rinse salt from the frame, drivetrain and brake rotors, and dry the bike. In cooler southern areas, keep the battery warm before riding and charging, and check tyre pressure when the temperature drops.","In all regions, check weather and trail conditions before you go. Wet sand, mud and steep gravel need slower speeds and gentler braking. Respect local rules about e-bikes on beaches, trails and shared paths, and keep to marked tracks to protect the environment."]},{"h":"A note on service and spare parts","p":["Every e-bike needs parts over time: tyres, tubes, brake pads, chains and sometimes a charger or battery. Ask how to get them, what they cost and how long delivery takes. Keep a record of the sizes and types used on your bike, and consider buying a few spares at the start. Learning basic maintenance helps, and so does a good relationship with a local bike shop.","If you have a problem that you cannot solve, contact us with photos and a description. We will help you work out what is needed."]},{"h":"A last word on the DiroDi Rover","p":["The Rover offers a comfortable way to ride rough and soft ground. Check the specification, choose the model that suits you and look after the bike. Use lights, a lock and a helmet, and respect the rules. If you want advice on which Rover to choose, we are glad to help."]},{"h":"Setting up a new bike","ul":["Read the manual and check every bolt.","Set tyre pressure for your surface.","Adjust the saddle and handlebars to fit you.","Charge the battery fully before the first ride.","Test the brakes in a safe area."]},{"h":"Maintenance for wide-tyre e-bikes","p":["Check tyre pressure often, clean the drivetrain after sandy or muddy rides, and check brake pads regularly. Rinse salt from the bike after beach riding, avoiding high-pressure water on electrical parts."]},{"h":"Choosing between Rover models","p":["The Rover range includes several versions with different power and battery options. Compare motor power, battery capacity and weight on the product pages, and check which version suits your riding and the rules in your area. The Rover Pro and Rover Plus Gen 6 are listed alongside the Gen 6 on the [DiroDi brand page](/brands/dirodi/)."]},{"h":"Who the DiroDi Rover suits","p":["Fat-tyre e-bikes like the Rover suit riders who want stability and comfort on rough or soft surfaces, and who value a relaxed ride over a light, sporty feel. The wide tyres soak up bumps and give grip on gravel, sand and dirt tracks, and the motor makes up for the extra rolling resistance. They are popular for beach paths, fire trails, farm tracks and general leisure riding.","They are heavier than ordinary e-bikes and take more room to store and transport. If you need to lift the bike onto a rack or carry it up steps, check the weight on the product page and think about whether you can handle it. A removable battery can reduce the weight when lifting."]},{"h":"Things to check when the bike arrives","ul":["Look for transport damage on the frame, wheels, display and battery.","Check that bolts, pedals, handlebars and brakes are tight.","Set tyre pressure for your surface and weight.","Charge the battery fully before the first ride, using the supplied charger.","Test the brakes in a safe area before you ride."]},{"h":"Maintenance for sandy and wet conditions","p":["Sand and salt are hard on drivetrains and bearings. After riding on a beach or in wet conditions, rinse the frame gently with fresh water (avoid direct high-pressure spray on the motor, battery and display), dry the bike and lubricate the chain. Check the brake pads and rotors for grit, and store the bike somewhere dry."]},{"h":"Other fat-tyre e-bikes","p":["Compare the Rover with other bikes in the [fat-tyre and scrambler e-bikes](/shop/fat-tyre-and-scrambler-e-bikes/) category, and read the [fat-tyre e-bike guide](/blog/fat-tyre-e-bikes/)."]},{"h":"Quick recap","p":["In this guide we covered what to check on a DiroDi Rover; range on a fat-tyre bike; rules for where you can ride; accessories and upgrades; understanding the Rover range; and planning rides on a fat-tyre bike. Come back to the section you need when you are deciding, and use the related categories and guides linked above to compare real products in Australian dollars. If you would like help applying any of this to your own bike, your budget and where you ride, message the team on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 or use the contact page, and we will reply with clear, honest advice."]}],"faq":[{"q":"What should I check before buying a DiroDi Rover Gen 6?","a":"Check the motor power, battery capacity, weight and rider limit on the product page, then match them to where you will ride. Ask the team on WhatsApp if you want the specification explained before you order. See the dirodi range for current models and prices."},{"q":"Is the DiroDi Rover Gen 6 legal to ride on roads?","a":"Rules depend on the bike’s motor power and your state. Pedal-assist e-bikes that meet the 250W standard can use public roads, while higher-powered bikes are for off-road and private property use only. Check your state road authority. Browse the dirodi category to compare models side by side."},{"q":"How far can a fat-tyre e-bike go on one charge?","a":"Range depends on battery capacity, rider weight, terrain, tyre pressure and assist level, so treat the maker’s figure as a best case. Soft sand and hills reduce it, and a larger battery adds range and weight. Message the team on WhatsApp if you want advice on the dirodi range."},{"q":"Which DiroDi bikes does Torque E-Bikes list?","a":"The DiroDi hub lists the DiroDi Rover models in the catalogue, including the Rover Gen 6 and Rover Pro. Each product page shows the specification and the price in Australian dollars, and freight is confirmed before payment. Prices are in Australian dollars and each product page lists its specifications."},{"q":"Where can I see other fat-tyre e-bikes at Torque E-Bikes?","a":"The fat-tyre and scrambler e-bikes category lists wide-tyre bikes from several brands. Open a product page for specifications and price, and message the team on WhatsApp if you want two models compared. Open the dirodi category to see everything that is currently in stock."}],"keywords":{"primary":"dirodi gen 6","supporting":["dirodi rover v6","dirodi rover plus gen 6","dirodi rover gen 3","dirodi rover pro 1000w","dirodi rover gen 5"]},"words":1344},"flight-risk-fr505-and-fr555":{"hook":"Flight Risk makes fat-tyre electric bikes in several models. Compare the motor, battery and frame on each product page, and check which models are listed before you decide.","intro":["Flight Risk makes fat-tyre electric bikes in several models, and the FR505 and FR555 are two of the names people search for. They are different bikes, so compare their specifications rather than relying on the name.","Torque E-Bikes lists the FR505, FR777 and FR888. The FR555 is not currently listed. This guide explains how to compare them and what to check."],"sections":[{"h":"The Flight Risk range","p":["Flight Risk sells a range of fat-tyre bikes with different motors, batteries and frames. See the models we list on the [Flight Risk brand page](/brands/flight-risk/), and the [Flight Risk FR505 product page](/shop/fat-tyre-and-scrambler-e-bikes/flight-risk-fr505/)."]},{"h":"Comparing the FR505 and FR555","p":["Compare motor power, battery capacity, weight, brakes and frame on the maker’s specification for each model year. Use the table on the maker’s page as the source, and be cautious about forum figures."],"ul":["Motor and drive type.","Battery capacity and range.","Weight and rider limit.","Brakes and suspension.","Frame size."]},{"h":"What to look for in a fat-tyre e-bike","p":["Check the tyre width, motor, battery, brakes and frame size, and think about the surface you ride on. Wide tyres suit sand, gravel and rough tracks but add weight, so match the bike to how you ride. Our [fat-tyre e-bike guide](/blog/fat-tyre-e-bikes/) covers this in more detail."]},{"h":"Rules and safe riding","p":["It depends on the model’s motor power and your state. Pedal-assist bikes at 250W or less can use public roads, while higher-powered bikes are off-road only. Read the [e-bike laws guide](/blog/off-road-vs-street-legal-australia/), wear a helmet, and see the [protective gear guide](/blog/helmets-and-protective-gear-for-e-bikes/)."]},{"h":"The Flight Risk models we list: how to think about them","p":["Torque E-Bikes lists the Flight Risk FR505, FR777 and FR888, and each has its own product page with the specification and price in Australian dollars. Rather than treating the numbers in the model names as a ranking, read the specifications and compare the motor, battery, weight and components. The right model for you depends on your weight, your terrain and how far you ride.","If you are choosing between two listed models, put them side by side and note the differences in battery capacity, motor rating and brake type. If one is clearly stronger in the areas that matter to you, that is your answer. If they are close, think about price and warranty. Open the [Flight Risk brand page](/brands/flight-risk/) to see every model together."]},{"h":"What a fat-tyre bike changes about everyday riding","p":["A fat-tyre bike changes how you plan rides. You can take sand and gravel tracks that would stop a normal bike, and you can ride in conditions where traction matters. You also carry more weight, so you will notice the bike’s mass when you wheel it, lift it onto a rack or push it up a ramp. Think about storage and transport: a heavy bike on a rack needs a strong rack, and the bike’s width affects how it fits in a car or shed.","Rolling resistance is higher with wide tyres, so range on hard roads is usually lower than a slim-tyre e-bike with the same battery. Plan accordingly, and use a lower assist level on flat ground to stretch the battery."]},{"h":"Questions to ask the seller about any fat-tyre bike","ul":["What is the rated continuous power, and is it pedal-assist only or does it have a throttle?","What battery capacity does it have, and who makes the cells?","Which brakes and rotor sizes are fitted?","What is the frame material and the rider weight limit?","How long is the warranty, and how are claims handled?","Are replacement parts such as tyres, tubes and chargers available?"]},{"h":"Safety and legal checks before you ride","p":["Wear a helmet and gloves, and use lights if you ride in low light. Check that the bike’s power and throttle behaviour match the rules for where you plan to ride, and read our [e-bike laws guide](/blog/off-road-vs-street-legal-australia/). Check tyre pressure, brakes and bolts before each ride, and take the battery with you when you park."]},{"h":"A buyer’s checklist for Flight Risk bikes","ul":["Confirm the exact model and year, and the included accessories.","Check the battery capacity, charger and warranty.","Check the brakes, tyres and frame size.","Confirm delivery arrangements and timeframe.","Check the rules for where you plan to ride.","Ask about spare parts and service support."],"p":["Put these questions to us or to any seller. A clear, specific answer is a good sign."]},{"h":"Making the most of your first month","p":["In your first month, learn how the bike behaves in different assist levels and on different surfaces. Keep a record of battery use on regular rides, and adjust your routes accordingly. Check bolts and brakes after the first week, and adjust the saddle and bars until you are comfortable. Keep the bike clean, and lubricate the chain. Ride with friends if you can, and ask for tips from experienced fat-tyre riders.","If you notice anything unusual, such as a strange noise, a loose part or reduced range, check it promptly. Contact the seller if it might be a fault, and keep records and photos."]},{"h":"A note on honesty about models we do not list","p":["We want you to buy the bike that suits you, even if it is not ours. If you want a Flight Risk model that we do not list, such as the FR555, tell us and we will check whether we can source it. If we cannot, we will say so, and suggest an alternative from the range or point you to another option. We would rather lose a sale than sell you a bike that does not suit."]},{"h":"Questions to ask the seller","ul":["What are the exact motor power and battery capacity?","What is the model year and what is included?","What does the warranty cover?","Are spare parts available in Australia?","Is the bike pedal assist, throttle or both?"]},{"h":"Charging and care","p":["Use the supplied charger, charge in a ventilated spot and do not leave the battery charging unattended. Check the tyres, brakes and bolts regularly, and store the bike dry. See the [battery care guide](/blog/electric-moto-maintenance-lithium-safety/)."]},{"h":"Deciding between the Flight Risk models listed","p":["Torque E-Bikes lists the FR505, FR777 and FR888. Compare their specifications on the product pages, think about weight and range, and choose the one that suits how and where you ride. If you want a model that is not listed, ask the team what can be sourced."]},{"h":"Understanding what separates models in a fat-tyre range","p":["Within a single brand’s fat-tyre range, the differences usually come down to motor power, battery size, frame design and component quality. A model with a bigger battery will go further but weigh more. A model with a stronger motor will climb better but may be heavier and more expensive. Suspension, brakes and drivetrain components also vary, and affect both comfort and reliability.","Rather than choosing by model number, decide what matters to you most: range, climbing, weight, comfort or price. Then look at the specifications of each model and see which suits those priorities. Where a number is missing, ask the seller."]},{"h":"Getting the most from a fat-tyre e-bike","ul":["Set tyre pressure for the surface: lower for sand and snow, higher for hard roads.","Use a lower assist level on flat ground to extend range.","Keep the battery between about 20% and 80% for daily use if the maker recommends it.","Carry a pump and spare tube that suit the wide tyres.","Check bolts and brakes regularly, because heavier bikes stress components."]},{"h":"Rules and where to ride","p":["Check the motor’s power rating and whether the bike has a throttle. Pedal-assist bikes within the 250W limit can be used on roads and paths, subject to state rules, and more powerful bikes are for private property only. Read the [e-bike laws guide](/blog/off-road-vs-street-legal-australia/) and ask the team if you are unsure about a particular model."]},{"h":"Where to see them","p":["Browse the [fat-tyre and scrambler e-bikes](/shop/fat-tyre-and-scrambler-e-bikes/) category, or message the team on WhatsApp to ask about a model that is not listed."]},{"h":"Quick recap","p":["In this guide we covered the Flight Risk range; comparing the FR505 and FR555; what to look for in a fat-tyre e-bike; rules and safe riding; the Flight Risk models we list: how to think about them; and what a fat-tyre bike changes about everyday riding. Come back to the section you need when you are deciding, and use the related categories and guides linked above to compare real products in Australian dollars. If you would like help applying any of this to your own bike, your budget and where you ride, message the team on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 or use the contact page, and we will reply with clear, honest advice."]}],"faq":[{"q":"What is the difference between the Flight Risk FR505 and FR555?","a":"They are different models with different specifications, so compare motor power, battery capacity, weight and frame on the maker’s page for each model year. Do not rely on the name alone, and ask the team on WhatsApp for help comparing."},{"q":"Are Flight Risk e-bikes road legal in Australia?","a":"It depends on the model’s motor power and your state. Pedal-assist bikes at 250W or less can use public roads, while higher-powered bikes are off-road only. Check the product page and your state road authority before riding. See the flight risk range for current models and prices."},{"q":"What should I look for in a fat-tyre e-bike?","a":"Check the tyre width, motor, battery, brakes and frame size, and think about the surface you ride on. Wide tyres suit sand, gravel and rough tracks but add weight, so match the bike to how you ride. Browse the flight risk category to compare models side by side."},{"q":"Which Flight Risk models does Torque E-Bikes list?","a":"The Flight Risk hub lists the models currently in the catalogue, including the FR505, FR777 and FR888. If you want a model that is not listed, message the team and they will check availability. The team can confirm fitment or availability on WhatsApp."},{"q":"Where can I find the Flight Risk FR505 at Torque E-Bikes?","a":"The Flight Risk FR505 has its own product page with the specification and the price in Australian dollars. You can also reach it through the Flight Risk hub or the fat-tyre e-bikes category. Prices are in Australian dollars and each product page lists its specifications."}],"keywords":{"primary":"flight risk 505","supporting":["flight risk 555","flight risk fr555","flight risk 777","flight risk 505 guide","how to choose flight risk 505"]},"words":1315},"e-bike-tyres-and-tubes":{"hook":"The size and valve type are printed on the tyre sidewall. Match them when you buy a replacement tyre or tube, and replace tyres that are cracked, worn flat or repeatedly puncturing.","intro":["Tyres are the only thing connecting your bike to the ground, and e-bike tyres carry extra weight and torque. Matching the size and valve type, and keeping the pressure right, improves safety, grip and range.","This guide covers how to read tyre sizes, choose tubes, set pressure and spot when to replace them."],"sections":[{"h":"How to read an e-bike tyre size","p":["The size is printed on the sidewall, for example 26 x 4.0 or 700 x 40c. The first number is the approximate diameter and the second is the width. You may also see an ETRTO number, such as 40-622, which gives width and bead diameter. Match both the diameter and the width when you replace a tyre."]},{"h":"Tubes and valve types","p":["Tubes have either a Schrader valve (like a car tyre) or a Presta valve (thinner, with a lock nut). Your rim has a hole drilled for one of them, so use the matching valve type, and choose a tube sized for your tyre width. Tubeless tyres use sealant instead of a tube and need tubeless-ready rims."]},{"h":"Tyre pressure","p":["Use the pressure range printed on the tyre sidewall and adjust within it for load and surface. Lower pressure grips better on rough ground, while higher pressure rolls easier on smooth roads and usually improves battery range. Check pressure before each ride with a gauge."]},{"h":"Tyre types for e-bikes","ul":["Commuter tyres with puncture protection.","Road and gravel tyres for sealed and unsealed surfaces.","Knobbly tyres for off-road riding.","Fat tyres for sand and soft ground."]},{"h":"When to replace a tyre","p":["Replace a tyre when the tread is worn flat, the sidewall is cracked or cut, or it punctures repeatedly. E-bikes are heavier and faster than normal bikes, so worn tyres reduce braking grip and safety."]},{"h":"Choosing tyres for different e-bike types","p":["Commuter e-bikes suit tyres with puncture protection, a slight tread and a moderate width. E-mountain bikes use knobbly tyres with strong sidewalls, and many riders choose a wider tyre for grip and comfort. Fat-tyre bikes use very wide tyres designed for low pressure. Folding bikes use small wheels, so look for durable tyres with good puncture protection. Cargo bikes need tyres with a high load rating.","Check the tyre’s maximum pressure and load rating, because e-bikes are heavy. Choose tyres from reputable brands and check reviews for the specific model."]},{"h":"Changing a tyre step by step","p":["Deflate the tyre and push the bead into the centre of the rim. Use tyre levers to lift one side of the tyre over the rim, and remove the tube. Check the tyre and rim for debris, then fit a new tube with a little air, tuck it into the tyre and refit the tyre onto the rim, working the last section with your hands rather than levers. Check that the tube is not pinched, inflate to the right pressure and refit the wheel.","Take care with hub-motor wheels: disconnect the motor cable, support the bike and follow the instructions for removing and refitting the wheel, including torque arms and axle nuts."]},{"h":"Common tyre problems and causes","ul":["**Repeated flats:** debris in the tyre, a poorly seated tube or a rim tape fault.","**Bulges in the sidewall:** a damaged tyre that must be replaced.","**Cracks:** age or sun damage.","**Uneven wear:** wrong pressure or misaligned wheel.","**Slipping on corners:** tyres too hard or worn."]},{"h":"Tyre pressure and range","p":["Higher pressure reduces rolling resistance and increases range on smooth roads, and lower pressure improves comfort and grip. Choose a pressure in the middle of the range for general use, and adjust for load and surface. Check pressure weekly, because tyres lose air slowly."]},{"h":"Choosing tyres for wet weather and winter","p":["Wet roads call for tyres with softer compounds and a tread pattern that clears water. Lower pressures within the allowed range can improve grip. Avoid riding on worn tyres in the wet, and take extra care with painted lines, drain covers and metal surfaces. In cold weather, rubber stiffens, so allow longer braking distances. Check pressure often, because it drops with temperature."]},{"h":"Understanding tyre compounds, casings and tread","p":["Tyre compound is the rubber mix. Softer compounds grip better but wear faster, and harder compounds last longer but grip less. Casing is the structure under the tread: thicker casings resist cuts and carry heavier loads, and thinner casings are lighter and more supple. Tread is the pattern: smooth for roads, semi-slick for mixed use and knobbly for off-road. Choose according to your priorities, and remember that e-bikes are heavy, so durability matters.","Check the maker’s information on load rating and the recommended pressure range, and choose tyres that suit your rims. Wider tyres need wider rims, and narrow rims limit the tyre size you can fit."]},{"h":"A simple tyre buying checklist","p":["Before you buy tyres, note the size on your old tyres, the rim width, the valve type and the load rating. Choose a tread that suits your riding, and check that the tyre has puncture protection if you ride in town. Compare prices, and read reviews for the specific tyre. Buy a spare tube while you are at it, and check that your pump suits the valve. Fit the tyres carefully, and check them again after the first ride."]},{"h":"Fixing a puncture","ul":["Remove the wheel and tyre, and find the cause of the puncture.","Fit a new tube or patch the old one.","Check inside the tyre for thorns or glass.","Refit the tyre without pinching the tube and inflate to the right pressure.","On a hub-motor wheel, take care with the cable and torque the axle nuts to the maker’s setting."]},{"h":"Tubeless and puncture protection","p":["Tubeless tyres use sealant to seal small punctures and can run at lower pressures. They need compatible rims and tyres. Puncture-protection belts and thicker tubes help commuter bikes avoid flats."]},{"h":"Choosing tyres for your riding","ul":["Smooth, puncture-protected tyres for commuting.","Semi-slick tyres for mixed road and path riding.","Knobbly tyres for trails and loose ground.","Wide fat tyres for sand and snow."]},{"h":"Storing and caring for tyres","p":["Keep tyres out of direct sun and away from oil. Check them for cuts and embedded glass after each ride, and replace tubes that have been patched many times. Keep a spare tube and a pump with you."]},{"h":"Tubeless, tubes and puncture protection","p":["Most e-bikes use tubes, which are simple and cheap to replace. Tubeless tyres use sealant instead and can run at lower pressures with fewer flats, but they need tubeless-ready rims and tyres and a little more setup. Puncture-protection layers inside commuter tyres add resistance to thorns and glass, and are worth having if you ride in town.","Whatever you choose, check the tyre’s load rating, because e-bikes are heavy. A tyre rated for a lighter bike may not suit an e-bike with a rider, battery and luggage."]},{"h":"A tyre check routine","ul":["Check pressure weekly, and before long rides.","Look for cuts, bulges and embedded objects.","Check tread depth and wear patterns.","Spin the wheel and look for wobble or flat spots.","Replace tyres in pairs if both are worn."]},{"h":"Carrying a repair kit","p":["A spare tube, tyre levers, a pump or CO2 inflator and a multi-tool will get you out of most puncture problems. Learn how to remove your wheel before you need to, especially if it has a hub motor, because the cable and torque arm need care. See the [care, tools and hydration](/shop/care-tools-and-hydration/) category."]},{"h":"Quick answers","ul":["**How do I know my tyre size?** Read the sidewall.","**Can I fit wider tyres?** Only if the frame, fork and rim allow it.","**How often should I check pressure?** Weekly, and before long rides.","**Should I go tubeless?** It can reduce flats, but needs compatible rims and tyres."]},{"h":"Products and related guides","p":["See the [wheels, tyres and tubes](/shop/wheels-tyres-and-tubes/) category and the [care, tools and hydration](/shop/care-tools-and-hydration/) category for puncture repair gear. Read the [fat-tyre e-bike guide](/blog/fat-tyre-e-bikes/) for wide-tyre bikes."]},{"h":"Quick recap","p":["In this guide we covered how to read an e-bike tyre size; tubes and valve types; tyre pressure; tyre types for e-bikes; when to replace a tyre; and choosing tyres for different e-bike types. Come back to the section you need when you are deciding, and use the related categories and guides linked above to compare real products in Australian dollars. If you would like help applying any of this to your own bike, your budget and where you ride, message the team on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 or use the contact page, and we will reply with clear, honest advice."]}],"faq":[{"q":"How do I read the size on an e-bike tyre?","a":"The size is printed on the sidewall, for example 26 x 4.0 or 700 x 40c. Match both the diameter and the width when you replace a tyre, and check that the tube valve suits the rim. See the wheels, tyres & tubes range for current models and prices."},{"q":"What tyre pressure should I use on an e-bike?","a":"Use the range printed on the tyre sidewall and adjust within it for load and surface. Lower pressure grips better on rough ground, while higher pressure rolls easier on smooth roads and usually improves battery range. Browse the wheels, tyres & tubes category to compare models side by side."},{"q":"When should I replace an e-bike tyre?","a":"Replace a tyre when the tread is worn flat, the sidewall is cracked or cut, or it punctures repeatedly. E-bikes are heavier and faster than normal bikes, so worn tyres reduce braking grip and safety. Message the team on WhatsApp if you want advice on the wheels, tyres & tubes range."},{"q":"What tyres and tubes does Torque E-Bikes list?","a":"The wheels, tyres and tubes category lists tyres, tubes and related wheel parts. Each product page states the size and the bikes it suits, and the team can confirm fitment on WhatsApp before you order. Prices are in Australian dollars and each product page lists its specifications."},{"q":"Where do I find tools for fixing a puncture?","a":"The care, tools and hydration category lists tools and care products, and the wheels, tyres and tubes category lists tubes. Message the team on WhatsApp if you are unsure what suits your wheel size. Open the wheels, tyres & tubes category to see everything that is currently in stock."}],"keywords":{"primary":"bike tyre pressure","supporting":["cycle tyre pressure","bike tire psi","psi for cycle tyres","tubeless bike tyres","how to air up a bike tire"]},"words":1322},"e-bike-seats-saddles-kickstands-and-mudguards":{"hook":"Seats, mudguards and kickstands are fitted to specific mounts. Check the mounting type and the model before ordering, and tighten every bolt before riding.","intro":["Seats, mudguards and kickstands are fitted to specific mounts on your bike, so the right size and mounting matter more than the look. Choosing well improves comfort and keeps the bike tidy and practical.","This guide covers how to choose and fit each part."],"sections":[{"h":"Choosing a seat or saddle","p":["Comfort comes from width, shape and padding that suit your body and riding. A wider, padded saddle suits upright commuting. A narrower saddle suits sportier riding. Check the rail type so it fits your seat post clamp."]},{"h":"Seat posts and clamps","p":["Measure the outside diameter of the seat tube or post with callipers, or read the size marked on the post. Common sizes include 27.2 mm, 30.9 mm and 31.6 mm. A post that is too small can slip, and one that is too large will not fit the frame."]},{"h":"Kickstands","p":["Check the mount type on the frame, such as a plate by the rear axle or a dedicated bolt, and choose a stand of the right length. Tighten it fully and check that it supports the bike’s weight, because e-bikes are heavy."]},{"h":"Mudguards and fenders","p":["Match the mudguard to the wheel diameter and the tyre width, and leave clearance for the tyre to turn. Fat tyres need wide mudguards, and some frames need specific mounting points."]},{"h":"How to choose a saddle by riding style","p":["Upright riders, such as commuters and leisure riders, usually prefer a wider saddle with more padding and a short nose, because their weight rests on their sit bones. Riders who lean forward, such as road and gravel riders, usually prefer a longer, narrower saddle with less padding, because their weight is spread between the saddle and the bars. Off-road riders often choose a saddle with a rounded profile and tough covers.","Measure your sit bones if you can: many bike shops offer a simple measurement. Choose a width that supports them. Try a saddle for a few weeks before judging it, adjust the angle in small steps and consider changing the saddle if you still get numbness or soreness after the adjustment."]},{"h":"Seat posts: size, material and suspension","p":["Seat posts differ in diameter and length. Measure the inside of the seat tube or read the size marked on the old post, and check that the new post is long enough to reach your saddle height with enough length inside the frame. Aluminium posts are common and affordable, carbon posts absorb some vibration and suspension posts add comfort on rough roads. Dropper posts, which can lower the saddle with a lever, are popular on mountain bikes.","Use grease or carbon paste according to the material, tighten the clamp to the specified torque and check the post regularly for creaking or slipping."]},{"h":"Kickstands and footrests: matching the part to the bike","ul":["Rear-mount kickstands bolt to a plate near the rear axle, and mid-mount stands bolt to the frame.","Check the stand’s load rating, because e-bikes are heavy.","Check the length so that the bike leans slightly, not too far.","Footrests and pegs must suit your model and be rated for the rider’s weight.","Tighten bolts and recheck after the first few rides."]},{"h":"Mudguards and body parts for specific models","p":["Mudguards and body panels fit specific wheel sizes and frames, so check the product page for compatibility. If you are unsure, send the team your bike’s make, model and year, and ask for confirmation before you order. See the [seats, fenders and body](/shop/seats-fenders-and-body/) category for what is listed."]},{"h":"Avoiding common saddle and seat post mistakes","ul":["Do not raise the seat post above the minimum insertion mark.","Do not tilt the saddle sharply up or down to fix discomfort.","Do not use a clamp that does not match the seat tube size.","Do not overtighten bolts on carbon posts.","Do not ignore pain: if a saddle hurts after several rides, change it."],"p":["A comfortable setup lets you ride longer and enjoy it more. Take time to adjust your saddle height, angle and fore-aft position, and keep notes so you can reset it after maintenance or transport."]},{"h":"Preparing a bike for sale or storage: seats, mudguards and stands","p":["If you plan to sell your bike, clean and check the saddle, seat post, mudguards and kickstand. Replace worn parts, tighten loose bolts and check for rust. A tidy, well-maintained bike sells faster and for more. If you store the bike for a long period, lower the saddle, cover the bike and keep it in a dry place. Remove the battery and store it as the maker advises.","Keep spare bolts and the original parts if you have upgraded. A buyer may want them, and you may need them if you return the bike to stock for warranty."]},{"h":"Quick fixes for common seat and mudguard problems","p":["A creaking saddle is often a dry seat post or a loose clamp: remove the post, clean it, add grease or carbon paste as appropriate and refit it to the correct torque. A saddle that slips needs a clean seat tube and a properly sized post. A rattling mudguard usually needs a tightened bolt or a rubber washer between the stay and the frame. A kickstand that drifts needs its bolt tightened with thread locker. If a part is cracked or bent, replace it, because it may fail suddenly.","Carry a small multi-tool, and check these fittings after rough rides."]},{"h":"Setting saddle height and position","p":["Set the saddle so your leg is nearly straight at the bottom of the pedal stroke, with a slight bend in the knee. Adjust the fore-aft position and tilt for comfort, and check the clamp is tight. A saddle that is too high or too low causes knee and back pain."]},{"h":"Seat covers and body parts","p":["Seat covers protect a seat from weather and add colour, and body parts such as fenders and panels fit specific models. Check the compatibility on the product page."]},{"h":"Getting a comfortable fit","ul":["Set the saddle height so your knee has a slight bend at the bottom of the stroke.","Level the saddle, then adjust the tilt a few degrees at a time.","Move the saddle forward or back to suit your reach.","Choose a saddle width that suits your sit bones."]},{"h":"Mudguards, kickstands and practical fittings","p":["Mudguards keep you clean and dry, and a kickstand rated for the bike’s weight makes loading easier. Check that these fittings do not clash with tyres, brakes or racks."]},{"h":"Saddle shape and comfort","p":["A saddle that is comfortable for one rider can be painful for another, because sit-bone width and riding position differ. Wider, padded saddles suit upright riding, and narrower saddles suit more forward positions. Cut-outs or channels can reduce pressure. If you ride long distances, pay more attention to fit than to padding, because too much padding can cause chafing.","Give a new saddle a few rides before judging it, and adjust height, angle and position in small steps. A saddle that is tilted too far up or down can cause numbness."]},{"h":"Mudguards and fittings that last","ul":["Choose mudguards with sturdy stays and enough clearance for your tyres.","Check that fittings are rated for the weight of an e-bike.","Use thread locker on bolts that rattle loose.","Replace cracked mudguards, because they can jam in the wheel."]},{"h":"Kickstands and bike stands","p":["A good kickstand matters on a heavy bike. Check the rated load, the length and the mount type, and test it with the bike loaded. A kickstand that is too short or too long makes the bike lean or fall. A centre stand or a twin-leg stand is more stable for cargo bikes."]},{"h":"Footrests, pegs and body parts","p":["Foot pegs and body parts fit specific models, so check the compatibility on the product page. See the [seats, fenders and body](/shop/seats-fenders-and-body/) category, and ask the team on WhatsApp if you are unsure about fit. For the whole commuting setup read the [commuter e-bike guide](/blog/commuter-e-bikes/)."]},{"h":"Quick recap","p":["In this guide we covered choosing a seat or saddle; seat posts and clamps; kickstands; mudguards and fenders; how to choose a saddle by riding style; and seat posts: size, material and suspension. Come back to the section you need when you are deciding, and use the related categories and guides linked above to compare real products in Australian dollars. If you would like help applying any of this to your own bike, your budget and where you ride, message the team on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 or use the contact page, and we will reply with clear, honest advice."]}],"faq":[{"q":"How do I measure a seat post for a replacement?","a":"Measure the outside diameter of the seat tube or post with callipers, or read the size marked on the post. A post that is too small can slip, and one that is too large will not fit the frame. See the seats, fenders & body range for current models and prices."},{"q":"What size mudguard do I need?","a":"Match the mudguard to the wheel diameter and the tyre width, and leave clearance for the tyre to turn. Fat tyres need wide mudguards, and some frames need specific mounting points. Browse the seats, fenders & body category to compare models side by side."},{"q":"How do I fit a kickstand on an e-bike?","a":"Check the mount type on the frame, such as a plate by the rear axle or a dedicated bolt, and choose a stand of the right length. Tighten it fully and check it supports the bike’s weight. Message the team on WhatsApp if you want advice on the seats, fenders & body range."},{"q":"Which seats, kickstands and mudguards does Torque E-Bikes list?","a":"The seats, fenders and body category lists seats, seat covers, kickstands, foot pegs and body parts for several bikes. Each product page names the models it fits. Prices are in Australian dollars and each product page lists its specifications. Open the seats, fenders & body category to see everything that is currently in stock."},{"q":"Can Torque E-Bikes confirm whether a seat fits my bike?","a":"Yes. Message your bike’s make, model and year on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 and the team will check fitment before you order. Freight and price are confirmed before payment. The team can confirm fitment or availability on WhatsApp. Prices are in Australian dollars and each product page lists its specifications."}],"keywords":{"primary":"kickstand on a bike","supporting":["bicycle saddle post","saddle tube bike","cycle saddle clamp","kickstand on mtb","bicycle front fender"]},"words":1311},"e-bike-grips-pedals-and-handlebar-controls":{"hook":"Grips, pedals and handlebar controls are the contact points on an e-bike. Match the grip diameter, pedal thread and controller connector to your bike before you order.","intro":["Grips, pedals and handlebar controls are the contact points between you and your e-bike. The right sizes and connectors make the bike comfortable and safe, and the wrong ones cause problems.","This guide covers how to choose and fit them."],"sections":[{"h":"Choosing grips","p":["Most flat-bar bikes use 22.2 mm (7/8 inch) handlebars, but throttle and display fittings can change the space available. Measure your bar, check the grip length, and check that a twist throttle fits alongside the grip. Lock-on grips stay put without glue, and slip-on grips are simple but can rotate."]},{"h":"Pedals","p":["Flat pedals suit casual and off-road riding, and clipless pedals suit sporty riding with matching shoes. Pedal threads differ left and right: the left pedal has a reverse thread and the right pedal a normal one, usually marked L and R. Most e-bike pedals use a 9/16 inch thread, but check your cranks first."]},{"h":"Brake levers and handlebar controls","p":["Many e-bikes use brake levers with a cut-off sensor that stops motor power when you brake. Check that a replacement lever has the right connector for your controller before you fit it, and that the lever suits your brake type."]},{"h":"Fitting tips","ul":["Tighten to the maker’s torque settings.","Use thread-locking compound where recommended.","Check that controls do not clash with each other or the display.","Test the brakes and throttle before riding."]},{"h":"Choosing grips for different riding conditions","p":["Rubber grips are comfortable and affordable, and silicone or foam grips can reduce vibration. Textured grips help in the wet, and lock-on grips give a secure hold without glue. Ergonomic grips with a flat platform support your palm and can reduce numbness on long rides. If you wear gloves, choose a grip that works well with them. If you ride off-road, grips with a flange can stop your hand slipping.","Pay attention to grip diameter. Large hands often prefer thicker grips, and small hands prefer thinner ones. If your hands go numb on rides, a change in grip or bar angle can help."]},{"h":"Choosing pedals for your shoes and your riding","p":["Flat pedals with pins grip your shoes and let you put a foot down quickly. They suit commuting, trail riding and riders who want freedom of movement. Platform pedals with reflectors suit general use. Clipless pedals lock your shoe to the pedal, so you pedal more efficiently, but you need compatible shoes and practice to release. Choose according to your riding and confidence.","Pedal size matters. A larger platform spreads load across your foot and improves comfort. If you ride in wet conditions, choose pedals with good grip and drain features."]},{"h":"Levers, throttles and displays","p":["Brake levers should be easy to reach and use with one or two fingers. If your bike uses brake cut-off sensors, choose levers that fit the sensor, or check that the controller accepts the lever you want. Throttles should suit the hand and not interfere with grips. Displays should be readable in sunlight and secure on the handlebar.","When replacing electrical parts, check the connector type and the communication protocol. Many displays and controllers work only with matching parts. If you are not sure, ask the seller or the team to confirm compatibility."]},{"h":"Fitting tips that prevent problems","ul":["Clean and dry the handlebar before fitting grips.","Use the right tools and torque settings.","Check that the cable routing allows full steering movement.","Test brakes, throttle and display before riding.","Recheck bolts after the first ride."]},{"h":"A short checklist for replacing worn parts","ul":["Replace grips when they are cracked, shiny or loose.","Replace pedals if they wobble, bind or have worn pins.","Replace brake levers if they are bent, loose or damaged.","Check cables, connectors and displays for damage.","Fit new parts with correct torque and test before riding."],"p":["Keep a few spare bolts, a hex key set and some grease in your tool kit, and check these parts regularly. They are small, but they affect comfort and safety on every ride."]},{"h":"Setting up your cockpit for comfort","p":["Your cockpit is the combination of grips, levers, display and bars. Set brake levers so your fingers reach them without bending your wrists, and angle them slightly downward. Place the display where you can see it with a glance, and angle it to avoid glare. Rotate the bars so your wrists stay neutral. Small adjustments reduce fatigue and help you ride longer.","After adjusting, ride for a few minutes and note anything that feels off, such as pressure on the heel of your hand or reaching for the throttle. Tweak the setup and repeat. A cockpit that fits you makes every ride more comfortable and safer."]},{"h":"When to see a mechanic","p":["See a mechanic if you find cracks in a crank or pedal axle, if a pedal will not thread in properly, if brake levers feel loose or if displays and controls show errors. A mechanic has the tools and experience to diagnose and fix problems safely. Do not ride with a loose or damaged control, because it can fail when you need it most.","Regular checks and a good relationship with a workshop keep small problems from becoming big ones."]},{"h":"A last word on grips, pedals and controls","p":["Small parts make a big difference to comfort and safety. Choose parts that fit your bike and your hands, fit them carefully and check them regularly. If you need help finding a compatible part, send us your bike’s details."]},{"h":"Comfort, ergonomics and vibration","p":["Ergonomic grips with a wider contact area reduce hand fatigue on long rides, and padded grips cut vibration. Set brake levers at a comfortable reach and angle so your wrists stay straight."]},{"h":"Pedal grip and shoes","p":["Pedals with pins give grip with flat shoes, and clipless pedals hold your shoe to the pedal for efficiency. Match pedals to your shoes and riding, and practise releasing clipless pedals before riding in traffic."]},{"h":"Choosing pedals for your riding","ul":["Flat pedals with pins for grip and easy dismounts.","Platform pedals with reflectors for commuting.","Clipless pedals for sporty riding with matching shoes."]},{"h":"Fitting grips and controls properly","p":["Clean the bar, slide the grip on with a little grip adhesive or use lock-on clamps, and tighten to the maker’s settings. Set brake levers so you can reach them with one or two fingers and your wrist straight. Test brake and throttle operation before you ride."]},{"h":"Choosing grips for comfort and control","p":["Grips are a small part with a big effect on comfort. Thicker grips and ergonomic shapes spread pressure across your hand and reduce numbness on long rides. Softer rubber absorbs vibration, while firmer grips give a more direct feel. If your bike has a twist throttle, the grip next to it needs to be compatible, and if you ride in gloves, consider grips with a textured surface.","Lock-on grips use clamps and do not need adhesive, so they are easy to remove and adjust. Slip-on grips are cheaper but can rotate if they are not fitted well. Whichever you choose, tighten clamps to the maker’s setting and check them after the first ride."]},{"h":"Pedals, cranks and thread problems","ul":["Check which way each pedal threads on: the left pedal is reverse threaded.","Grease the threads and tighten firmly to avoid creaking and damage to the crank.","Do not force a pedal that will not start, because you can strip the crank.","Replace worn pedals, because loose or damaged pedals can cause accidents."]},{"h":"Controls and displays","p":["Check that displays and switches suit your controller before buying. Many e-bikes use proprietary connectors, so a part that looks the same may not fit. If you are unsure, send the team your bike’s make and model and ask them to check before you order. See the [controls, grips and pedals](/shop/controls-grips-and-pedals/) category."]},{"h":"Products and related guides","p":["See the [controls, grips and pedals](/shop/controls-grips-and-pedals/) category and the [brakes](/shop/brakes/) category. Read the [pedal assist vs throttle guide](/blog/pedal-assist-vs-throttle/) and the [brakes guide](/blog/e-bike-and-dirt-bike-brakes/)."]},{"h":"Quick recap","p":["In this guide we covered choosing grips; pedals; brake levers and handlebar controls; fitting tips; choosing grips for different riding conditions; and choosing pedals for your shoes and your riding. Come back to the section you need when you are deciding, and use the related categories and guides linked above to compare real products in Australian dollars. If you would like help applying any of this to your own bike, your budget and where you ride, message the team on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 or use the contact page, and we will reply with clear, honest advice."]}],"faq":[{"q":"What size are e-bike handlebar grips?","a":"Most grips fit a 22mm handlebar, but throttle and display fittings can change the space available. Measure your bar and check the grip length before you order, and check that a twist throttle fits the grip. See the controls, grips & pedals range for current models and prices."},{"q":"Are bicycle pedals left and right threaded?","a":"Yes. The left pedal has a reverse thread and the right pedal has a normal thread, and they are usually marked L and R. Most e-bike pedals use a 9/16 inch thread, but check your cranks first. Browse the controls, grips & pedals category to compare models side by side."},{"q":"Do brake levers on an e-bike cut the motor?","a":"Many e-bikes use brake levers with a cut-off sensor that stops motor power when you brake. Check that a replacement lever has the right connector for your controller before you fit it. Message the team on WhatsApp if you want advice on the controls, grips & pedals range."},{"q":"What grips and pedals does Torque E-Bikes list?","a":"The controls, grips and pedals category lists handlebar grips, pedals, throttles, displays and switches. Each product page states the models it fits and the connector type where it matters. Prices are in Australian dollars and each product page lists its specifications."},{"q":"Where do I find a replacement display or throttle?","a":"The controls, grips and pedals category lists displays and throttle kits for several bikes. Message your bike’s make and model on WhatsApp and the team will confirm which one is compatible. Open the controls, grips & pedals category to see everything that is currently in stock."}],"keywords":{"primary":"pedal of a bike","supporting":["cycle grip","pedal on a bike","bikes with pedals","pedal of a bike guide","how to choose pedal of a bike"]},"words":1317},"conversion-kit-finder":{"hook":"A conversion kit adds a motor, battery and controller to a bicycle. The right kit depends on the bike type, rider weight, terrain and whether you will ride on roads or off-road only.","intro":["A conversion kit adds a motor, battery and controller to a bicycle. The right kit depends on your bike type, your weight, the terrain and whether you will ride on roads or off-road only.","Use the finder below to narrow the choice, then check the fitment notes on the product pages before you buy."],"sections":[{"h":"How the finder works","p":["Choose your bike type, rider weight, terrain and where you will ride. The finder suggests a kit type from our range and gives a plain-English legal note. It is a guide, not a guarantee of fit, so confirm compatibility with the team."]},{"h":"What else you will need","p":["A conversion also needs a compatible battery, good brakes and installation time. Read our [conversion kit guide](/blog/e-bike-conversion-kits-explained/) and our [brakes guide](/blog/e-bike-and-dirt-bike-brakes/)."]},{"h":"Understanding the four questions","p":["The finder asks about your bike, your weight, the terrain and where you will ride, because those four answers decide most of what matters in a kit. Your bike type tells us about frame strength, wheel size and how much room there is for a motor and battery. Your weight affects how hard the motor and battery must work. The terrain tells us whether you need strong climbing torque or can manage with a gentler motor. And where you will ride decides which power level is appropriate under Australian rules.","None of these answers is a test. There is no right or wrong choice, and you can run the finder several times with different answers to see how the suggestion changes. For example, switching from flat to hilly terrain usually points towards a mid-drive motor and a larger battery."]},{"h":"What the finder cannot tell you","ul":["It cannot see your frame, so it cannot confirm strength or condition.","It cannot measure your dropout width, wheel size or brake type.","It cannot check that a specific kit fits your bike’s specific model.","It cannot tell you the rules in every situation, so check your state regulator.","It cannot replace a mechanic’s inspection of an older or unusual bike."],"p":["Use the suggestion as a starting point. Then read the product page for any kit you are considering, measure your bike and confirm fit before you order."]},{"h":"A quick measurement checklist before you buy","ul":["Measure the rear dropout width between the inside faces of the frame.","Note the wheel size marked on the tyre and the rim width.","Identify the brake type: rim, mechanical disc or hydraulic disc.","Count the gears and check whether you have a cassette or a freewheel.","Check the frame material and whether the dropouts are steel or aluminium.","Take photos of your bike from both sides and of the rear and front dropouts."],"p":["Send the photos and measurements to the team on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189, and we will help you check that the kit you are considering suits your bike."]},{"h":"Hub kit or mid-drive: how the finder decides","p":["The finder leans towards a mid-drive kit when you ride hills, carry heavy loads, ride off-road or have a mountain bike. A mid-drive motor uses your bike’s gears, which keeps the motor in an efficient range when you climb and feels natural. It also keeps the weight low and central, which helps handling. The trade-off is more work to install and extra wear on the chain and gears.","The finder leans towards a rear hub kit when your terrain is mostly flat and your bike is a commuter or hybrid. A hub kit is simpler to fit, leaves your drivetrain alone and often costs less. If you are on the border between the two, think about how much work you want to do and how steep your hills are. If you are unsure, send us your answers and photos of your bike and we will suggest a kit."]},{"h":"What to do if the finder suggests a kit you cannot find","p":["If the suggestion points to a kit type we do not list, or if our range does not match your bike, message the team with your answers and photos of your bike. We will tell you honestly whether we have something suitable, and if not we will say so. It is better to know before you spend money than after.","You can also use the suggestion as a guide when you look elsewhere. The key points are the motor type, power level, battery size and the checks for frame and brakes. Take those to any supplier and ask them to confirm that their kit suits your bike."]},{"h":"Talk to the team about your conversion","p":["If you want help turning the finder’s suggestion into a shopping list, message the team on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 with your answers and photos of your bike. We will check the details with you, and tell you honestly whether we have a kit that suits your bike."]},{"h":"Common questions the finder helps answer","ul":["Do I need a hub motor or a mid-drive?","How much power do I really need?","How big should the battery be?","Is my frame strong enough?","Can I use this on the road?"]},{"h":"After the finder: next steps","p":["Use the suggestion to shortlist kits, then check each product page for fitment notes and specifications. Measure your dropout width, wheel size and brake type, and check them against the kit. If you want a second opinion, send the team your bike’s make, model and photos of the frame and dropouts."]},{"h":"Rules for converted bikes","p":["A pedal-assist conversion that stays within the 250W limits can result in a road-legal e-bike. Higher-powered kits are for off-road and private property use only. Read the [e-bike laws guide](/blog/off-road-vs-street-legal-australia/)."]},{"h":"Quick recap","p":["In this guide we covered how the finder works; what else you will need; understanding the four questions; what the finder cannot tell you; a quick measurement checklist before you buy; and hub kit or mid-drive: how the finder decides. Come back to the section you need when you are deciding, and use the related categories and guides linked above to compare real products in Australian dollars. If you would like help applying any of this to your own bike, your budget and where you ride, message the team on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 or use the contact page, and we will reply with clear, honest advice."]}],"faq":[{"q":"Which conversion kit do I need for my bike?","a":"It depends on the bike type, rider weight, terrain and whether you will ride on roads. Answer the four finder questions and compare the result with the fitment notes on the product pages before you buy. See the motors, drivetrain & sprockets range for current models and prices."},{"q":"Can any bicycle be converted to an e-bike?","a":"Not every frame suits a kit. Check the dropout width, brake type, wheel size and frame strength, and think about the extra weight of the battery. A bike shop can confirm whether your frame is suitable. Browse the motors, drivetrain & sprockets category to compare models side by side."},{"q":"Is a converted e-bike road legal in Australia?","a":"Only pedal-assist conversions that meet the 250W standard can be used on public roads, subject to state rules. Kits above 250W are for off-road and private property use only. Check your state road authority first. Message the team on WhatsApp if you want advice on the motors, drivetrain & sprockets range."},{"q":"Which conversion kits can the finder point me to at Torque E-Bikes?","a":"The motors, drivetrain and sprockets category lists conversion kits, motors and drivetrain parts. Each product page shows the specification and the price in Australian dollars, and the team confirms fitment on request. Prices are in Australian dollars and each product page lists its specifications."},{"q":"Can the team help me choose a conversion kit?","a":"Yes. Message the team on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 with your bike’s make, model and wheel size and where you ride. They will suggest kits from the range and confirm freight before you pay. Open the motors, drivetrain & sprockets category to see everything that is currently in stock."}],"keywords":{"primary":"ebike conversion kit finder","supporting":["which conversion kit do i need","ebike conversion kit compatibility","conversion kit for my bike","conversion kit size guide","what ebike conversion kit fits my bike"]},"words":974},"diy-2000w-conversion-guide":{"hook":"A 2000W conversion is an off-road machine for private property use only in Australia. Plan the frame, torque arms, battery, wiring and brakes before you buy parts.","intro":["A 2000W conversion turns a mountain bike into a very powerful off-road machine. It is not a road-legal e-bike: bikes above 250W are for off-road and private property use only in Australia.","This guide is a planning checklist rather than a step-by-step build, because the right steps depend on your parts. It focuses on the decisions that most often go wrong: frame strength, torque arms, wiring, battery safety and brakes. If you are not confident with high-current electrical work, have a qualified person do it."],"sections":[{"h":"Check the frame first","p":["Standard mountain bike frames are not designed to withstand high motor torque. The reactive force on the dropouts can bend or crack an aluminium frame, so a strong steel or purpose-built frame is safer, and torque arms are essential. If you are unsure of your frame’s strength, choose a lower-powered kit."]},{"h":"Torque arms and wheel build","p":["Fit heavy-duty torque arms on both sides of the axle so the motor cannot spin in the dropouts. Check that the wheel is built with strong spokes and rim for the extra load, and that the axle nuts are torqued to the maker’s settings."]},{"h":"Battery, wiring and safety","ul":["Use a quality battery with a protection board, and the charger supplied with it.","Use wire gauge rated for the current, and secure connectors so they cannot arc or loosen.","Fit a fuse or breaker suited to the system.","Keep the battery out of heat, and never charge it unattended."],"p":["Read our [battery care guide](/blog/electric-moto-maintenance-lithium-safety/). Lithium batteries are Class 9 dangerous goods, so shipping needs approved carriers."]},{"h":"Brakes and tyres","p":["A bike that goes faster and weighs more needs better brakes and tyres. Upgrade to strong hydraulic disc brakes and check rotor size, and make sure tyres suit the speed and load. See the [brakes guide](/blog/e-bike-and-dirt-bike-brakes/) and the [tyre guide](/blog/e-bike-tyres-and-tubes/)."]},{"h":"Chain and drivetrain","p":["If the kit uses the bike’s chain, align and tension it carefully. See the [chain care guide](/blog/chain-alignment-high-torque-motors/)."]},{"h":"Why 2,000W is a serious step up","p":["Two thousand watts is eight times the power of a road-legal e-bike. At that level the motor can accelerate a bike far faster than a bicycle frame, tyres and brakes were designed for. Heat becomes an issue in the motor, controller and wiring, and the battery must supply large currents. Treat a 2,000W build as building a small electric motorcycle, with all the care that implies.","This is also why a 2,000W conversion is an off-road machine. It cannot be used on public roads in Australia, and you need a suitable place to ride it."]},{"h":"Choosing the frame and fork","p":["A frame designed for e-bikes or downhill riding is a better starting point than a light cross-country frame. Look for strong dropouts with torque-arm provision, a robust fork with a disc brake mount and a frame that can take the weight of the battery. Steel frames can handle torque better than thin aluminium, but check the frame’s condition and have it inspected if you are not sure.","Suspension forks need to be rated for the speed and loads, and the headset and bearings must be in good condition. A failure at speed can be serious, so do not compromise on these parts."]},{"h":"Wiring and protection in practice","ul":["Use wire of the correct gauge for the current, and keep runs as short as sensible.","Use quality connectors rated for the current, and crimp them properly.","Fit a fuse or circuit breaker near the battery.","Insulate and protect wires where they pass near moving parts.","Keep water out of connectors and controllers."]},{"h":"Testing and first rides","p":["Test every system before the first ride. Lift the wheel and spin the motor slowly, check that the throttle returns to zero, test the brake cut-off and check that nothing gets hot at idle. Take the first ride in an open area at low power, check for loose bolts and heat after a few minutes and increase power gradually. Wear full protective gear, and tell someone where you are riding."]},{"h":"A short note on responsibility","p":["A high-power build is an exciting project, but you are responsible for it. If you let someone else ride it, you are responsible for explaining the risks and making sure they wear protective gear. If you sell it, be honest about what it is. If you ride it, do so only where it is legal and safe. Keeping these rules in mind keeps you, others and the hobby in good shape."]},{"h":"Maintenance for a high-power build","ul":["Check torque arms, axle nuts and wiring after every ride in the first weeks.","Inspect the frame and dropouts for cracks regularly.","Check brake pads, rotors and fluid.","Inspect the battery, connectors and cables for heat damage.","Replace worn tyres and chains early."]},{"h":"Planning for where you will ride a high-power build","p":["Before you build, find a place to ride it: a private property with permission, a licensed track or an approved venue. Check venue rules about electric bikes, age, insurance and protective gear. Plan how you will transport the bike and charge the battery on site. Let people at the venue know what you are riding, because a high-powered conversion is different from a commercial dirt bike."]},{"h":"Recording and documenting your build","p":["Keep a build log with photos, part numbers, wiring diagrams, torque settings and test results. Note any changes, problems and fixes. A log helps you troubleshoot, repeat a build and show others how it was made. If you sell the bike, the log shows that the work was done with care. It also helps with warranty claims on individual parts.","Share your build safely with the community, but avoid encouraging unsafe or unlawful riding. Be honest about what the bike is and where it can be ridden."]},{"h":"Final safety reminders for a high-power build","p":["Wear full protective gear every time you ride, use a fuse or breaker on the battery, check connections after every ride in the first weeks and never leave the battery charging unattended. Ride only where you have permission, and not on public roads. If something feels wrong, stop and check it. A cautious builder is a safe builder, and a safe builder keeps enjoying the hobby."]},{"h":"Test before you ride","ul":["Check every bolt, torque arm and connector.","Lift the wheel and test the motor with the wheel off the ground.","Test the brakes and brake cut-off.","Ride slowly in a safe, private area first."]},{"h":"When to ask for help","p":["If you are unsure about wiring, frame strength or brakes, ask a qualified person to check the build before you ride it. A conversion is only as safe as its weakest part."]},{"h":"Charging and testing safely","p":["Charge the battery in a safe place, check the pack and connectors for damage and test the system at low power first. Never leave a high-power battery charging unattended, and keep a suitable fire extinguisher nearby."]},{"h":"Parts list for a high-power build","ul":["A motor and controller rated for the power you intend.","A battery with the right voltage, capacity and current rating, and a protection system.","Torque arms for both sides of the axle.","Heavy-duty spokes and a strong rim.","Brakes suited to the extra speed and weight.","Wire and connectors rated for the current, plus a fuse or breaker.","A display or controller panel and a throttle.","Tools: torque wrench, crimper, multimeter, thread locker and heat-shrink."]},{"h":"Common reasons builds fail","ul":["Frame or dropout failure from too much torque.","Overheating connectors from undersized wire or poor crimps.","Brake failure from underrated brakes.","Battery damage from an unsuitable charger or poor storage.","Wheel failure from weak spokes or a weak rim."]},{"h":"Rules and gear","p":["A 2000W build is for private property use only. Read our [e-bike laws guide](/blog/off-road-vs-street-legal-australia/), wear full protective gear, and see the [conversion kit finder](/tools/conversion-kit-finder/) to match a kit to your bike."]},{"h":"Quick recap","p":["In this guide we covered check the frame first; torque arms and wheel build; battery, wiring and safety; brakes and tyres; chain and drivetrain; and why 2,000W is a serious step up. Come back to the section you need when you are deciding, and use the related categories and guides linked above to compare real products in Australian dollars. If you would like help applying any of this to your own bike, your budget and where you ride, message the team on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 or use the contact page, and we will reply with clear, honest advice."]}],"faq":[],"keywords":{"primary":"diy 2000w conversion","supporting":[]},"words":1304}}
